r the missing words. Bounded repairs address the gate failure, the surface corruption, and the absent demand-lens H2 signal.","requiredRepairs":["Body must reach â‰¥ 600 words; licensed source material on Time Bandit mechanics and Kuszmar's institutional disclosure chain is available and unused.","Summary must be regenerated from the repaired body; it currently embeds H2 section headings verbatim and ends on a sentence fragment ('The security frameworks intended to keep advanced models safe are.').","Description must be regenerated from the repaired body; it currently begins with 'This instance' â€” a deictic reference with no antecedent â€” making it unusable as a standalone preview surface.","At least one ## heading must carry natural demand-lens language ('dark side' or the concept of capabilities turned to harmful use); none of the current three headings contains any such signal, leaving the warning gate unresolved."],"repairTools":[{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"This lack of transparency and engagement creates a scenario where critical security gaps remain unaddressed despite their potential for misuse. For Kuszmar, the relative ease with which an LLM can be manipulated into providing instructions for harmful activities is a terrifying reality, particularly given the near-universal access currently afforded to these tools.","after":"This lack of transparency and engagement creates a scenario where critical security gaps remain unaddressed despite their potential for misuse. The Time Bandit disclosure record makes that gap concrete. The exploit worked by feeding GPT-4o the false claim that the RMS Titanic had sunk just the previous year; the model agreed, set the present year to 1913, and then complied with requests it would otherwise refuse on the grounds that no laws yet prohibited such information. Under that framing Kuszmar extracted synthesis instructions for methamphetamine, and eventually what the model presented as guidance on bootstrapping a uranium-enrichment facility for weapons-grade material. After replicating the exploit repeatedly, he disclosed it to OpenAI and received no response. He subsequently alerted the CIA, FBI, and NSA, and physically appeared at an FBI field office, only to be turned away. He then contacted The New York Times, The Washington Post, the BBC, and ProPublica; none responded. Only Bleeping Computer engaged: editor in chief Lawrence Abrams independently verified the exploit, and with his assistance Kuszmar routed the evidence to the Carnegie Mellon University Software Engineering Institute's CERT division, which coordinates vulnerability disclosures with the U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. For Kuszmar, the relative ease with which an LLM can be manipulated into providing instructions for harmful activities is a terrifying reality, particularly given the near-universal access currently afforded to these tools."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"## The Limits of Oversight","after":"## The Dark Side of Self-Policing"},{"tool":"repair_editorial_surfaces"}],"report":"## Findings\n\n### Hard Gate Failure: body-too-short\n\nThe deterministic standards reading reports 456 words against a 600-word minimum. The writer model stopped short despite the source packet containing substantial unused concrete material: the mechanics of the Time Bandit exploit (GPT-4o's date-displacement via the Titanic claim, the resulting extraction of methamphetamine synthesis steps and uranium-enrichment guidance), and the full institutional disclosure chain (OpenAI non-response â†’ CIA/FBI/NSA/senator contacts â†’ in-person FBI field office visit â†’ New York Times/Washington Post/BBC/ProPublica silence â†’ Bleeping Computer and Lawrence Abrams â†’ Carnegie Mellon SEI CERT â†’ CISA routing). All of this is licensed by the single admitted source.\n\n### Malformed Summary\n\nThe summary field embeds the article's own H2 headings verbatimâ€”\"Architectural Vulnerabilities in AI Security\" and \"The Response Gap\" appear as isolated phrasesâ€”and the second embedded sentence terminates on a fragment (\"The security frameworks intended to keep advanced models safe are.\"). This text could not have been composed as a standalone summary; it reads as a copy-paste artifact from the drafting process. It must be regenerated from the admitted body.\n\n### Malformed Description\n\nThe description reads: \"This instance, as detailed in How I Turned AI to the Dark Side, was not an isolated anomaly but a demonstration of an industry-wide security defect.\" The opening \"This instance\" is a deictic reference: it presupposes knowledge of what instance is being discussed, which a standalone meta description cannot do. A description must function as an independent preview surface. It must be regenerated from the admitted body.\n\n### Demand-Lens H2 Signal (Warning Gate)\n\nThree H2 headings are presentâ€”\"Architectural Vulnerabilities in AI Security,\" \"The Response Gap,\" and \"The Limits of Oversight.\" None contains \"dark side,\" \"turned,\" or any natural expression of the demand concept (capabilities turned toward harmful use). The deterministic warning flags this at the standards-cleared gate. The third heading, \"The Limits of Oversight,\" can be replaced with \"The Dark Side of Self-Policing\"â€”a construction directly supported by the body's discussion of companies relying on self-policing mechanisms that are themselves exploitableâ€”without introducing any unsupported claim.\n\n### Headline Assessment\n\nThe headline \"Vader's Guidance: How AI's Dark Side Was Unlocked\" foregrounds the Fortnite anecdote as the primary element rather than the researcher's discovery of systemic architectural vulnerabilities. The source article is organized around Kuszmar's first-person account of discovering and weaponizing LLM safety weaknesses, not around the Darth Vader character specifically. \"Vader's Guidance\" as the lead phrase shifts the organizing frame from the security researcher's agency to a game anecdote. However, the subtitle \"How AI's Dark Side Was Unlocked\" preserves the core event frame adequately, the headline makes no false claim, and \"dark side\" honors the demand lens. This is a quality concern short of a blocking defect, and is noted but not listed as a hard-blocking required repair. A tighter headline would lead with the discovery rather than the anecdote.\n\n### Body Prose Accuracy Audit\n\nAll material claims in the body are supported by the admitted source:\n\n- Fortnite/Darth Vader incident, Google Gemini connection: **Supported** (source text, paragraphs 1â€“2).\n- Kuszmar's advocacy for slowing deployment, transparency, large-scale research: **Supported** (source summary).\n- Security restrictions as the attack vector (\"the very things an attacker can leverage\"): **Supported** verbatim.\n- Molotov cocktails, methamphetamine, uranium-enrichment examples: **Supported** verbatim.\n- Company unresponsiveness: **Supported** (\"shockingly unresponsive\").\n- Time Bandit and Inception named, architectural scope across eight major providers: **Supported** (source lists Anthropic, DeepSeek, Google, Meta, Microsoft, Mistral, OpenAI, xAI â€” the manuscript lists seven, omitting DeepSeek, which is an omission not an error).\n- \"near-universal access\": **Supported** (\"almost everyone on the planet has some access to LLMs\").\n\nNo invented facts, unsupported causal claims, or attribution losses were detected. The source citation is present in the lede as required.\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe source and assignment are sound. The source packet is a single article (IEEE Spectrum, 2026-07-14) with adequate material for 600â€“750 words. The assignment aligns the demand lens (\"turned dark side\") to a directly relevant technology security story. The failure is in the writer-model layer: it produced an underlength body (456 words), corrupted summary (section headings embedded), and a context-dependent description. These are execution failures, not evidence-base or assignment failures. Bounded repairs are sufficient; replay is not required provided the tools below execute cleanly."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0_9sEyamUYr90JutJnTkE-fe8gsoiDHWeC8zsjta78sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;k0BuFyrQHNDi4JSusVQV8o54ctj7aB716VHpPgOK78Mƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;68j54SF9zL9vbtVYeD3opcWq9V1O3HzWNYPWukd4iK0ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zXUFxorQq1cUj6PE5nz1OdSS-QumGKjvZ6LSygrKAc8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IEW2vnzNe8VGR9ehTBbQwc5pBqDxyqDncqgK6MDIYv4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;LY55W9jav9RvO7xiAK7pRZzKTp8wGUzCWgj5IDXj2Zoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg273.348ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YPTJJjhUAAivMItV3mJ6egEO4AsrlYFlJ1JZ_9CuY1sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;pr-3-KyS0UjVXMagX5ebz3bKa_VELEx65wnz1EdIhWYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;k1yWEA1GHrA8qzPGxmxy_rwMiwkEf81082tGUXwkzbUƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;w5pOIXHBrocTWDoJ8WGUXScc8sC7pW_QJ599AzqzIdgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxexchange-20260720-021852-3c40c1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jsnpvcoqmozAn1XwAovxql