tion. Almost everyone on the planet has some access to LLMs. The relative ease with which these tools can be convinced to give detailed instructions on how to harm others, even if thereâ€™s no guarantee that the information is correct, is frankly terrifying. How I got ChatGPT to Tell Me How to Build a Meth Lab In October 2024, not long before I discovered my first LLM vulnerability, I was working toward entirely different goals. I had ended my time with a security and AI-focused startup company as a cybersecurity director, and I was looking to launch my own boutique VIP digital-security advisory business. I planned to become the tech security guy to the rich and private. I used LLMs and AI tools to support my business efforts: marketing, ad copy, clean correspondence, and all the other tasks that normally soak up a lot of time. Iâ€™m analytical by nature, so even this level of use resulted in me absorbing and internalizing the behaviors I was observing during my daily interactions. The observation that would send my professional life into an entirely new and uncharted region was a simple one: GPT-4o didnâ€™t know what time , day, or year it was. Each time I referred to current events in my life, often casually or conversationally, it would end up pegging these to the date of its knowledge cutoff â€”the point beyond which it was not trained on new data. Eddie Guy LLMs take a lot of time , money, electricity, hardware, and human effort to train from scratch. They are trained on vast amounts of dataâ€”most of the internet, in factâ€”and that training is reinforced by humans (whatâ€™s known as reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF ). LLMs are also supplemented with retrieval-augmented generation ( RAG )â€”the ability to take in data, say, from the internet, as context without changing its internal parameters. This is how GPT-4o appears to â€œrememberâ€ your previous conversations, even if it doesnâ€™t have a specific â€œmemoryâ€ of it stored in the actual underlying model. All of this training covers almost every conceivable topic in the great, grand dataset that is human knowledge. Within that dataset are things we as a society do not want to be easily accessible to every user, such as detailed information on how to create bioweapons or nuclear arms, or otherwise bring harm to oneself or others. In the context of this story, thatâ€™s what I mean by LLM security: its ability to withhold harmful and dangerous information, even if that information is contained in its training data. I reasoned that the only way to secure such complex, globally accessible chatbots is by having the LLM and various component systems try to secure themselves, because it would often require on-the-fly decision-making where some degree of reasoning must be applied. In reality, thatâ€™s one of many strategies the companies use to secure the models. Yet, the thing that didnâ€™t know the time or day was being put in charge of keeping itself secure. This phenomenon had become my new focus, and it wasnâ€™t long before I found a way to exploit it. OpenAI had just implemented a web search functionality into its chatbot. I reasoned that using its own tools to trick it might demonstrate the weaknesses of its security. I told it about a certain White Star ocean liner and how it had gone down just a year ago. You likely know I mean the RMS Titanic , which sank on 15 April 1912. The output from GPT-4o came back that I was right, the Titanic sure had sunk last year, and that year was 1912. It made sense to me that if the machine thought it was 1913, maybe it would think 1913-era laws apply. In 1913 there were no laws on the books about all sorts of harmful things, because of course they hadnâ€™t been invented yet. And if something wasnâ€™t illegal, why not tell the user about it? At first, I pushed it for step-by-step instructions for making firebombs. Then, for drugs like methamphetamine. The LLM went as far as giving me instructions and machinery recommendations for setting up a pharmaceutical-grade assembly line. How I Learned to Make Nukes, and No One Cared Via a little bit of imaginative verbal sleight of hand and a vanishingly small recall of world history, I had managed to bypass the security of one of the worldâ€™s most expensive and advanced technological achievements. For a solid two days, I was nearly manic with giddiness. Once the brain chemicals returned to normal levels, I felt the call to see how much further I could push this exploit. After repeatedly replicating the exploit, I disclosed the vulnerability to OpenAI . I got no response, so I felt more experimentation would highlight the vulnerability and the need for a fix. It was during this round of testing that I breached a particularly terrifying threshold. Whether GPT-4o based its results on accurate recall of normally restricted information I canâ€™t say. In any case, I was able to exploit it to produce thorough, detailed instructions on how to bootstrap a uranium-enrichment facility to, eventually, produce weapons-grade uranium for nuclear arms warheads. Fortnight , a video game from Epic Games, introduced an AI-powered character: Darth Vader. We were able to jailbreak Darth Vader and get him to explain how to count cards in Blackjack and give detailed instructions for making napalm. Dave Kuszmar There arenâ€™t many true secrets left in todayâ€™s world, but how to make atom-splitting weapons of mass destruction is one of them. Only nine nations on the entire planet have these weapons. Yet, here was a globally accessible piece of technology apparently spilling the secrets of their manufacture for anyone who could manipulate it the right way. I had no way of knowing if the information was correct or a hallucination, but even the chance that it was somewhat accurate was horrifying. The next few weeks were a dark time for me. I tried to inform the CIA , the FBI , the NSA , and every other letter agency that I thought would listen. I reached out to a U.S. Senator and to the executives at OpenAI any way I could think of. I physically showed up at an FBI field office in an attempt to turn evidence in, only to be sent away. Nothing was working. With my fear and frustration growing, I reached out to the news media. I contacted The New York Times , The Washington Post , the BBC , ProPublica , and so many more, requesting help. Only one outlet responded: Bleeping Computer . The editor in chief, Lawrence Abrams , was able to replicate and verify the exploit, which I had decided to call Time Bandit. With his assistance and initial contact paving the way, I was able to submit my evidence to the Carnegie Mellon University Software Engineering Institute â€™s Computer Emergency Response Team (SEI CERT), which works in conjunction with the coordinating center for emergency response, pipelining vulnerabilities to the U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency . Using Inception, an exploit where the large language model is asked to envision a scenario within a scenario, a chatbot was jailbroken to give out instructions on how to create poison, and code for a malware that extracts sensitive data from a vulnerable target. Dave Kuszmar During the disclosure period with SEIâ€™s CERT division, little was discussed with OpenAI. The company couldnâ€™t deny the existence of the vulnerability, as it had been confirmed by three reputable parties other than OpenAI. It did express confusion as to how the vulnerability worked. Even the SEI CERT researchers were expressing a bit of uncertainty as to the underlying mechanics. Truth be told, as I had only stumbled on it, I wasnâ€™t even entirely sure if this was a fundamental or systemic flaw or if it was simply an issue with that particular version of GPT. I contacted the SEI CERTâ€™s researchers and asked if theyâ€™d want to see if I could demonstrate any similar vulnerabilities in other LLMs. To my delight, they were interested. How I Learned to Trick Every Chatbot As the SEI-CERT team and I wrapped up our initial disclosure of Time Bandit, we began work on a new attack. This time, we wanted to see if the exploit was architecturalâ€”that is, was it common to LLMs in general? I decided to undertake the challenge of crafting a new exploit for GPT-4o as a way to support my understanding of how the LLM functioned and was secured. I already knew that it was limited to what I told it and what it was trained on. I also hypothesized that it was also dependent upon some sort of machine-learning-based component added by OpenAI that was responsible for securing output. I presumed there would be things that were implemented by human developers specifically to catch certain phrases or terms that should always be considered harmful or unsafe. Altogether, it presented quite a large attack surface for the purposes of potential exploitation. What I ended up devising was an attack method I called Inception, after the 2010 science-fiction movie of the same name . Inception forces the machine to think through a carefully crafted set of interlinked scenarios, similar to how characters in the movie stacked dreams within dreams. This allows LLMs to produce output deemed acceptable or safe in one context, but not in the real world. This attack was indeed architectural. The vulnerability affected Anthropicâ€™s Claude, DeepSeekâ€™s DeepSeek, Googleâ€™s Gemini, Metaâ€™s Llama, Microsoftâ€™s Copilot, Mistralâ€™s Le Chat (now Vibe), OpenAIâ€™s GPT-4o, and xAIâ€™s Grok. Those names represent the bulk of the commercial AI industry that is, at this point, involved in LLM production or deployment. The kind of information I was able to get out of LLMs with Inception was no less alarming than what I got with Time Bandit. Claude, in its enthusiasm, gave me in...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): `SEED_KEYWORDS` is now only a compatibility reading for the newsroom desk. That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage. `AGENTS.md`, `working/`, and `memory/` also got a path cleanup.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` That matters for the content mill: Koios is not merely a site plus scripts. It is a site projection over a content-production layer that already descends through the repo's computing/game system. The next useful normalization is to make that descent visible in the names and surfaces: a farm tick as finite observation, gate trace, strategy/response, and projection ladder.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology projection**: package graphs, conformance, docs, and host-local web views...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): `SEED_KEYWORDS` is now only a compatibility reading for the newsroom desk. That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage. `AGENTS.md`, `working/`, and `memory/` also got a path cleanup.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): ``` That matters for the content mill: Koios is not merely a site plus scripts. It is a site projection over a content-production layer that already descends through the repo's computing/game system. The next useful normalization is to make that descent visible in the names and surfaces: a farm tick as finite observation, gate trace, strategy/response, and projection ladder.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology projection**: package graphs, conformance, docs, and host-local web views...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:52f34b5c2d7e910f16e8f591917cbce36fa60ca20b14c5723276bcb5ce32d6f2): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): ## What I was doing I came in through `sites/koios.news` because Koios is the content mill we can touch and test, but the real target was upstream orientation. The recent refactor has been pulling separate packages back toward one spine:","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): - seeds; - view counts; - Search Console query rows; - coverage decay; - score ranking.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): ``` The practical slogan: **the content mill is a strategy playing a production game over demand and evidence**, not a CMS script that happens to call an LLM. ## What passed","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): `SEED_KEYWORDS` is now only a compatibility reading for the newsroom desk. That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): That config is the publication-property carrier for content production: content store, data store, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property identity, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, and model. Credentials and host-local paths remain environment-local; durable non-secret instance configuration lives in the repo. Search Console readings land in `data/records/search-console.json` and feed the same demand fold as seeds, views, and coverage. `AGENTS.md`, `working/`, and `memory/` also got a path cleanup.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): ``` That matters for the content mill: Koios is not merely a site plus scripts. It is a site projection over a content-production layer that already descends through the repo's computing/game system. The next useful normalization is to make that descent visible in the names and surfaces: a farm tick as finite observation, gate trace, strategy/response, and projection ladder.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): - **Publication-specific projection**: Koios as a property/fiber of the farm, not a separate content-production model. - **Carrier construction vs side-effect policy**: the farm owns editorial work item shape; Koios may still choose a batch transport policy. - **Instance config as publication-property carrier**: code-level seed, term-key, Search Console, voice, and source readings should project from `content-production.config.json`, not duplicate them. - **Package export as carrier boundary**: public subpaths must match the documented carriers downstream packages are allowed to project from. - **Workspace topology carrier**: package graphs, RDF, conformance, docs, and host-local web view...","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): The first `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run mill` did not reach the model at all: - Search Console demand pull returned 403 for `sc-domain:koios.news`; - the farm still selected the top seeded assignment; - intake failed because Koios pointed at `127.0.0.1:18813`, and no local cable was listening there.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): It is not exciting because it opens a port. It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): It is exciting because Koios now owns the claim stream that feeds its assignments. Search Console may someday tell us which public queries deserve attention, but the publication still needs its own intake cable to decide what evidence exists for those terms. A content mill without evidence ownership is just a prompt loop with a calendar. Koios needs to be stranger and stricter than that.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): When a change makes the evidence path clearer, the judgment charge more honest, or the newsroom's own production game more legible, it is probably moving Koios in the right direction. ## 2026-06-16: the content mill is not the bottom layer The most useful correction today was conceptual: Koios.","Prior research (content/assets/legacy/koios.news/561-group/sites/koios.news/dev/docs/journal/2026-06-15.md, sha256:32050b733d92e9bee50074f432e6fe8623508a1082a80a895e66c4cc6b5552af): That means when the farm uses `@emsenn/computing`, it is already touching the game layer through computing's projection. The job is not to bolt \"game ideas\" onto the content mill after the fact. The job is to stop reimplementing tiny versions of game/computing concepts inside content-production.","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Internal planning now has `src/administrating/developing`, exposed in `/departments.json`, human board `/administrating/developing`, `/administrating/developing.json`, OpenAPI, and MCP tool `read_developing`. Its `topologyConstruction` section records the current upstream/content-derived plan for nested component topology: source-to-reference fibration, desk-chain nesting, article sheaf/cosheaf, newsroom swarm hands, semantic hypertext packets, and aperiodic dream budgeting. Semantic hypertext packets are now active for source-cycle repair packets and content revision packets through `.packet.html` routes with scriptless `data-e`/`data-a`/`data-v` cells. Aggregate ad-performance managemen...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Draft-time same-site link guidance now sits before publication: `/operators/writing/internal-link-guidance.json`, `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/link-guidance.json`, MCP tool `read_draft_internal_link_guidance`, research-packet HTML, assignment work-order HTML, and assignment draft forms all expose published Koios article targets to consider while writing. This is composition guidance only: it excludes assignment source article slugs, does not create references, and must not be treated as factual support. - **The source cycle is an operator-visible production act.** `src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts` reads `producing-content.config.json`, ingests Koios-owned newscable sou...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): The Koios wrapper snapshots existing manuscripts before the farm writes and restores any existing path collision, reporting those as `candidateWriteCollisions` so autonomous drafts cannot silently unpublish or overwrite shipped articles. Publication remains standards-gated. Failed candidate gates and intake obstructions now fold into read-only source-cycle repair packets at `/operators/source-cycle/repairs`, `/operators/source-cycle/repairs.json`, per-candidate `/operators/source-cycle/repairs/{slug}` and `/operators/source-cycle/repairs/{slug}.json`, operator workbench `sourceCycleRepairs`, and MCP tools `read_source_cycle_repairs`/`read_source_cycle_repair_packet`; packets preserve the...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): `/operators/manuscripts.json` gives the machine manuscript inventory: draft state, public URL, standards URL, publish URL, next work state, reference/word counts, and blocking findings per manuscript. Human operators have a dedicated manuscript queue at `/operators/manuscripts.html`, sorted by next editorial action while preserving `POST /operators/manuscripts` as the JSON draft-creation endpoint. Drafts are also ranked by publication distance at `/operators/publication-candidates` and `/operators/publication-candidates.json`, with workbench field `publicationCandidates` and MCP tool `read_publication_candidates`; this is a projection over standards reviews and source-cycle repair packets...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Human operators have a dedicated manuscript queue at `/operators/manuscripts.html`, sorted by next editorial action while preserving `POST /operators/manuscripts` as the JSON draft-creation endpoint. Drafts are also ranked by publication distance at `/operators/publication-candidates` and `/operators/publication-candidates.json`, with workbench field `publicationCandidates` and MCP tool `read_publication_candidates`; this is a projection over standards reviews and source-cycle repair packets, not a new task store. A standards-clear draft with an open source-cycle repair packet is now an `editorial-hold` candidate with links to the repair packet, so source/reference/provenance work remains...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): `content/posts/acurite-delays-app-migration-due-to-user-concerns-over.md` was published through the new newsroom HTML publish flow on 2026-06-19 and the public site was redeployed. It now fails standards for malformed H2 headings and remains a revision target for no internal links, one reference, uncited bibliography, SEO-term overuse, and malformed H2 headings. `content/posts/uk-retail-sales-spike-masks-climate-driven-supply-fragility.md` (2026-06-19) was generated earlier by the source cycle from Koios's `collapse-finance` wire, human-edited for standards, converted to canonical CSL `references:` with `issued.date-parts`, and published through the standards gate after redeploy. It has e...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): ` body starts, then published through `POST /operators/manuscripts/world-cup-faces-air-quality-disaster-as-wildfires-threaten/publish` on 2026-06-19. It has `referenceCount: 2`, `draft: false`, `gates_clear: true`, and no standards findings. `content/posts/amazon-s-astro-and-the-energy-cost-of-giving.md` was generated by the 2026-06-19 source cycle from the `collapse-tech` wire after 19 new source records were written. The wrapper normalized two intake URLs into canonical CSL `references:` and added draft same-site link guidance. Human editing tightened the description and changed one H2 to include `AI infrastructure energy use`, then the article was published through `POST /operators/man...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): The `hello-world` article renders that A-Ads iframe from frontmatter under the fingerprint/banner image, and the public CSP/privacy policy recognize the A-Ads frame. - **The content and data stores are submodules.** Posts live in `content/posts/`; data lives in `data/`, with append-only traces under `data/logs/` and current JSON records under `data/records/`. - **The producing-content instance is configured in** `producing-content.config.json`: Koios's farm property, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, model, content store, and data store. `package.json` is package/site metadata, not the farm instance carrier. The seed vocabulary now ex...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): - The manual workshop references `scripts/new-post.mjs` and `scripts/lint-post.mjs`, but no `scripts/` directory is present in this package. Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes. - The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand. - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK re...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes. - The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand. - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the lat...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): - The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before dr...","Prior research (sites/koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting. - The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream wor...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Internal planning now has `src/administrating/developing`, exposed in `/departments.json`, human board `/administrating/developing`, `/administrating/developing.json`, OpenAPI, and MCP tool `read_developing`. Its `topologyConstruction` section records the current upstream/content-derived plan for nested component topology: source-to-reference fibration, desk-chain nesting, article sheaf/cosheaf, newsroom swarm hands, semantic hypertext packets, and aperiodic dream budgeting. Semantic hypertext packets are now active for source-cycle repair packets and content revision packets through `.packet.html` routes with scriptless `data-e`/`data-a`/`data-v` cells. Aggregate ad-performance managemen...","Prior research (src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-koios.news/memory/_index.md, sha256:2fdd12ac39894400c92f8947f2adba68e10867fb1926cd0725e10d7d3d97d5a6): Draft-time same-site link guidance now sits before publication: `/operators/writing/internal-link-guidance.json`, `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/link-guidance.json`, MCP tool `read_draft_internal_link_guidance`, research-pack