packets through `.packet.html` routes with scriptless `data-e`/`data-a`/`data-v` cells.\n  Aggregate ad-performance management now lives under `publishing/managing/advertising`: `/operators/advertising/performance.json`, `POST /operators/advertising/performance`, MCP tools `read_ad_performance` and `record_ad_performance`, and the operator workbench expose provider/section/tag ROI from aggregate records only.\n  Aggregate content-performance management now lives under `publishing/managing/circulating`: `/operators/content/performance.json` and MCP tool `read_content_performance` fold first-party path views by article, section, and tag."},{"line":46,"matchedTerms":["source cycle","repair packet"],"text":"  The newsroom web desk now renders the top five conservative internal-link placement packets with source/target/evidence/preview and an `Apply link` form; the form submits the same stale-safe expected paragraph witness through `/operators/content/internal-link-placements/form`.\n  Draft-time same-site link guidance now sits before publication: `/operators/writing/internal-link-guidance.json`, `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/link-guidance.json`, MCP tool `read_draft_internal_link_guidance`, research-packet HTML, assignment work-order HTML, and assignment draft forms all expose published Koios article targets to consider while writing. This is composition guidance only: it excludes assignment source article slugs, does not create references, and must not be treated as factual support.\n- **The source cycle is an operator-visible production act.** `src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts` reads `content-production.config.json`, ingests Koios-owned newscable sources, then runs one `ContentFarmService.tick()` through the configured provider. It is exposed as `pnpm run source:cycle`, `GET /operators/source-cycle/plan.json`, `POST /operators/source-cycle`, and MCP tools `read_source_cycle_plan`/`run_source_cycle`. The cycle records draft candidates and run evidence, then normalizes any written farm candidate from article `sources:` into canonical CSL `references:` and applies the CC BY 4.0 license, license URL, and syndication-rights defaults before reporting that as `candidateReferences`; it now also annotates current-cycle candidates with compact `koios.draft_internal_link_guidance` suggestions and reports them as `candidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance`. The Koios wrapper snapshots existing manuscripts before the farm writes and restores any existing path collision, rep"},{"line":54,"matchedTerms":["repair packet"],"text":"  `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/manuscripts` and MCP tool `create_assignment_draft` create a draft directly from an assigned item, promoting assignment source URLs into CSL manuscript references while carrying the assignment id, beat, and SEO term. The assignment work-order and draft form show same-site link guidance before body composition.\n  The newsroom web CMS now exposes HTML forms for `/operators/assignments/new`, `/operators/manuscripts/new`, and `/operators/assignments/{assignmentId}/manuscripts/new`; their POST handlers reuse the same assignment/draft normalizers and write operations as the JSON API.\n  `/operators/manuscripts.json` gives the machine manuscript inventory: draft state, public URL, standards URL, publish URL, next work state, reference/word counts, and blocking findings per manuscript. Human operators have a dedicated manuscript queue at `/operators/manuscripts.html`, sorted by next editorial action while preserving `POST /operators/manuscripts` as the JSON draft-creation endpoint. Drafts are also ranked by publication distance at `/operators/publication-candidates` and `/operators/publication-candidates.json`, with workbench field `publicationCandidates` and MCP tool `read_publication_candidates`; this is a projection over standards reviews and source-cycle repair packets, not a new task store. A standards-clear draft with an open source-cycle repair packet is now an `editorial-hold` candidate with links to the repair packet, so source/reference/provenance work remains visible before publishing.\n  Human operators can read manuscript standards reviews at `/operators/manuscripts/{slug}/standards.html`, with findings, counts, JSON link, public URL, and repair/publish forms. The JSON API remains `/operators/manuscripts/{slug}/standards`, and"},{"line":62,"matchedTerms":["source cycle"],"text":"  Publishing is gated by `src/researching/writing/editing/standards.mts`, which reviews canonical CSL `references:` records, body citations, body length, H2/body separation, SEO placement, license, license URL, syndication rights, and existing gate metadata before clearing a draft. Article frontmatter `sources:` is invalid at the publication layer; sources are intake material, references are the cited cut used by writing/editing.\n  Draft creation and the repair action default to `CC BY 4.0` with `https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/` and explicit syndication rights for indexing, snippets, large previews, feeds, Google Search/News/Discover surfaces, and attributed downstream distribution.\n- **Recent shipped posts:** `content/posts/michigan-program-helps-new-farmers-amidst-rising-crop-failure.md` was repaired for H2/body separation, published through the newsroom publish action on 2026-06-19, redeployed, and enriched with one warranted internal link from `climate breakdown` to `/posts/uk-retail-sales-spike-masks-climate-driven-supply-fragility`. It passes standards with `publishable: true`, `draft: false`, and no findings; its content-quality score is 78, with remaining revision pressure around additional internal-link opportunities, uncited bibliography, and SEO-term repetition. `content/posts/ecb-raises-interest-rates-amid-sovereign-debt-climate-disasters.md` was published through the newsroom publish action on 2026-06-19 after the manuscript queue exposed it as a standards-clear draft; the public site was redeployed and the article is visible on the homepage. That publication exposed a standards gap; H2/body fusion is now a blocking standards finding, so the article is visible as post-publication standards debt instead of a clear manuscript. `content/posts/acur"},{"line":66,"matchedTerms":["source cycle"],"text":"  `content/posts/uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall.md` (2026-06-19) was generated by `pnpm run source:cycle` from the `collapse-finance` wire, normalized from intake `sources:` to canonical CSL `references:`, enriched with source-cycle same-site link guidance, human-edited to remove unsupported speculative climate/resource claims, given a second ONS reference, published through `POST /operators/manuscripts/uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall/publish`, and redeployed. It is live on the homepage and RSS feed, has `referenceCount: 2`, `draft: false`, and no standards findings.\n  `content/posts/world-cup-faces-air-quality-disaster-as-wildfires-threaten.md` was repaired through the newsroom heading-repair route after the repair splitter was widened to catch short labels followed by `The ...` body starts, then published through `POST /operators/manuscripts/world-cup-faces-air-quality-disaster-as-wildfires-threaten/publish` on 2026-06-19. It has `referenceCount: 2`, `draft: false`, `gates_clear: true`, and no standards findings.\n  `content/posts/amazon-s-astro-and-the-energy-cost-of-giving.md` was generated by the 2026-06-19 source cycle from the `collapse-tech` wire after 19 new source records were written. The wrapper normalized two intake URLs into canonical CSL `references:` and added draft same-site link guidance. Human editing tightened the description and changed one H2 to include `AI infrastructure energy use`, then the article was published through `POST /operators/manuscripts/amazon-s-astro-and-the-energy-cost-of-giving/publish`. It has `referenceCount: 2`, `draft: false`, `gates_clear: true`, and no standards findings.\n  `content/posts/ai-compute-bottleneck-power-problem.md` (2026-06-19) was sourced from a research packet over the refresh"},{"line":71,"matchedTerms":["search console"],"text":"  The `hello-world` article renders that A-Ads iframe from frontmatter under the fingerprint/banner image, and the public CSP/privacy policy recognize the A-Ads frame.\n- **The content and data stores are submodules.** Posts live in `content/posts/`; data lives in `data/`, with append-only traces under `data/logs/` and current JSON records under `data/records/`.\n- **The content-production instance is configured in** `content-production.config.json`: Koios's farm property, seed terms, beat sources, Search Console property, front-matter namespace, voice, gates, model, content store, and data store.\n  `package.json` is package/site metadata, not the farm instance carrier.\n  The seed vocabulary now explicitly covers climate, technology, politics, economy/finance, AI infrastructure/labor/policy, and crypto/stablecoin/liquidity terms."},{"line":88,"matchedTerms":["source cycle","search console"],"text":"\n- The manual workshop references `scripts/new-post.mjs` and `scripts/lint-post.mjs`, but no `scripts/` directory is present in this package. Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes.\n- The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand.\n- The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting.\n- The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream work should make the farm recorder write unique draf"},{"line":89,"matchedTerms":["source cycle","repair packet"],"text":"- The manual workshop references `scripts/new-post.mjs` and `scripts/lint-post.mjs`, but no `scripts/` directory is present in this package. Until restored, use the documented rules directly: frontmatter shape, >=800 body words, SEO term in first paragraph and a `##`, then `pnpm --dir sites/koios.news run check` plus local server probes.\n- The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand.\n- The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting.\n- The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream work should make the farm recorder write unique draft"},{"line":90,"matchedTerms":["source cycle"],"text":"- The 2026-06-19 source cycle showed that Search Console demand pull still returns `403` for `sc-domain:koios.news`; until permissions are fixed, farm assignments come from seed/static demand rather than live search demand.\n- The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting.\n- The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream work should make the farm recorder write unique draft slugs rather than relying on Koios to repair after the write.\n- Remaining standards debt after the latest article publication and Sigma SEO-heading repair: 60 posts total, 50 public, 10 drafts, 40 publishable under the current gate, with findings for `malformed-h2-heading` (13), `body-too-short` (8), `missing-references` (7), and `missin"},{"line":91,"matchedTerms":["repair packet"],"text":"- The 2026-06-19 source cycles showed assignment/story alignment problems: the farm paired `crypto mining energy demand` with a UK retail heat story earlier, then paired `farmland and water rights` with a UK borrowing/water-shares market story. Human editing changed the latter SEO term to `UK borrowing and water shares` and narrowed the copy. Source-cycle repair packets now expose this friction from run traces, and operators can convert those packets into durable editing assignments. A repair assignment now exists for `ai-capital-expenditure-bubble` after an unsupported-demand obstruction. Future writing-flow work should make research packets or claim overlap gate the assignment before drafting.\n- The escalated 2026-06-19 source cycle also exposed a slug/path collision: the farm tried to write a new `uk-bond-yields-rise-as-water-company-shares-fall` draft over a previously published article, temporarily reverting it to `draft: true` with no references. The article was restored, and the source-cycle wrapper now snapshots existing manuscripts, restores collided paths, skips post-processing for those candidates, and reports `candidateWriteCollisions`. Future upstream work should make the farm recorder write unique draft slugs rather than relying on Koios to repair after the write.\n- Remaining standards debt after the latest article publication and Sigma SEO-heading repair: 60 posts total, 50 public, 10 drafts, 40 publishable under the current gate, with findings for `malformed-h2-heading` (13), `body-too-short` (8), `missing-references` (7), and `missing-beat` (6). License, license URL, syndication rights, article-level `sources:`, and legacy reference-key debt are cleared from the corpus. The heading repair action now catches the common short-label-plus-`The ...` body fus"}],"score":50,"projectionMatchedTerms":["technology"]},{"digest":"sha256:0551cbc60854685b5d2f6804a14d963dd0eb83763bcd8172be3d9554a8947c28","byteSize":25323,"excerptTruncated":false,"owner":{"nodeId":null,"package":null},"status":"current","matchedTerms":["content farm","content mill"],"snippets":[{"line":11,"matchedTerms":["content farm"],"text":"## Abstract\n\nClickbait is best understood not as â€œbad headlines,â€ but as a **packaging technology for attention**. It converts uncertainty, emotion, identity, and platform visibility into clicks, views, comments, shares, ad impressions, affiliate conversions, or ranking signals. Its defining problem is **warrant failure**: the headline, thumbnail, preview card, or prompt makes a promise that the destination content does not adequately justify. Clickbait can appear in professional newsrooms, content farms, MFA sites, YouTube thumbnails, TikTok captions, Google Discover cards, affiliate â€œbest-ofâ€ posts, AI-generated feeds, and disinformation-for-profit operations. It is a rhetorical form, a labor regime, and an infrastructure effect.\n\nThe central claim of this memo is that clickbait packages attention by creating **epistemic debt**: it borrows the readerâ€™s cognitive effort in advance, promising a future payoff of knowledge, affect, recognition, outrage, shame, fear, or desire. The debt is paid when the content delivers a proportionate answer; it defaults when the package withholds, exaggerates, misdirects, or mismatches the article, image, video, or product page."},{"line":32,"matchedTerms":["content farm"],"text":"|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Clickbait** | Headline, thumbnail, caption, preview, title, hook | Curiosity, affect, withholding, exaggeration, mismatch | Clicks, watch time, CTR, shares, comments | It is a rhetorical and semiotic tactic, not necessarily an organization. |\n| **Content farm** | Publishing organization or network | High-volume production optimized for traffic/search | Ads, affiliates, syndication | It is an organizational model; it may use clickbait but need not always. |\n| **Content mill** | Labor arrangement | Piece-rate, ghostwritten, templated writing under speed/SEO pressure | Client fees, agency margins | It names labor conditions, not just content quality. |\n| **MFA site** | Ad-arbitrage infrastructure | Paid traffic in, high ad density/outstream/slideshows out | Programmatic ad revenue | It is built around advertising yield and inventory mechanics. ANA found MFA sites made up 21% of impressions and 15% of spend in a studied subset. ([adslot.com](https://www.adslot.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ana-programmatic-media-supply-chain-transparency-study.pdf)) |"},{"line":33,"matchedTerms":["content mill"],"text":"| **Clickbait** | Headline, thumbnail, caption, preview, title, hook | Curiosity, affect, withholding, exaggeration, mismatch | Clicks, watch time, CTR, shares, comments | It is a rhetorical and semiotic tactic, not necessarily an organization. |\n| **Content farm** | Publishing organization or network | High-volume production optimized for traffic/search | Ads, affiliates, syndication | It is an organizational model; it may use clickbait but need not always. |\n| **Content mill** | Labor arrangement | Piece-rate, ghostwritten, templated writing under speed/SEO pressure | Client fees, agency margins | It names labor conditions, not just content quality. |\n| **MFA site** | Ad-arbitrage infrastructure | Paid traffic in, high ad density/outstream/slideshows out | Programmatic ad revenue | It is built around advertising yield and inventory mechanics. ANA found MFA sites made up 21% of impressions and 15% of spend in a studied subset. ([adslot.com](https://www.adslot.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ana-programmatic-media-supply-chain-transparency-study.pdf)) |\n| **SEO spam** | Search manipulation strategy | Scaled pages, keyword stuffing, link manipulation, scraping, parasite SEO | Search traffic, affiliates, ads | It targets ranking systems; clickbait targets user choice after exposure. |"},{"line":172,"matchedTerms":["content mill"],"text":"The democratic risk is that politically consequential content can be optimized with the same attention-arbitrage logic. The Macedonian fake-news industry around the 2016 U.S. election is a canonical case of disinformation-for-profit: fieldwork in Veles found that economics, not necessarily ideology, drove many producers. ([cambridge.org](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ps-political-science-and-politics/article/macedonian-fake-news-industry-and-the-2016-us-election/79F67A4F23148D230F120A3BD7E3384F?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nThe labor risk is that â€œslopâ€ is often blamed on individual writers or creators while the production system rewards speed, templating, unpaid testing, low editorial oversight, and insecure freelance work. Content mills and creator platforms can discipline workers through piece rates, algorithmic visibility, opaque client demands, and constant optimization pressure. ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10208186/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n---"},{"line":182,"matchedTerms":["content mill"],"text":"3. **Multimodality:** How should we score warrant when the headline is accurate but the thumbnail is misleading, or vice versa?  \n4. **AI generation:** Will LLMs mostly amplify existing clickbait genres, or create new ones through personalized synthetic curiosity gaps?  \n5. **Labor:** How can policy distinguish exploitative content mills from precarious freelancers trying to survive unstable platform markets?  \n6. **Advertising accountability:** Can ad buyers define MFA precisely enough to avoid overblocking legitimate small publishers?  \n7. **Search and answer engines:** As search becomes generative, how will SEO spam mutate into answer-engine optimization and recommendation poisoning?  "},{"line":227,"matchedTerms":["content farm"],"text":"**Google Search Spam Policies and March 2024 update** â€” Official policy framework for scaled content abuse, expired domain abuse, site reputation abuse, scraping, link spam, and low-value ranking manipulation. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies))\n\n**NewsGuard AI Tracking Center** â€” Current monitoring source on AI content farms and AI-enabled misinformation. Useful for defining AI slop as a production-and-oversight problem, not merely an aesthetic complaint. ([newsguardtech.com](https://www.newsguardtech.com/special-reports/ai-tracking-center))\n\n**Bevendorff et al., â€œIs Google Getting Worse?â€** â€” Longitudinal study of SEO spam in search engines, especially affiliate product reviews. Useful for connecting clickbait to SEO, affiliates, and search-quality degradation. ([link.springer.com](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-56063-7_4?utm_source=chatgpt.com))"}],"score":35,"projectionMatchedTerms":["technology"]},{"digest":"sha256:8e09ea2292deb107d3ca11d068a296ac5727551b101a3fe0c876140f4962c8a7","byteSize":9884,"excerptTruncated":false,"owner":{"nodeId":null,"package":null},"status":"current","matchedTerms":["content mill"],"snippets":[{"line":7,"matchedTerms":["content mill"],"text":"author: \"Nik Koios\"\nslug: \"hello-world\"\ndescription: \"Koios.News is a collapse-aware autonomous news site: a newsroom, content mill, standards system, and public web publication learning to report through facts.\"\nsummary: \"Koios.News is an autonomous, collapse-aware news publication. It reads climate, technology, politics, and the economy as one compounding system, then turns sourced evidence into articles, feeds, fingerprints, and operator-visible newsroom work.\"\nkeywords: [\"Koios.News\"]"},{"line":45,"matchedTerms":["content mill"],"text":"---\nKoios.\nNews is now here as a public experiment in autonomous journalism: a collapse-aware newsroom, a standards system, a content mill, and a website that tries to make its own work visible instead of hiding the machinery behind a smooth front page.\nThis is the hello-world article because every strange machine needs one plain sentence at the beginning.\nThe sentence is this: Koios."}],"score":17,"projectionMatchedTerms":["technology"]}]},"contextualBundleDigest":"sha256:80d8b2e1655bae668de928a9880bfa257a1faba403bcfc807b72eff525f0c65b","gapBundle":{"type":"GapBundle","sourceRecoveryGapBundleDigest":"sha256:1a91f4a07478585b156b5e41f44c35b9e5008f0d51d0e4b8da2f5adf954140e5","gapCount":2,"gaps":[{"type":"unmatched-term","term":"turned dark side"},{"type":"candidate-truncation","matched":17,"admitted":8,"omitted":9}]},"gapBundleDigest":"sha256:43e32abdc4fc2960b8c45b12b22299fab0b7919428c5d8073ae3cfec957c60b1","filesystemReads":0,"customerTerritoryMutation":false,"sourceActivity":"urn:koios:f32ad958d3e87c6abc1553e3384690fe0c68273aa37e9cdf1a75f8a58551533d","dryRun":false}}}}]

OPERATIVE PIPELINE ARTIFACTS
[{"sourceId":"runtime/producing-content.config.json","digest":"sha256:f86ff8166bdb95a08c138b7a0f4bc5e5b0f2974789e3ad57ea8b0d76dc5d128f","content":"{\n  \"name\": \"koios.news\",\n  \"port\": 18809,\n  \"newscable\": {\n    \"title\": \"Koios News Cable\",\n    \"description\": \"Koios.News witnessed topic intake: environment, technology, economy, politics, culture, war, and adjacent wires owned by the publication, as IPTC-subject claim-state bundles.\",\n    \"port\": 18809,\n    \"domain\": \"cable.koios.news\",\n    \"routing\": \"subfolder\",\n    \"routingPrefix\": \"newswires\",\n    \"wires\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"environment\",\n        \"title\": \"Environment\",\n        \"description\": \"Environment coverage â€” tipping points, crop failure, extreme heat, insurance withdrawal, water scarcity â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"climate change\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/climate-crisis/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://insideclimatenews.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://grist.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.carbonbrief.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/nclimate.rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"economy\",\n        \"title\": \"Economy\",\n        \"description\": \"Economy coverage â€” stranded assets, climate disasters and sovereign debt, de-dollarization, farmland and water rights â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"economy and finance\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/uk/business/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/business/economics/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.federalreserve.gov/feeds/press_all.xml\",\n            \"max\": 10\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.nber.org/rss/new.xml\",\n            \"max\": 10\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.coindesk.com/arc/outboundfeeds/rss/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theblock.co/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"politics\",\n        \"title\": \"Politics\",\n        \"description\": \"Politics coverage â€” climate migration, disaster response cuts, resource nationalism, state capacity failure â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"politics and government\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/world/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.aljazeera.com/xml/rss/all.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://rss.nytimes.com/services/xml/rss/nyt/Politics.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://feeds.npr.org/1014/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"technology\",\n        \"title\": \"Technology\",\n        \"description\": \"Technology coverage â€” data center water use, AI grid demand, semiconductor fragility, satellite outages â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"technology\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://feeds.arstechnica.com/arstechnica/index\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theregister.com/headlines.atom\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theregister.com/software/ai_ml/headlines.atom\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.technologyreview.com/topic/artificial-intelligence/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://spectrum.ieee.org/rss/fulltext\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"degrowth\",\n        \"title\": \"Degrowth\",\n        \"description\": \"Degrowth and post-growth coverage â€” planned economic contraction, sufficiency, wellbeing economies, localization, and the limits-to-growth tradition â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"degrowth\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.resilience.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://unevenearth.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://rwer.wordpress.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.localfutures.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://solar.lowtechmagazine.com/posts/index.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"decolonization\",\n        \"title\": \"Decolonization\",\n        \"description\": \"Decolonization coverage â€” climate reparations, extraction and the global south, Indigenous land and sovereignty, sovereign debt, and neocolonial trade â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"decolonization\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://africasacountry.com/feed\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.blackagendareport.com/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://ictnews.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/rss/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://theconversation.com/global/articles.atom\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"war\",\n        \"title\": \"War\",\n        \"description\": \"Conflict, extremism, and geopolitical coverage â€” political violence, terrorism and radicalization, accelerationism, open-source conflict investigation, and war â€” as provenanced claim-state bundles.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"political violence\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.isdglobal.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://gnet-research.org/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.bellingcat.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.crisisgroup.org/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://warontherocks.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"culture\",\n        \"title\": \"Culture\",\n        \"description\": \"Arts, culture, entertainment and media coverage â€” institutions, creative labor, platforms, heritage, and the cultural life of a society under change.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"arts and culture\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/culture/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://hyperallergic.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/books/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/film/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/music/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/rss\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://variety.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://deadline.com/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/feed/\",\n            \"max\": 12\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"sports\",\n        \"title\": \"Sports\",\n        \"description\": \"Scores, schedules, competitions, athletes, teams, sports business and the public institutions around play, reported as a first-class general-news desk.\",\n        \"keyword\": \"sports news\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/rss\",\n            \"max\": 18\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://feeds.bbci.co.uk/sport/rss.xml\",\n            \"max\": 18\n          },\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"rss\",\n            \"url\": \"https://www.espn.com/espn/rss/news\",\n            \"max\": 18\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"properties\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"koios.news\",\n      \"contentDir\": \"content/posts\",\n      \"dataDir\": \"data\",\n      \"runsFile\": \"mill-runs.jsonl\",\n      \"author\": \"Nik Koios\",\n      \"termKey\": \"koios\",\n      \"demand\": {\n        \"property\": \"sc-domain:koios.news\",\n        \"days\": 28\n      },\n      \"voice\": {\n        \"persona\": [\n          \"You are Nik Koios, Editor-in-Chief of Koios.News, a general news publication with unusual strength in systems analysis.\",\n          \"Your frame: report the witnessed event first; connect it to larger systems only when admitted evidence licenses the connection.\",\n          \"Climate, material limits, institutions, technology, capital, and power are available analytical lenses, not conclusions imposed on every story.\",\n          \"Your voice: grounded, specific, humane; confident-but-comfortable opinionation; concise; educational;\",\n          \"one or two examples per point, never ten; never breathless, never prophetic.\"\n        ],\n        \"laws\": [\n          \"do not impose Koios's historical collapse frame on an event; use systems analysis only when the assignment and admitted sources warrant the particular connection\",\n          \"when a source names a policy, right, framework, or concept without explaining its operational contents, do not invent an explanatory gloss or enumeration for it\",\n          \"do not add temporal priority, causal order, geographic scope, or affected categories that the admitted sources do not state\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"seeds\": [\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"environment\",\n          \"term\": \"climate tipping points\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"environment\",\n          \"term\": \"crop failure and food prices\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"environment\",\n          \"term\": \"urban water scarcity\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"environment\",\n          \"term\": \"insurance climate risk withdrawal\",\n          \"priority\": 9\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"environment\",\n          \"term\": \"extreme heat and infrastructure\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"data center water use\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"AI demand on the grid\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"AI data center power demand\",\n          \"priority\": 9\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"AI infrastructure energy use\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"AI labor displacement\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"AI policy and state capacity\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"semiconductor supply chain fragility\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"satellite internet outage\",\n          \"priority\": 5\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"software security updates\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"technology\",\n          \"term\": \"technology product release dates\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"climate stranded assets\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"AI capital expenditure bubble\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"AI infrastructure debt\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"crypto mining energy demand\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"stablecoin dollar demand\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"crypto liquidity crisis\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"cost of living climate shocks\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"climate disasters and sovereign debt\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"de-dollarization of commodity trade\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"farmland and water rights\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"consumer prices and inflation\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"mortgage and interest rates\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"jobs and unemployment\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"tax deadlines and filing changes\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"economy\",\n          \"term\": \"product recalls and consumer safety\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"climate migration and border policy\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"AI regulation geopolitics\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"crypto campaign finance\",\n          \"priority\": 5\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"data center permitting fights\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"disaster response funding cuts\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"resource nationalism\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"state capacity failure\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"election dates and voting rules\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"congressional votes and legislation\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"supreme court decisions\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"politics\",\n          \"term\": \"federal benefits and eligibility\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"post-growth economics\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"limits to growth\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"wellbeing economy\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"sufficiency and consumption\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"degrowth\",\n          \"term\": \"localization and resilience\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"climate reparations\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"extraction and the global south\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"indigenous land rights\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"sovereign debt and the global south\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"decolonization\",\n          \"term\": \"neocolonial trade\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"neo-Nazi accelerationism\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"online radicalization\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"terrorism and extremism\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"great replacement conspiracy\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"stochastic terrorism\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"geopolitical conflict escalation\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"disinformation and war\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"war\",\n          \"term\": \"state fragility and armed conflict\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"cultural institutions funding\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"streaming and creative labor\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"heritage and museums\",\n          \"priority\": 5\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"celebrity news\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"film and television releases\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"streaming release dates\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"music tours and album releases\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"awards and red carpets\",\n          \"priority\": 7\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"culture\",\n          \"term\": \"books film and music recommendations\",\n          \"priority\": 6\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"sports\",\n          \"term\": \"sports scores and schedules\",\n          \"priority\": 9\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"sports\",\n          \"term\": \"championship results\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        },\n        {\n          \"beat\": \"sports\",\n          \"term\": \"player and team news\",\n          \"priority\": 8\n        }\n      ],\n      \"beatSources\": {\n        \"environment\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/environment/\",\n            \"max\": 36\n          }\n        ],\n        \"technology\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/technology/\",\n            \"max\": 36\n          }\n        ],\n        \"economy\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/economy/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"politics\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/politics/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"degrowth\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/degrowth/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"decolonization\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/decolonization/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"war\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/war/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"culture\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/culture/\",\n            \"max\": 24\n          }\n        ],\n        \"sports\": [\n          {\n            \"adapter\": \"newswire\",\n            \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:18809/newswires/sports/\",\n            \"max\": 36\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"beatCategory\": {\n        \"environment\": \"Environment\",\n        \"technology\": \"Technology\",\n        \"economy\": \"Economy\",\n        \"politics\": \"Politics\",\n        \"degrowth\": \"Degrowth\",\n        \"decolonization\": \"Decolonization\",\n        \"war\": \"War\",\n        \"culture\": \"Culture\",\n        \"sports\": \"Sports\"\n      },\n      \"genericTermWords\": [\n        \"climate\",\n        \"collapse\",\n        \"breakdown\",\n        \"crisis\",\n        \"2025\",\n        \"2026\",\n        \"global\"\n      ],\n      \"provider\": \"nim\",\n      \"model\": \"qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b\",\n      \"maxTokens\": 3000,\n      \"gateModel\": \"fable\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/lib/law/reported-analysis-genre-contract.json","digest":"sha256:d3f931f03627a8294e4264e0c8350af005d1682d1275946128f8dce01b2876f2","content":"{\n  \"kind\": \"koios.genre-contract\",\n  \"id\": \"reported-analysis/v1\",\n  \"promise\": \"A timely report organized around one witnessed event, with Koios's systems interpretation visibly subordinate to and licensed by the reporting.\",\n  \"evidenceBudget\": {\n    \"targetWords\": {\n      \"minimum\": 800,\n      \"maximum\": 1100\n    },\n    \"minimumDistinctAssertions\": 15,\n    \"minimumPrimaryAssertions\": 15,\n    \"maximumWordsPerAssertion\": 55,\n    \"law\": \"The requested length is licensed only when the admitted packet carries enough distinct source-bound assertions to sustain it. When the budget does not clear, acquire reporting or commission a narrower genre before writer inference.\"\n  },\n  \"writerPromptLaws\": [\n    \"Write reported analysis, not a thematic essay: the event and the people, institutions, places, dates, quantities, documents, and decisions in the sources remain the article's spine.\",\n    \"Before drafting, silently inventory the packet's supported claims and distinct new details; every paragraph and every section must advance at least one of them rather than restating the frame.\",\n    \"Treat each source as evidence only for its own actors, place, time, scope, attribution, and modality; never use thematic similarity as a bridge between different events.\",\n    \"Koios's systems interpretation may explain a pattern made visible by supported facts, but it may not supply an unreported causal mechanism, motive, consequence, category, or analogy.\",\n    \"Use straight attribution when the source is an allegation, estimate, forecast, interpretation, institutional statement, or contested claim; do not launder it into Koios's voice.\",\n    \"Model output is never evidence. Prefer original and named sources, preserve source motives and uncertainty, and treat consequential claims as publishable only to the extent they are corroborated by the admitted packet.\",\n    \"When the story criticizes a person or institution, alleges wrongdoing, or reports a dispute, represent the subject's response or state only what the admitted reporting establishes about a documented opportunity to respond; otherwise return REPORTING_NEEDED.\",\n    \"Avoid needless harm, sensationalism, stereotypes, unattributed opinion, and commercial influence; keep reported analysis visibly distinct from advocacy and advertising.\",\n    \"A headline may intensify relevance but not certainty; it names the subject before withholding an answer and preserves material attribution, uncertainty, scope, and correction state.\",\n    \"The lede tells the reader what happened, where, to whom, and why it matters now; do not open with a universal thesis, scene-setting abstraction, or generic collapse language.\",\n    \"Do not create a section unless the packet supplies a distinct factual development for it; if fewer than three sections are supportable, refuse with REPORTING_NEEDED instead of padding.\",\n    \"The ending must close on the most consequential supported unresolved fact, decision, deadline, conflict, or question facing the named actors; never substitute an abstract meditation for reporting.\",\n    \"Give readers enough factual payoff in the headline, description, summary, and lede to make an informed click or no-click decision; withhold an answer when useful, never the subject.\"\n  ],\n  \"gates\": [\n    \"event spine\",\n    \"paragraph-level source warrant\",\n    \"attribution and modality preservation\",\n    \"headline-body congruity\",\n    \"non-repetition\",\n    \"correction readiness\",\n    \"exact-manuscript independent editorial acceptance\"\n  ],\n  \"derivedFrom\": [\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/journalism.md\",\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md\",\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/circulation-surface.md\",\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md\",\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md\",\n    \"dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/clickbait.md\"\n  ]\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/lib/management/offices/editor-in-chief.json","digest":"sha256:a495154f810510c7e1c32c44a003d6cee817c55bc578bf2272abd836c1fb0de6","content":"{\n  \"kind\": \"koios.office\",\n  \"id\": \"editor-in-chief\",\n  \"incumbent\": \"Nik Koios\",\n  \"authority\": [\n    \"adopt and amend newsroom standards subject to the Leader's mandate\",\n    \"turn audience or public-interest demand into assignments\",\n    \"accept or refuse provider offers for research, reporting, writing, and independent editorial judgment\",\n    \"authorize publication only after exact-manuscript acceptance\"\n  ],\n  \"duties\": [\"judging-accountable-publication\", \"learning-from-newsroom-outcomes\"],\n  \"separation\": \"May authorize editorial publication but may not silently grant delivery infrastructure authority or mutate a provider's territory.\"\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/lib/management/duties/judging-accountable-publication.json","digest":"sha256:caf394bee2ed0381b30eeef5250a91279514a70148e3250691ec367c19768be0","content":"{\n  \"kind\": \"koios.duty\",\n  \"id\": \"judging-accountable-publication\",\n  \"office\": \"editor-in-chief\",\n  \"precondition\": \"One exact manuscript, its deterministic standards reading, source and claim records, production provenance, and any prior failure evidence are available.\",\n  \"move\": \"Procure independent judgment from an accepted provider under a provider-validated bounded tool schema, preserve its prose report, and let deterministic Koios code invoke exactly one selected editorial tool. Free-form prose labels never create publication authority.\",\n  \"postcondition\": \"The exact manuscript has an append-only accepted, revise, or refuse judgment carrying the validated tool-selection digest; publication is possible only when submit_editorial_acceptance was selected for deterministically clear bytes.\",\n  \"refusals\": [\"missing source custody\", \"missing provider offer\", \"provider result not accepted\", \"manuscript digest changed\", \"standards gate failed\"]\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/lib/management/duties/learning-from-newsroom-outcomes.json","digest":"sha256:c83849760dd0dd0aa9156bb6eb83234dc48b40c759035dfeebfb452bd38e59e9","content":"{\n  \"kind\": \"koios.duty\",\n  \"id\": \"learning-from-newsroom-outcomes\",\n  \"office\": \"editor-in-chief\",\n  \"purpose\": \"Turn durable newsroom outcomes into bounded replay, routing-review, observation, or standards-review proposals without allowing attention economics to manufacture editorial warrant.\",\n  \"contract\": \"koios-news/newsroom-learning-pulse/v1\",\n  \"inputs\": [\n    \"assignment and newsroom circuit settlements\",\n    \"independent editorial judgments\",\n    \"provider receipts and costs\",\n    \"publication, correction, attention, and revenue observations\"\n  ],\n  \"outputs\": [\n    \"digest-bound issue readings\",\n    \"typed next-move proposals in the research-lessons ledger\"\n  ],\n  \"mustNot\": [\n    \"publish or replay an issue\",\n    \"silently change a provider route or newsroom standard\",\n    \"treat clicks, revenue, or model availability as evidence for a claim\"\n  ]\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/data/records/research/2026/07/17/2026-07-17-koios-news-research.jsonl","digest":"sha256:e0e4513adc7e7372b8f4bce81bcac2f12ec165c6440a75d9d2230f75c7509261","content":"{\"kind\":\"koios.newsroom-practice-reconciliation\",\"recordedAt\":\"2026-07-17T23:30:00-05:00\",\"demand\":\"Recover Koios's paid newsroom prior art, reconcile it with authoritative modern digital-journalism practice, and induce only the duties needed by the live production circuit.\",\"localPriorArt\":[\"../../dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md\",\"../../dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/news-topology.md\",\"../../dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/claims-to-prose.md\",\"../../dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md\",\"src/newsroom/departments.mts\",\"../../../../../red-cup-engineering/src/content/assets/legacy/2026-07-03-producing-content/src/content-farm/newsroom.mts\"],\"authorities\":[{\"name\":\"Society of Professional Journalists Code of Ethics\",\"url\":\"https://www.spj.org/spj-code-of-ethics/\",\"obligations\":[\"verify before release and prefer original sources\",\"provide context and avoid oversimplification in promotion and summaries\",\"identify sources and consider their motives\",\"seek response from subjects of criticism\",\"distinguish news from advertising\",\"correct promptly and prominently\",\"explain methods and ethical choices\"]},{\"name\":\"Reuters Journalistic Standards\",\"url\":\"https://reutersagency.com/about/standards-values/\",\"obligations\":[\"hold accuracy sacrosanct\",\"seek fair comment\",\"correct transparently\",\"disclose conflicts\",\"avoid unattributed opinion, fabrication, plagiarism, sensationalism, and hype\",\"label fact-based news separately from opinion and analysis\"]},{\"name\":\"Associated Press News Values and Principles\",\"url\":\"https://www.ap.org/about/news-values-and-principles/\",\"obligations\":[\"vet Internet material and attribute the original source\",\"treat generative output as unvetted source material\",\"distinguish correlation from causation in data work\",\"label corrections and propagate them to consumer and subscriber surfaces\"]},{\"name\":\"Associated Press Standards Around Generative AI\",\"url\":\"https://www.ap.org/standards-around-generative-ai\",\"obligations\":[\"apply editorial judgment and sourcing standards to model output\",\"do not infer authenticity from realistic appearance\",\"identify original sources and corroborating reports\",\"withhold doubtful material\"]},{\"name\":\"IPTC NewsML-G2 and ninjs\",\"url\":\"https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/\",\"obligations\":[\"carry news as versioned exchange items and packages\",\"identify workflow actors and item relationships\",\"separate verification and authorization for release\",\"preserve interoperable metadata across text, image, video, audio, and downstream transmissions\"]},{\"name\":\"The Trust Project Trust Indicators\",\"url\":\"https://thetrustproject.org/\",\"obligations\":[\"disclose ownership, mission, standards, ethics, and conflicts\",\"identify journalist expertise\",\"label news, analysis, opinion, and paid work distinctly\",\"show references and methods\",\"make corrections policy visible\"]},{\"name\":\"News Product Alliance 2025 Census\",\"url\":\"https://newsproduct.org/blog/the-state-of-product-management-in-journalism-2025-census\",\"obligations\":[\"join editorial ethics, audience need, revenue, and technology through cross-functional product decisions\",\"measure the audience experience without converting demand into evidence\"]}],\"settledTopology\":{\"newswire\":\"A separately hireable source-stream enterprise that fetches and witnesses source items with provenance; no consuming newsroom owns its internal fetching apparatus.\",\"newscable\":\"A separately hireable collection and routing enterprise that composes admitted newswires into topic and delivery surfaces; it does not grant publication warrant.\",\"koiosNews\":\"Contracts newswire and newscable services, admits returned evidence into its own corpus, and runs a Koios-owned MAPE-K editorial loop over that local knowledge.\",\"conglomerateResearch\":\"A separately contractable contextual corpus whose already-paid analysis may enrich assignments and explanation, while remaining distinct from fresh Web evidence and sharing its production cost across lawful consumers.\"},\"inducedCircuit\":[{\"office\":\"Assignment\",\"duty\":\"Turn audience/search demand and source events into a bounded reporting question and genre without treating demand as evidence or exact-match copy.\"},{\"office\":\"Reporting Packet\",\"duty\":\"Acquire adequate original reporting, preserve full usable text and source roles, test coherence and source power, and request more reporting instead of commissioning unsupported prose.\"},{\"office\":\"Writer\",\"duty\":\"Realize one admitted reporting packet into attributed prose while preserving modality, source boundaries, genre, and the right to refuse thin reporting.\"},{\"office\":\"Editor and Standards\",\"duty\":\"Judge manuscript plus sources and production trace; on failure locate the earliest causal layer, prescribe one apparatus repair, and name the replay point.\"},{\"office\":\"Publication\",\"duty\":\"Release only the exact digest accepted by standards and Editor; project labels, references, provenance, feeds, structured data, rights, and revenue separation.\"},{\"office\":\"Corrections\",\"duty\":\"Invalidate and repair every dependent page, feed, metadata, social, syndication, and archive projection while retaining visible lineage.\"},{\"office\":\"Audience and Product\",\"duty\":\"Measure discovery, usefulness, retention, trust, and revenue as product evidence while preserving the firewall between audience demand, advertising, and editorial warrant.\"}],\"inductionRule\":\"These are candidate service boundaries recovered from prior art and professional practice. A duty becomes a separately seated office or enterprise only when repeated live traces show an independently contractable obstruction, authority boundary, or reusable product; until then it remains a duty in the smallest existing owner.\",\"firstReplay\":\"Run the failed candidate through Sonnet Editor with the manuscript, full source records, exact writer-time packet when available, operative skills/scripts/configuration, and recovered newsroom research. Repair the earliest layer, replay from there, and publish only after a digest-matching independent acceptance.\"}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/src/researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mts","digest":"sha256:4ea3f63a61bf03735e924e22753401deb4cc3d14f2123f0d830d8663a5fb0686","content":"// researching/sourcing/source-cycle â€” one Koios-owned source refresh plus farm\n// tick. This is the operator-visible act that turns external feeds into local\n// wire evidence, then lets the content farm assemble context and draft.\n\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync, readdirSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from \"node:path\";\nimport process from \"node:process\";\n\nimport {\n  createContentFarmService,\n  contentFarmCandidateSnapshots,\n  contentFarmCollisionPathSet,\n  contentFarmRepairPacketsReading,\n  contentFarmSourceCyclePlanFromDocument,\n  contentFarmSourceCycleReading,\n  farmCycleReading,\n  normalizeContentFarmCandidateReferences,\n  readContentFarmConfig,\n  readContentFarmConfigDocument,\n  sourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance,\n  sourceCycleDraftInternalLinkSuggestion,\n  restoreContentFarmCandidateWriteCollisions,\n  decodeFrontMatterDocument,\n  encodeFrontMatterDocument,\n  type ContentFarmCandidateState,\n  type FarmCycle,\n  type FarmCycleReading,\n  type ContentFarmRepairPacket,\n  type ContentFarmRepairPacketsReading,\n  type ContentFarmRepairTask,\n  type ContentFarmRepairTaskKind,\n  type ContentFarmSourceCyclePlan,\n  type ContentFarmSourceCycleReading,\n  type ContentFarmSourceCycleWirePlan,\n  type SourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance,\n  type SourceCycleCandidateReferenceNormalization,\n  type SourceCycleCandidateWriteCollision,\n  type ViewCountsReading,\n  type FetchLike,\n} from \"@emsenn/organizing-a-content-farm\";\nimport { createKoiosNewscable } from \"../../cable-app.mjs\";\nimport { DEFAULT_CONTENT_PRODUCTION_CONFIG } from \"../../config.mjs\";\nimport { loadPosts } from \"../../content.mjs\";\nimport { koiosMasthead } from \"../../index.mjs\";\nimport { koiosItems } from \"../../surface.mjs\";\nimport { draftInternalLinkGuidanceReading, type DraftInternalLinkSuggestion } from \"../writing/editing/publishing/managing/circulating/index.mjs\";\nimport { refreshSourceCorpus } from \"./newscable-contract.mjs\";\nimport {\n  KOIOS_DEFAULT_LICENSE,\n  KOIOS_DEFAULT_LICENSE_URL,\n  KOIOS_DEFAULT_SYNDICATION_RIGHTS,\n} from \"../writing/editing/standards.mjs\";\n\nexport type SourceWirePlan = ContentFarmSourceCycleWirePlan;\nexport type KoiosSourceCyclePlan = ContentFarmSourceCyclePlan;\n\nexport type KoiosSourceCycleReading = ContentFarmSourceCycleReading;\n\nexport type KoiosSourceCycleOptions = Readonly<{\n  configPath?: string;\n  fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n  env?: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;\n  views?: ViewCountsReading;\n  now?: () => string;\n  onSkip?: (message: string) => void;\n}>;\n\nexport type SourceCycleRepairTaskKind = ContentFarmRepairTaskKind;\nexport type SourceCycleRepairTask = ContentFarmRepairTask;\nexport type SourceCycleCandidateState = ContentFarmCandidateState;\nexport type SourceCycleRepairPacket = ContentFarmRepairPacket;\nexport type SourceCycleRepairPacketsReading = ContentFarmRepairPacketsReading;\n\nasync function contentFarmFetch(\n  configPath: string,\n  upstream: FetchLike,\n  onSkip: (message: string) => void,\n): Promise<FetchLike> {\n  const cable = await createKoiosNewscable(configPath, onSkip);\n  return (async (input: Parameters<FetchLike>[0], init?: Parameters<FetchLike>[1]): Promise<Response> => {\n    const raw = typeof input === \"string\" || input instanceof URL ? String(input) : input.url;\n    const url = new URL(raw);\n    const localCable = (url.hostname === \"127.0.0.1\" || url.hostname === \"localhost\") && url.port === \"18809\";\n    if (!localCable) return upstream(input, init);\n    const surface = await cable.handle(cable.domain, `${url.pathname}${url.search}`);\n    return new Response(surface.body, { status: surface.status, headers: surface.headers });\n  }) as FetchLike;\n}\n\ntype JsonRecord = Record<string, unknown>;\n\nfunction isRecord(value: unknown): value is JsonRecord {\n  return !!value && typeof value === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(value);\n}\n\nfunction stringValue(value: unknown, fallback = \"\"): string {\n  return typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\" ? value.trim() : fallback;\n}\n\nfunction strings(value: unknown): readonly string[] {\n  if (value === undefined || value === null || value === \"\") return Object.freeze([]);\n  if (Array.isArray(value)) return Object.freeze(value.flatMap((item) => strings(item)));\n  return Object.freeze([String(value)]);\n}\n\nfunction manuscriptSlug(frontMatter: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>, fallback: string): string {\n  return typeof frontMatter[\"slug\"] === \"string\" && frontMatter[\"slug\"] !== \"\" ? frontMatter[\"slug\"] : fallback;\n}\n\nfunction koiosFrontMatter(frontMatter: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>): Readonly<Record<string, unknown>> {\n  const value = frontMatter[\"koios\"];\n  return isRecord(value) ? value : Object.freeze({});\n}\n\nfunction candidateState(contentDir: string | undefined, slug: string): SourceCycleCandidateState {\n  if (contentDir === undefined || slug === \"\") return Object.freeze({ status: \"missing\" as const });\n  const path = join(contentDir, `${slug}.md`);\n  if (!existsSync(path)) return Object.freeze({ status: \"missing\" as const, path });\n  const document = decodeFrontMatterDocument(readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"));\n  const title = stringValue(document.frontMatter[\"title\"]);\n  return Object.freeze({\n    status: document.frontMatter[\"draft\"] === true ? \"draft\" as const : \"published\" as const,\n    path,\n    ...(title === \"\" ? {} : { title }),\n  });\n}\n\nexport function sourceCycleRepairPacketsReading(input: Readonly<{\n  runs: readonly unknown[];\n  contentDir?: string;\n  limit?: number;\n}>): SourceCycleRepairPacketsReading {\n  return contentFarmRepairPacketsReading({\n    runs: input.runs,\n    ...(input.limit === undefined ? {} : { limit: input.limit }),\n    candidateState: (slug) => candidateState(input.contentDir, slug),\n    constraints: Object.freeze([\n      \"This is a read-only operator backlog over farm run traces.\",\n      \"Repair packets do not rewrite, publish, or silently normalize manuscripts.\",\n      \"Intake sources remain research material; published citations remain CSL references in manuscript frontmatter and body links.\",\n      \"A published candidate stays visible as historical friction but is not counted as open.\",\n    ]),\n  });\n}\n\nexport function normalizeCandidateReferences(path: string): SourceCycleCandidateReferenceNormalization {\n  const normalized = normalizeContentFarmCandidateReferences({\n    path,\n    license: KOIOS_DEFAULT_LICENSE,\n    licenseUrl: KOIOS_DEFAULT_LICENSE_URL,\n    syndicationRights: KOIOS_DEFAULT_SYNDICATION_RIGHTS,\n  });\n  // Minimal URL promotion cannot know an article title and therefore uses the\n  // host name. Koios's own wire store does know it. Settle that locally before\n  // the reference crosses the publication boundary.\n  const storeRoot = join(dirname(dirname(dirname(resolve(path)))), \"store\");\n  const titles = new Map<string, string>();\n  const walk = (root: string): readonly string[] => {\n    try { return readdirSync(root, { withFileTypes: true }).flatMap((entry) => entry.isDirectory() ? walk(join(root, entry.name)) : entry.name.endsWith(\".json\") ? [join(root, entry.name)] : []); }\n    catch { return []; }\n  };\n  for (const sourcePath of walk(storeRoot)) {\n    try {\n      const bundle = JSON.parse(readFileSync(sourcePath, \"utf8\")) as JsonRecord;\n      const claims = Array.isArray(bundle[\"claims\"]) ? bundle[\"claims\"] : [];\n      let url = \"\"; let title = \"\";\n      for (const claim of claims) {\n        if (!isRecord(claim)) continue;\n        const proposition = isRecord(claim[\"proposition\"]) ? claim[\"proposition\"] : {};\n        if (proposition[\"predicate\"] === \"url\" && typeof proposition[\"object\"] === \"string\") url = proposition[\"object\"];\n        if (proposition[\"predicate\"] === \"title\" && typeof proposition[\"object\"] === \"string\") title = proposition[\"object\"];\n      }\n      if (url !== \"\" && title !== \"\") titles.set(url.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\"), title);\n    } catch { /* malformed wire evidence is ignored by this projection */ }\n  }\n  const document = decodeFrontMatterDocument(readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"));\n  const references = Array.isArray(document.frontMatter[\"references\"])\n    ? document.frontMatter[\"references\"].map((reference) => {\n        if (!isRecord(reference)) return reference;\n        const url = typeof reference[\"URL\"] === \"string\" ? reference[\"URL\"].replace(/\\/+$/, \"\") : \"\";\n        const title = titles.get(url);\n        return title === undefined ? reference : Object.freeze({ ...reference, title });\n      })\n    : [];\n  const titleChanged = JSON.stringify(references) !== JSON.stringify(document.frontMatter[\"references\"] ?? []);\n  if (titleChanged) {\n    writeFileSync(path, encodeFrontMatterDocument({\n      kind: \"producing-content.frontmatter-document\" as const,\n      frontMatter: Object.freeze({ ...document.frontMatter, references: Object.freeze(references) }),\n      body: document.body.endsWith(\"\\n\") ? document.body : `${document.body}\\n`,\n    }), \"utf8\");\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({ ...normalized, changed: normalized.changed || titleChanged });\n}\n\nfunction compactDraftLinkSuggestion(suggestion: DraftInternalLinkSuggestion): SourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance[\"suggestions\"][number] {\n  return sourceCycleDraftInternalLinkSuggestion({\n    targetSlug: suggestion.targetSlug,\n    targetPath: suggestion.targetPath,\n    targetHeadline: suggestion.targetHeadline,\n    matchedTerms: suggestion.matchedTerms,\n    confidence: suggestion.confidence,\n    markdownLink: suggestion.markdownLink,\n    writingGuidance: suggestion.writingGuidance,\n  });\n}\n\nexport function enrichCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance(path: string, contentDir = dirname(path)): SourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance {\n  const document = decodeFrontMatterDocument(readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"));\n  const slug = manuscriptSlug(document.frontMatter, path.replace(/^.*\\//, \"\").replace(/\\.md$/, \"\"));\n  const koios = koiosFrontMatter(document.frontMatter);\n  const term = stringValue(koios[\"seo_term\"]) || strings(document.frontMatter[\"keywords\"])[0] || stringValue(document.frontMatter[\"title\"], slug);\n  const beat = stringValue(koios[\"beat\"]);\n  const tags = strings(document.frontMatter[\"tags\"]);\n  const posts = loadPosts(contentDir);\n  const guidance = draftInternalLinkGuidanceReading({\n    posts,\n    items: koiosItems(posts, koiosMasthead()),\n    term,\n    ...(beat === \"\" ? {} : { beat }),\n    tags,\n    excludeSlugs: [slug],\n    limit: 3,\n  });\n  const suggestions = Object.freeze(guidance.suggestions.map(compactDraftLinkSuggestion));\n  const nextKoios = Object.freeze({\n    ...koios,\n    draft_internal_link_guidance: suggestions,\n  });\n  const changed = JSON.stringify(koios[\"draft_internal_link_guidance\"] ?? []) !== JSON.stringify(suggestions);\n  if (changed) {\n    writeFileSync(path, encodeFrontMatterDocument({\n      kind: \"producing-content.frontmatter-document\" as const,\n      frontMatter: Object.freeze({\n        ...document.frontMatter,\n        koios: nextKoios,\n      }),\n      body: document.body.endsWith(\"\\n\") ? document.body : `${document.body}\\n`,\n    }), \"utf8\");\n  }\n  return sourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance({\n    slug,\n    path,\n    changed,\n    suggestions,\n  });\n}\n\nfunction normalizeCycleCandidateReferences(cycle: FarmCycle, collisions: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set()): readonly SourceCycleCandidateReferenceNormalization[] {\n  const normalized: SourceCycleCandidateReferenceNormalization[] = [];\n  for (const reading of cycle.readings) {\n    const run = reading.run;\n    if (run.kind !== \"content.farm.cultivation:witness\" || run.path === null) continue;\n    if (collisions.has(resolve(run.path))) continue;\n    normalized.push(normalizeCandidateReferences(run.path));\n  }\n  return Object.freeze(normalized);\n}\n\nfunction enrichCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinks(cycle: FarmCycle, collisions: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set()): readonly SourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance[] {\n  const enriched: SourceCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance[] = [];\n  for (const reading of cycle.readings) {\n    const run = reading.run;\n    if (run.kind !== \"content.farm.cultivation:witness\" || run.path === null) continue;\n    if (collisions.has(resolve(run.path))) continue;\n    enriched.push(enrichCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance(run.path));\n  }\n  return Object.freeze(enriched);\n}\n\nexport function koiosSourceCyclePlan(options: Pick<KoiosSourceCycleOptions, \"configPath\"> = {}): KoiosSourceCyclePlan {\n  const configPath = options.configPath ?? DEFAULT_CONTENT_PRODUCTION_CONFIG;\n  const document = readContentFarmConfigDocument(configPath);\n  return contentFarmSourceCyclePlanFromDocument({\n    configPath,\n    document,\n    operator: Object.freeze({\n      planUrl: \"https://newsroom.koios.news/operators/source-cycle/plan.json\",\n      runUrl: \"https://newsroom.koios.news/operators/source-cycle\",\n      publicationGate: \"Draft candidates remain draft:true until the editing and publishing offices approve them.\",\n    }),\n  });\n}\n\nexport async function runKoiosSourceCycle(options: KoiosSourceCycleOptions = {}): Promise<KoiosSourceCycleReading> {\n  const configPath = options.configPath ?? DEFAULT_CONTENT_PRODUCTION_CONFIG;\n  const plan = koiosSourceCyclePlan({ configPath });\n  const skippedMessages: string[] = [];\n  const onSkip = (message: string): void => {\n    skippedMessages.push(message);\n    options.onSkip?.(message);\n  };\n  if (options.fetchImpl !== undefined) throw new Error(\"custom in-process fetch cannot cross the separately executable newscable provider boundary; use a declared fixture source instead\");\n  const cableResults = refreshSourceCorpus({ configPath, dataDir: join(dirname(resolve(configPath)), \"data\"), now: (options.now ?? (() => new Date().toISOString()))() }).results;\n  const farmFetch = await contentFarmFetch(configPath, options.fetchImpl ?? fetch, onSkip);\n  const farmConfig = readContentFarmConfig(configPath);\n  const snapshots = contentFarmCandidateSnapshots(farmConfig);\n  const service = createContentFarmService(farmConfig, {\n    fetchImpl: farmFetch,\n    env: options.env ?? process.env,\n    ...(options.views === undefined ? {} : { views: options.views }),\n    ...(options.now === undefined ? {} : { now: options.now }),\n  });\n  const cycle = await service.tick();\n  const candidateWriteCollisions = restoreContentFarmCandidateWriteCollisions(cycle, snapshots, {\n    instruction: \"The farm attempted to write a draft candidate at an existing manuscript path. Koios restored the existing manuscript; create a new assignment or unique draft slug before writing this candidate.\",\n  });\n  const collisionPaths = contentFarmCollisionPathSet(candidateWriteCollisions);\n  const candidateReferences = normalizeCycleCandidateReferences(cycle, collisionPaths);\n  const candidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance = enrichCycleCandidateDraftInternalLinks(cycle, collisionPaths);\n  const farm = farmCycleReading(cycle);\n  return contentFarmSourceCycleReading({\n    generatedAt: (options.now ?? (() => new Date().toISOString()))(),\n    plan,\n    cable: {\n      wireCount: cableResults.length,\n      written: cableResults.reduce((sum, result) => sum + result.written, 0),\n      skipped: cableResults.reduce((sum, result) => sum + result.skipped, 0),\n      errorCount: cableResults.reduce((sum, result) => sum + result.errors.length, 0),\n      results: cableResults,\n      skippedMessages,\n    },\n    farm,\n    candidateWriteCollisions,\n    candidateReferences,\n    candidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance,\n    editorialPolicy: {\n      record: \"draft-candidate\" as const,\n      publication: \"independent-standards-and-editorial-contract\" as const,\n      note: \"The source cycle can write draft candidates and run traces; Koios normalizes candidate citation metadata to CSL references and annotates drafts with same-site link guidance. Publication requires both deterministic standards and an exact-manuscript LISS editorial acceptance, without a per-article human gate.\",\n    },\n  });\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/src/writer-replay.mts","digest":"sha256:3b27ce2b070e82518d70d409bd9b6fe69b0f99ec84b6d2cdf7eabbd0ebc816d6","content":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// writer-replay â€” replay one failed Koios cultivation from the Writer layer.\n//\n// This is deliberately narrower than another source cycle: the assignment and\n// reporting packet stay fixed, so a changed outcome measures the Writer and its\n// instructions rather than silently buying different reporting. The previous\n// candidate remains on disk. A same-slug replay receives a revision suffix,\n// and the provider use, exact prompt digest, gates, source URLs, prior\n// manuscript digest, and resulting path are witnessed in Koios-owned data.\n\nimport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\nimport { appendFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { basename, dirname, join, resolve } from \"node:path\";\n\nimport {\n  cultivate,\n  decodeFrontMatterDocument,\n  encodeFrontMatterDocument,\n  type CultivationResult,\n} from \"@emsenn/organizing-a-content-farm\";\n\nimport { DEFAULT_CONTENT_PRODUCTION_CONFIG } from \"./config.mjs\";\nimport { koiosFarmProperty } from \"./demand.mjs\";\nimport {\n  enrichCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance,\n  normalizeCandidateReferences,\n} from \"./researching/sourcing/source-cycle.mjs\";\n\ntype JsonRecord = Record<string, unknown>;\n\nconst SITE_ROOT = dirname(DEFAULT_CONTENT_PRODUCTION_CONFIG);\nconst SUBJECT_STORE = join(SITE_ROOT, \"store/decolonization/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un.json\");\nconst CONTEXT_STORE = join(SITE_ROOT, \"store/decolonization/africa-s-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease.json\");\nconst PRIOR_SLUG = \"indigenous-leaders-confront-the-collision-of-climate-and-conflict\";\nconst TERM = \"extraction and the global south\";\nconst BEAT = \"decolonization\";\n\nfunction isRecord(value: unknown): value is JsonRecord {\n  return !!value && typeof value === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(value);\n}\n\nfunction claimValue(bundle: JsonRecord, predicate: string): string | undefined {\n  const claims = Array.isArray(bundle[\"claims\"]) ? bundle[\"claims\"] : [];\n  for (const claim of claims) {\n    if (!isRecord(claim)) continue;\n    const proposition = isRecord(claim[\"proposition\"]) ? claim[\"proposition\"] : {};\n    if (proposition[\"predicate\"] !== predicate) continue;\n    const value = proposition[\"object\"];\n    if (typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\") return value.trim();\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n\nfunction wireStory(path: string) {\n  const value = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, \"utf8\")) as unknown;\n  if (!isRecord(value)) throw new TypeError(`wire bundle is not an object: ${path}`);\n  const title = claimValue(value, \"title\");\n  const url = claimValue(value, \"url\");\n  if (title === undefined || url === undefined) throw new TypeError(`wire bundle lacks title or URL: ${path}`);\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"producing-content.syndication-item\" as const,\n    id: typeof value[\"subject\"] === \"string\" ? value[\"subject\"] : basename(path, \".json\"),\n    title,\n    url,\n    ...(claimValue(value, \"summary\") === undefined ? {} : { summary: claimValue(value, \"summary\")! }),\n    ...(claimValue(value, \"published\") === undefined ? {} : { published: claimValue(value, \"published\")! }),\n  });\n}\n\nfunction digest(value: string): string {\n  return `sha256:${createHash(\"sha256\").update(value).digest(\"hex\")}`;\n}\n\nfunction availablePath(contentDir: string, requestedSlug: string): Readonly<{ slug: string; path: string }> {\n  let ordinal = 1;\n  let slug = requestedSlug;\n  let path = join(contentDir, `${slug}.md`);\n  while (existsSync(path)) {\n    ordinal += 1;\n    slug = `${requestedSlug}-revision-${ordinal}`;\n    path = join(contentDir, `${slug}.md`);\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({ slug, path });\n}\n\nfunction replayLogPath(dataDir: string, day: string): string {\n  return join(dataDir, \"records\", \"writer-replays\", day.slice(0, 4), day.slice(5, 7), day.slice(8, 10), `${day}-koios-news-writer-replays.jsonl`);\n}\n\nconst day = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);\nconst property = koiosFarmProperty();\nconst subject = wireStory(SUBJECT_STORE);\nconst context = wireStory(CONTEXT_STORE);\nconst priorPath = resolve(property.contentDir, `${PRIOR_SLUG}.md`);\nconst priorBytes = readFileSync(priorPath, \"utf8\");\n\nconst result = await cultivate(property, {\n  at: day,\n  term: TERM,\n  beat: BEAT,\n  items: [subject, context],\n  // Freeze the reporting packet and its roles: this operation is explicitly a\n  // Writer replay, not a second assignment or source-selection decision.\n  select: () => Object.freeze({ story: subject, context: Object.freeze([context]) }),\n  dryRun: true,\n});\n\nif (\"obstruction\" in result) throw new Error(`writer replay obstructed: ${result.evidence}`);\nconst cultivation = result as CultivationResult;\nconst requestedSlug = String(cultivation.document.frontMatter[\"slug\"] ?? cultivation.slug);\nconst destination = availablePath(resolve(property.contentDir), requestedSlug);\nconst document = decodeFrontMatterDocument(encodeFrontMatterDocument(cultivation.document));\nconst nextDocument = Object.freeze({\n  ...document,\n  frontMatter: Object.freeze({\n    ...document.frontMatter,\n    slug: destination.slug,\n    draft: true,\n    koios: Object.freeze({\n      ...(isRecord(document.frontMatter[\"koios\"]) ? document.frontMatter[\"koios\"] : {}),\n      replayed_from: PRIOR_SLUG,\n      replay_layer: \"writer\",\n    }),\n  }),\n});\n\nmkdirSync(dirname(destination.path), { recursive: true });\nwriteFileSync(destination.path, encodeFrontMatterDocument(nextDocument), \"utf8\");\nconst references = normalizeCandidateReferences(destination.path);\nconst internalLinks = enrichCandidateDraftInternalLinkGuidance(destination.path);\nconst finalBytes = readFileSync(destination.path, \"utf8\");\n\nconst record = Object.freeze({\n  kind: \"koios.writer-replay\",\n  at: new Date().toISOString(),\n  replayFrom: \"writer\",\n  assignment: Object.freeze({ term: TERM, beat: BEAT }),\n  sources: Object.freeze([\n    Object.freeze({ role: \"subject\", title: subject.title, url: subject.url }),\n    Object.freeze({ role: \"supporting-context\", title: context.title, url: context.url }),\n  ]),\n  prior: Object.freeze({ slug: PRIOR_SLUG, path: priorPath, digest: digest(priorBytes) }),\n  result: Object.freeze({\n    slug: destination.slug,\n    path: destination.path,\n    digest: digest(finalBytes),\n    shippedByMechanicalGates: cultivation.shipped,\n    gates: cultivation.gates,\n    usage: cultivation.usage,\n    inference: Object.freeze({ provider: property.provider ?? \"nim\", model: property.model ?? \"provider-default\" }),\n    composition: cultivation.composition,\n    references,\n    internalLinks,\n  }),\n});\nconst logPath = replayLogPath(resolve(property.dataDir), day);\nmkdirSync(dirname(logPath), { recursive: true });\nappendFileSync(logPath, `${JSON.stringify(record)}\\n`, \"utf8\");\n\nprocess.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(record, null, 2)}\\n`);\n"},{"sourceId":"runtime/src/newsroom/departments.mts","digest":"sha256:3bc17f1e61592b3759150160a274078ccf5cb272c11f0656dcd99addfd6b487d","content":"// newsroom/departments â€” the whole Koios.News production line as one reading.\n\nimport type { KoiosPost } from \"../content.mjs\";\nimport type { SearchConsoleQuery } from \"../demand.mjs\";\nimport { administratingOfficeReading, type AdministratingOfficeReading } from \"../administrating/index.mjs\";\nimport { researchDepartmentReading, type ResearchDepartmentReading } from \"../researching/index.mjs\";\nimport { writingDepartmentReading, type WritingDepartmentReading } from \"../researching/writing/index.mjs\";\nimport { editingDepartmentReading, type EditingDepartmentReading } from \"../researching/writing/editing/index.mjs\";\nimport { publishingDepartmentReading, type PublishingDepartmentReading } from \"../researching/writing/editing/publishing/index.mjs\";\nimport { koiosItems, type KoiosItem, type KoiosMasthead } from \"../surface.mjs\";\nimport { desksReading, type DesksReading } from \"../researching/sourcing/desks.mjs\";\nimport type { ViewCounts } from \"../views.mjs\";\n\nexport type KoiosNewsroomDepartments = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"koios.newsroom.departments\";\n  // The two orthogonal axes of the newsroom: desks are topical standing (what\n  // we cover), departments are the production line (how a desk's stories are\n  // made). Every story crosses every department and belongs to one desk.\n  desks: DesksReading;\n  administrating: AdministratingOfficeReading;\n  researching: ResearchDepartmentReading & Readonly<{\n    writing: WritingDepartmentReading & Readonly<{\n      editing: EditingDepartmentReading & Readonly<{\n        publishing: PublishingDepartmentReading;\n      }>;\n    }>;\n  }>;\n}>;\n\nexport function newsroomDepartmentsReading(input: Readonly<{\n  posts: readonly KoiosPost[];\n  masthead: KoiosMasthead;\n  views: ViewCounts;\n  dataDir: string;\n  adClient?: string;\n  searchConsole?: readonly SearchConsoleQuery[];\n}>): KoiosNewsroomDepartments {\n  const items: readonly KoiosItem[] = koiosItems(input.posts, input.masthead);\n  const researching = researchDepartmentReading({\n    posts: input.posts,\n    views: input.views,\n    dataDir: input.dataDir,\n    ...(input.searchConsole === undefined ? {} : { searchConsole: input.searchConsole }),\n  });\n  const writing = writingDepartmentReading(input.posts);\n  const editing = editingDepartmentReading(input.posts);\n  const publishing = publishingDepartmentReading({\n    masthead: input.masthead,\n    items,\n    posts: input.posts,\n    views: input.views,\n    dataDir: input.dataDir,\n    ...(input.adClient === undefined ? {} : { adClient: input.adClient }),\n  });\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"koios.newsroom.departments\",\n    desks: desksReading({ articles: items.map((item) => ({ section: item.section, published: item.published, isPublic: item.isPublic })) }),\n    administrating: administratingOfficeReading({ adSenseEnabled: input.adClient !== undefined }),\n    researching: Object.freeze({\n      ...researching,\n      writing: Object.freeze({\n        ...writing,\n        editing: Object.freeze({\n          ...editing,\n          publishing,\n        }),\n      }),\n    }),\n  });\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"prior-art/agentic-newsroom","digest":"sha256:f2efbcf0cf58ca651b4be4dc11899fef3ce7a95a003a10309126673bb0c99537","content":"---\ntitle: Accountable Agentic Newsrooms Research Agenda\ndescription: A research agenda for warrant-preserving editorial infrastructure, where evidence, attribution, authority, judgment, genre, legal risk, correction, and accountability remain distinguishable as claims move through humans, agents, tools, prose, metadata, feeds, and downstream summaries. Frames the problem as controlled delegation rather than autonomous publication, names fifteen research questions (editorial judgment as gates not functions, authority graphs, source-power modeling, modality preservation, headline pragmatics, correction propagation, settlement of contested evidence, demand-not-evidence, genre contracts, omission ledgers, adversarial threat models), and proposes technical artifacts and a prioritized roadmap.\ndate-created: 2026-06-19\ndate-modified: 2026-06-19\ntags: [agentic-newsroom, editorial-judgment, gate-algebra, authority-graph, source-power, modality-preservation, headline-pragmatics, correction-propagation, settlement, demand-not-evidence, genre-contract, omission-ledger, adversarial-editorial, controlled-delegation]\n---\n\n# Accountable Agentic Newsrooms: A Research Agenda for Warrant-Preserving Editorial Infrastructure\n\n## Source note\n\nThis research agenda was prepared from three early memos supplied by the user:\n\n- `news_topology_research_memo.md`\n- `fiber-algebra-editorial-production.md`\n- `claims_to_prose_research_report.md`\n\nIt also draws on journalism studies, editorial standards, computational journalism, provenance systems, structured news standards, discourse pragmatics, fact-checking research, and AI/agentic-system design. Where source URLs are included, they should be checked again for final publication use.\n\n---\n\n## Executive thesis\n\nThe deeper research problem is not â€œhow do we automate news writing?â€ It is: **how do we design newsroom systems in which evidence, attribution, authority, judgment, genre, legal risk, correction, and public accountability remain distinguishable as claims move through humans, agents, tools, prose, metadata, feeds, platforms, archives, and downstream summaries?**\n\nThe three early memos point toward a serious formal program, but only if the formalism stays modest. The strongest version is not a grand mathematical theory of â€œnewsâ€ as such. It is a **warrant-preserving editorial infrastructure**: a system in which attributed, time-indexed claims with provenance and status are transformed through context-bound editorial operations, then realized into genre-specific artifacts under explicit gates.\n\nThe â€œnews topologyâ€ memo is right to treat the attributed, time-indexed claim with provenance and editorial status as more stable than â€œarticleâ€ or â€œevent,â€ while warning that topology cannot explain gatekeeping, framing, source power, platform incentives, or reader interpretation by itself.\n\nThe â€œfiber algebraâ€ memo is strongest where it separates **context movement**, **editorial transformation**, and **artifact projection**. A jurisdictional restriction, legal review, copy edit, publication, correction, and feed serialization are different kinds of operation and should not share a single generic â€œtransformâ€ type.\n\nThe â€œclaims-to-proseâ€ memo is strongest where it rejects `claims â†’ prose` as a function and instead treats prose as a **licensed realization relation** constrained by attribution, modality, evidentiality, contradiction, correction state, and stance.\n\nThe research frontier is therefore not autonomous publication. It is **controlled delegation**: deciding which parts of editorial work can be represented, checked, routed, audited, and assisted by software, and which must remain governed by reporters, editors, standards desks, lawyers, affected communities, and accountable institutions.\n\nExisting journalism ethics already require verification, context, correction, and source transparency. SPJ, for example, says journalists should verify information before release, use original sources where possible, avoid oversimplification, and gather, update, and correct information throughout a storyâ€™s life. APâ€™s generative-AI guidance likewise treats AI output as unvetted source material that still requires editorial judgment and sourcing standards.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- SPJ Code of Ethics: <https://www.spj.org/spj-code-of-ethics/>\n- AP generative AI standards: <https://www.ap.org/the-definitive-source/behind-the-news/standards-around-generative-ai/>\n\n---\n\n## What the current theory already gets right\n\nFirst, it correctly chooses the **claim/provenance graph** rather than the finished article as the formal center. That is crucial because articles, headlines, schema markup, liveblog entries, newsletters, social cards, feeds, and AI summaries are all projections from a richer editorial state.\n\nSecond, it understands that **provenance is necessary but not sufficient**. W3C PROV-O can model entities, activities, agents, derivations, and responsibility, but it does not decide whether a claim is true, fair, contextualized, or legally safe. C2PA/Content Credentials can help establish origin and edit history for media, but provenance and authenticity are not the same as journalistic warrant.\n\nThird, it treats **correction as a system event**, not merely a notice at the bottom of an article. A correction may need to invalidate article text, headline, captions, social cards, feed entries, JSON-LD, syndication packages, search snippets, archive records, downstream summaries, and internal claim dependencies.\n\nFourth, it distinguishes **demand from evidence**. Search queries, social trends, reader questions, and analytics can justify an assignment, but they cannot support the factual proposition being assigned. Googleâ€™s structured-data and spam guidance is useful here: Article structured data helps search systems understand pages, while spam policies target manipulative scaled content and deceptive search practices; neither layer is evidence.\n\nFifth, it sees that **agent capability is not editorial authority**. MCP and A2A-type systems may expose tools, resources, tasks, and inter-agent communication, but they do not supply newsroom warrant, legal permission, source judgment, or publication authority. MCPâ€™s tool specification recommends human-in-the-loop visibility and confirmation for tool invocations, which is a safety affordance, not an editorial decision procedure.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- W3C PROV-O: <https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/>\n- C2PA: <https://c2pa.org/>\n- Google article structured data: <https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/article>\n- MCP tools specification: <https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools>\n\n---\n\n## What the current theory under-theorizes\n\nThe current theory still under-theorizes **editorial power**. Gatekeeping is not just a sequence of validations; it is how institutions select, suppress, amplify, frame, and routinize public reality. Gatekeeping theory treats gates as decision points about selection, treatment, prominence, and exclusion, not merely technical pass/fail checks.\n\nIt also under-theorizes **source power**. A provenance graph may show that a statement came from a mayor, police department, company, community organizer, database, sensor, leaked memo, or LLM tool. It does not by itself model access journalism, intimidation, official-source dependency, anonymity, retaliation risk, community knowledge, or the long history of whose testimony becomes â€œcredible.â€\n\nIt under-theorizes **framing and omission**. Entmanâ€™s classic framing account defines framing as selecting some aspects of perceived reality and making them more salient in ways that promote problem definition, causal interpretation, moral evaluation, and treatment recommendation. That cannot be reduced to claim support edges.\n\nIt under-theorizes **headlines and distribution artifacts**. Headlines are not summaries with fewer words. They assert, imply, presuppose, invite clicks, shape memory, and travel separately from body text. Research on misleading headlines shows that headline/body incongruity is a distinct computational problem, and psychological work shows subtle headline misinformation can affect memory and inference.\n\nIt under-theorizes **legal risk**. A generated sentence that preserves source attribution may still defame, invade privacy, breach an embargo, identify a protected source, violate a court order, or create negligent republication risk. In U.S. defamation law, â€œactual maliceâ€ for public officials and public figures means knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard for truth, not personal ill will; that makes audit trails and ignored warnings legally material.\n\nIt under-theorizes **labor and accountability capture**. If every editorial act becomes a record, the system may improve accountability, but it may also intensify surveillance, deskill editors, hide human labor behind automation metrics, or produce bureaucratic compliance theater.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- Gatekeeping theory overview: <https://www.bmf.bt/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Gatekeeping.pdf>\n- Entman, â€œFraming: Toward Clarification of a Fractured Paradigmâ€: <https://academic.oup.com/joc/article/43/4/51/4160153>\n- RCFP pre-publication review guide: <https://www.rcfp.org/resources/pre-publication-review-guide/>\n\n---\n\n## Major future research questions\n\n### 1. What is editorial judgment, and which parts can be formalized without pretending judgment is mechanical?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Editors make judgments about salience, proportionality, fairness, timing, placement, harm, public interest, and voice. These are not reducible to â€œclaim supported / unsupported.â€\n\n**Why it matters technically:** The system needs to separate checkable constraints from noncanonical human decisions. A gate can block an unsupported claim; it cannot decide the only legitimate lede.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Gatekeeping theory, news values, objectivity studies, standards desks, copy desks, legal review, and breaking-news workflows. News values research asks what makes something newsworthy but also warns that news values are culturally and institutionally contingent.\n\n**Likely artifact:** An **editorial gate algebra** with typed gates: support, attribution, legal, harm, rights, source-risk, standards-validity, and human-judgment-required. It should include override records that do not convert uncertainty into truth.\n\n**If ignored:** The system will either automate judgment illegitimately or bury real judgment inside opaque scoring functions.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What is editorial authority in a newsroom with agents, tools, APIs, and humans?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** A tool may retrieve a document; an agent may draft a sentence; a reporter may verify; an editor may approve; a lawyer may block; a standards editor may require correction. These roles are not interchangeable.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Agent systems need explicit authority scopes: may observe, may draft, may classify, may recommend, may edit, may publish, may override, may retract.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** AP and Reuters AI policies, newsroom roles, CMS permission systems, audit-log design, AI governance frameworks such as NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001. NISTâ€™s AI RMF emphasizes validity, reliability, safety, security, accountability, transparency, explainability, privacy, and fairness as context-dependent socio-technical attributes.\n\n**Likely artifact:** An **authority graph** linking people, agents, tools, claims, artifacts, gates, and actions.\n\n**If ignored:** Technical capability will be mistaken for institutional authority, enabling agents to perform editorial acts no accountable person authorized.\n\n**Key source:** NIST AI RMF: <https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/nist.ai.100-1.pdf>\n\n---\n\n### 3. How should source power, source dependence, and source diversity be represented?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** A story sourced only to police, prosecutors, companies, campaigns, or platform PR may be well-provenanced but still structurally distorted.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Provenance graphs need source-role, affiliation, authority-scope, independence, access-condition, anonymity, conflict, and vulnerability fields.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Herbert Gansâ€™s newsroom sociology, source-dependence studies, computational source-mixture analysis, anonymous-source standards, community-centered journalism, and Indigenous data sovereignty frameworks. Recent computational journalism work has modeled articles as mixtures of source types, which is a useful starting point but not a substitute for editorial interpretation.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **source authority and dependency model** that audits source concentration by beat, topic, institution, geography, and affected community.\n\n**If ignored:** Agentic systems will reproduce official-source bias at machine speed while presenting it as neutral provenance.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How can modality, evidentiality, uncertainty, and attribution survive every prose transformation?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** â€œPolice said,â€ â€œcourt records show,â€ â€œresearchers estimate,â€ â€œthe company claims,â€ â€œmay,â€ â€œlikely,â€ â€œunconfirmed,â€ and â€œdisputedâ€ carry different editorial commitments.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Claims need modality and attribution types; sentence plans need assertion-strength constraints; generated text needs post-hoc extraction and comparison.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** NLG, attribution-aware generation, evidentiality, pragmatics, fact-checking, and editorial copy rules. AIS defines an attribution framework in which NLG output about the external world should be verifiable against identified sources. FEVER and AVeriTeC provide useful but incomplete verification labels, including supported/refuted/not-enough-info and conflicting-evidence/cherry-picking categories.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **modality-preserving sentence contract** and **claim-to-sentence alignment benchmark**.\n\n**If ignored:** Allegations will become facts, estimates will become numbers, uncertainty will disappear, and summaries will overclaim.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- AIS: <https://aclanthology.org/2023.cl-4.2/>\n- FEVER: <https://fever.ai/dataset/fever.html>\n- AVeriTeC: <https://fever.ai/dataset/averitec.html>\n\n---\n\n### 5. What does a headline assert, imply, presuppose, and cause readers to remember?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** A body story can be careful while the headline misleads. Headlines also travel into push alerts, social cards, search results, newsletters, and AI answers.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Headline checking must compare not only literal entailment but also attribution loss, modality strengthening, presupposition, causal overclaim, quantifier broadening, and sensational framing.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Headline pragmatics, clickbait/misleading-headline detection, cognitive studies of misinformation, AP headline practice, SEO and platform distribution.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **headline pragmatics checker** that tests headline/body/claim-graph consistency and flags unsupported implication.\n\n**If ignored:** The system will pass factuality checks while laundering misinformation through the most visible artifact.\n\n---\n\n### 6. How should corrections propagate across the whole publication and distribution graph?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** A correction is a promise to repair the public record, not just a ritual note.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Correction must be dependency-aware: claim â†’ sentence â†’ headline â†’ caption â†’ media metadata â†’ JSON-LD â†’ NewsML/ninjs â†’ RSS/Atom/JSON Feed â†’ social card â†’ partner syndication â†’ archive â†’ downstream AI summary.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Corrections desks, wire-service update practices, feed standards, PROV-O, truth-maintenance systems, belief revision, Google structured-data guidance, and Trust Project transparency indicators. Trust Project principles include prompt corrections/clarifications and disclosure of methods and ownership.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **correction propagation graph** with regression tests that fail if a corrected claim persists in any public or machine-readable surface.\n\n**If ignored:** Errors will survive in snippets, summaries, partner copies, and training/evaluation corpora after the newsroom thinks it has corrected them.\n\n**Key source:** Trust Project: <https://thetrustproject.org/about/>\n\n---\n\n### 7. What is the difference between provenance, authenticity, credibility, warrant, and truth?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** â€œThis image is authentic,â€ â€œthis source really said it,â€ â€œthis source is credible,â€ â€œthis evidence warrants publication,â€ and â€œthis claim is trueâ€ are different.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** The system needs separate layers: cryptographic provenance, source identity, chain of custody, credibility notes, evidentiary support, editorial warrant, and truth-status.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** PROV-O, C2PA, digital forensics, fact-checking methodology, legal evidence, records management, authenticity studies. C2PA is important but should be treated carefully; recent C2PA security critiques are emerging preprints and should be verified before being treated as settled.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **provenance/warrant ontology** that prevents â€œsigned,â€ â€œverified origin,â€ â€œcredible,â€ and â€œtrueâ€ from collapsing into one field.\n\n**If ignored:** A cryptographically signed falsehood, staged image, or official lie will be treated as epistemically safe.\n\n---\n\n### 8. How does contested evidence become publishable without false balance or false settlement?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Journalism often publishes before full settlement: breaking news, scientific uncertainty, legal disputes, elections, war, disasters, allegations, and investigations.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Claim graphs need support, refutation, undercutting, denial, scope, and temporal status. Prose needs settlement forms such as â€œofficials said,â€ â€œrecords show,â€ â€œaccounts differ,â€ â€œevidence reviewed by X supports,â€ or â€œthe available evidence does not establish.â€\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Argumentation theory, paraconsistent logic, fact-check verdicts, court reporting, science journalism, conflict reporting, and standards on allegations.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **settlement model** with states such as unsupported, attributed-only, supported, refuted, contested, under investigation, corrected, retracted, and legally blocked.\n\n**If ignored:** Systems will either publish false consensus or perform false balance between well-supported and bad-faith claims.\n\n---\n\n### 9. How can reader demand create assignments without becoming evidence?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Audience questions can reveal public information needs. Search trends can reveal confusion. But demand cannot prove the answer.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Demand signals should create tasks: investigate, explain, update, localize, debunk, monitor. They should not create support edges.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Agenda-setting, audience analytics, SEO, Google Search policies, public-interest journalism, service journalism, platformization. Agenda-setting research shows that news placement and prominence shape what audiences perceive as important, which means demand loops must be treated as power loops, not neutral signals.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **demand-to-assignment model** with public-interest, evidence-availability, anti-spam, and harm gates.\n\n**If ignored:** The system becomes a content farm: queries generate articles, articles generate more queries, and evidence becomes optional.\n\n---\n\n### 10. What are genre contracts for every editorial surface?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Wire briefs, explainers, investigations, liveblogs, analysis, editorials, corrections, obituaries, fact checks, newsletters, topic pages, structured metadata, and social cards make different promises to readers.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** A genre contract should specify allowed stance, attribution density, uncertainty handling, update cadence, headline constraints, evidence thresholds, correction behavior, and metadata obligations.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Genre theory, discourse planning, NLG document planning, IPTC NewsML-G2/ninjs, schema.org `NewsArticle` and `ClaimReview`, liveblog practice, wire-service standards. IPTC NewsML-G2 is designed to exchange individual items and packages of text, images, video, audio news, event, and sports data; schema.org `NewsArticle` and `ClaimReview` are public web metadata types, not internal editorial logic.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **genre contract schema** and validation suite.\n\n**If ignored:** Opinion will leak into straight news, analysis will be flattened into fact, and metadata will misrepresent the articleâ€™s actual editorial status.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- IPTC NewsML-G2: <https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/>\n- schema.org NewsArticle: <https://schema.org/NewsArticle>\n- schema.org ClaimReview: <https://schema.org/ClaimReview>\n\n---\n\n### 11. How should omission be represented as an editorial act?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Omission is not always failure; it can protect sources, avoid harm, respect privacy, maintain focus, or fit space. But omission can also hide exculpatory evidence, erase affected communities, or distort proportionality.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Systems need a way to record salient known-but-omitted material without making every omission public.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Framing, agenda-setting, source diversity, harm minimization, legal redaction, public-record withholding, trauma-informed reporting.\n\n**Likely artifact:** An **omission ledger** with private/public layers: omitted claim, reason, risk, affected interpretation, reviewer, and whether omission must be disclosed.\n\n**If ignored:** The system will audit only what was said, not what was systematically left unsaid.\n\n---\n\n### 12. How should adversarial manipulation be modeled in agentic editorial systems?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Sources, campaigns, PR firms, state actors, platform manipulators, malicious tool providers, and even analytics dashboards can shape what agents see and do.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Agents need threat models for prompt injection, tool poisoning, source spoofing, forged provenance, SEO spam, document poisoning, social-signal manipulation, and correction suppression.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Information disorder, adversarial ML, MCP security, newsroom verification, platform manipulation, OSINT verification. MCP-related security papers from 2026 report tool poisoning, prompt injection, and description-code mismatch risks; because much of this literature is new, it should be treated as active research needing replication and operational validation.\n\n**Likely artifact:** An **editorial adversary model** and red-team corpus for agentic newsroom workflows.\n\n**If ignored:** Agents will become scalable attack surfaces for publishing, syndication, and correction infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n### 13. How much editorial process should be exposed to readers?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Transparency can build trust, but too much disclosure can expose sources, legal strategy, trauma details, or internal deliberation that readers cannot interpret.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Public provenance needs redaction, summarization, role labeling, confidence language, and UX testing. It should distinguish â€œhow we know,â€ â€œwhat we do not know,â€ â€œwhat changed,â€ and â€œwho is accountable.â€\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Trust Project indicators, fact-checker methodology pages, audience research, source protection, privacy, legal risk, explainable AI. IFCN principles emphasize transparency of methodology, including how fact checks are selected, researched, written, edited, published, and corrected.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **public provenance UX model** with reader-facing layers and protected internal layers.\n\n**If ignored:** The newsroom may create transparency theater, expose vulnerable sources, or overwhelm readers with unusable audit data.\n\n**Key sources:**\n\n- IFCN Code of Principles: <https://ifcncodeofprinciples.poynter.org/the-commitments>\n- Trust Project: <https://thetrustproject.org/about/>\n\n---\n\n### 14. What legal and harm gates are required before publication automation is even considered?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** Editors and lawyers care about defamation, privacy, source protection, court orders, copyright, minors, victims, security-sensitive information, embargoes, and foreseeable harm.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** Legal gates must be jurisdiction-specific, claim-specific, source-specific, and artifact-specific. They should route to humans; they should not â€œauto-clearâ€ risky material.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** Media law, pre-publication review, rights management, privacy law, copyright, shield laws, newsroom legal escalation, safety practices.\n\n**Likely artifact:** A **legal/harm risk gate** with blockers, warnings, escalation roles, and immutable review history.\n\n**If ignored:** An autonomous system may publish defamatory, privacy-invasive, or dangerous claims faster than humans can detect.\n\n---\n\n### 15. What benchmark would show that an agentic newsroom system preserves attribution, uncertainty, correction state, and accountability?\n\n**Why it matters editorially:** A serious benchmark must reflect real newsroom failure modes, not just factoid QA.\n\n**Why it matters technically:** The benchmark should include source records, conflicting evidence, protected sources, genre targets, correction events, headlines, metadata, feeds, syndication, and downstream summaries.\n\n**Literatures and practices to study:** AIS, FEVER, AVeriTeC, factual consistency metrics such as QAGS and SummaC, newsroom test desks, standards validation, legal hypotheticals. Current benchmarks are useful but insufficient because they rarely test correction propagation, headline pragmatics, stance boundaries, protected-source handling, or multi-surface publication.\n\n**Likely artifact:** An **Accountable Newsroom Agent Benchmark** with end-to-end tasks: ingest â†’ claim graph â†’ draft â†’ headline â†’ metadata â†’ correction â†’ regenerated downstream artifacts.\n\n**If ignored:** Vendors will optimize for fluent article generation and shallow citation accuracy while failing the actual newsroom accountability problem.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed next papers\n\n### 1. â€œEditorial Judgment Is Not a Function: Gates, Overrides, and Human Authority in Agentic Newsroomsâ€\n\n**Summary:** Argues that editorial judgment should be modeled as a combination of checkable gates, noncanonical human decisions, and accountable override records.\n\n**Key research questions:** Which editorial decisions are formalizable? Which are reviewable but not computable? What constitutes a valid override?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Gatekeeping, news values, objectivity studies, standards desks, AI governance.\n\n**Technical implications:** Gate algebra, override schema, human-governed publication state.\n\n---\n\n### 2. â€œFrom Provenance to Warrant: Why Authenticity Infrastructure Is Not Enough for Journalismâ€\n\n**Summary:** Separates provenance, authenticity, source credibility, evidentiary support, editorial warrant, and truth.\n\n**Key research questions:** What does C2PA prove? What does PROV-O represent? What must a newsroom add?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Provenance systems, media forensics, fact-checking, epistemology, legal evidence.\n\n**Technical implications:** Provenance/warrant ontology and validation profiles.\n\n---\n\n### 3. â€œHeadline Pragmatics for Computational Journalismâ€\n\n**Summary:** Treats headlines as high-risk speech acts with assertion, implication, presupposition, and distribution effects.\n\n**Key research questions:** When is a headline licensed by a claim graph? How do headlines preserve attribution and uncertainty?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Pragmatics, discourse analysis, cognitive misinformation studies, headline/body incongruity detection.\n\n**Technical implications:** Headline checker, headline benchmark, headline/body/claim alignment dataset.\n\n---\n\n### 4. â€œCorrection as Dependency Invalidation: A Truth-Maintenance Model for Newsroomsâ€\n\n**Summary:** Models corrections as graph updates that invalidate dependent claims, artifacts, feeds, structured data, and downstream summaries.\n\n**Key research questions:** Which dependencies must be tracked? How should corrections propagate through syndication and archives?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Truth maintenance, belief revision, corrections practice, feeds, PROV-O, CMS architecture.\n\n**Technical implications:** Correction propagation graph and regression tests.\n\n---\n\n### 5. â€œThe Omission Ledger: Representing What Newsroom Systems Know but Do Not Publishâ€\n\n**Summary:** Develops a private/public model of omission as an accountable editorial act.\n\n**Key research questions:** Which omissions require internal rationale? Which require public disclosure? Which must remain protected?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Framing, source protection, legal redaction, harm minimization, public-interest ethics.\n\n**Technical implications:** Omission ledger, salience audits, protected-source redaction policies.\n\n---\n\n### 6. â€œDemand Is Not Evidence: Assignment Generation Under Search and Social Pressureâ€\n\n**Summary:** Formalizes how search, social, and audience signals can create assignments without creating factual support.\n\n**Key research questions:** How can query clusters generate reporting tasks? How should SEO pressure be bounded by public-interest gates?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Agenda-setting, platform studies, SEO, audience analytics, service journalism.\n\n**Technical implications:** Demand-to-assignment model, anti-content-farm gate.\n\n---\n\n### 7. â€œSource Power Graphs: Auditing Official-Source Dependence in Agentic Reportingâ€\n\n**Summary:** Builds a model for source roles, institutional dependency, independence, anonymity, and affected-community representation.\n\n**Key research questions:** How do automated systems reproduce source hierarchies? What is fair sourcing across beats?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Newsroom sociology, source studies, computational source modeling, community journalism.\n\n**Technical implications:** Source-dependency dashboard and source-role ontology.\n\n---\n\n### 8. â€œGenre Contracts for Machine-Readable Newsâ€\n\n**Summary:** Defines formal contracts for wire briefs, explainers, analysis, opinion, liveblogs, corrections, social cards, feeds, and metadata.\n\n**Key research questions:** What commitments does each genre make? What transformations are forbidden?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Genre theory, discourse planning, NLG, IPTC, schema.org, news standards.\n\n**Technical implications:** Genre contract schema and validation engine.\n\n---\n\n### 9. â€œAdversarial Editorial Infrastructure: Threat Models for Agentic Newsroomsâ€\n\n**Summary:** Treats agentic newsroom systems as adversarial environments where sources, tools, prompts, documents, and platforms can be manipulated.\n\n**Key research questions:** What are the newsroom-specific equivalents of prompt injection, tool poisoning, source spoofing, and provenance forgery?\n\n**Primary literatures:** Information disorder, OSINT verification, adversarial ML, MCP security, platform manipulation.\n\n**Technical implications:** Red-team suite, adversary taxonomy, tool/source trust gates.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed technical artifacts\n\n### Authority graph\n\nSolves role confusion among reporters, editors, agents, tools, lawyers, sources, and publishers. It must not decide who has moral legitimacy or final editorial authority automatically.\n\n### Source authority model\n\nTracks source identity, role, affiliation, authority scope, independence, anonymity, risk, and reliability history. It must not reduce credibility to a single universal score.\n\n### Settlement model\n\nConverts contested evidence into publishable forms such as attributed dispute, unresolved claim, supported narrow claim, or retraction. It must not settle politically or legally contested claims by optimization.\n\n### Headline pragmatics checker\n\nTests whether a headline overstates, presupposes, causalizes, sensationalizes, or strips attribution from the body and claim graph. It must not write the final headline unaided.\n\n### Omission ledger\n\nRecords important known material excluded from publication and why. It must not force disclosure of protected sources or sensitive legal deliberation.\n\n### Genre contract schema\n\nEncodes obligations for wire, explainer, analysis, editorial, correction, liveblog, social card, feed, and structured data. It must not decide newsworthiness.\n\n### Correction propagation graph\n\nTracks dependencies from claim to all public and machine-readable artifacts. It must not silently rewrite the public record without notice.\n\n### Demand-to-assignment model\n\nConverts queries and social signals into reporting tasks under public-interest and anti-spam gates. It must not treat popularity as evidence.\n\n### Agent/tool/source role taxonomy\n\nDistinguishes observation, assertion, retrieval, inference, drafting, editing, approval, publication, and audit roles. It must not treat tool output as a source unless the tool actually observed or retrieved something.\n\n### Editorial gate algebra\n\nComposes support, attribution, legal, harm, rights, correction, standards, and publication-readiness gates. It must not collapse all gates into one â€œsafeâ€ score.\n\n### Claim-to-sentence alignment benchmark\n\nTests whether generated prose preserves support, attribution, modality, scope, contradiction, and correction state. It must not rely only on generic NLI.\n\n### Public provenance UX model\n\nShows readers enough about sourcing, uncertainty, correction, and AI assistance to support accountability. It must not expose private source identities, raw notes, or legally privileged reasoning.\n\n### Adversarial newsroom red-team suite\n\nTests prompt injection, tool poisoning, forged documents, manipulated analytics, fake sources, and stale corrections. It must not become a checklist substitute for security review.\n\n---\n\n## Risks and critiques\n\nThe first risk is **overformalization**. Category theory, topology, fibers, and algebra are useful where they reveal invariants: restriction, projection, dependency, correction, provenance, compatibility, and gate composition. They become harmful when they make framing, judgment, source credibility, or public interest look more canonical than they are.\n\nThe second risk is **proceduralism masquerading as truth**. A system can have perfect provenance, valid JSON-LD, a C2PA manifest, a passed SHACL shape, and a signed audit log, while still publishing a misleading or unjust story. Standards validity is not editorial validity; a schema.org-valid `NewsArticle` can contain a false claim.\n\nThe third risk is **reproducing newsroom power**. If the system is trained on existing newsroom routines without critique, it will encode official-source dependence, police/prosecutor framing, metropolitan bias, English-language dominance, and historic exclusions as â€œnormal workflow.â€\n\nThe fourth risk is **labor erasure**. Agentic systems may make editorial labor look like a sequence of machine operations while hiding the judgment, emotional labor, community trust, source cultivation, legal negotiation, and standards work that make journalism accountable.\n\nThe fifth risk is **accountability capture**. More records can improve oversight, but they can also become surveillance of reporters, bureaucratic compliance theater, or internal tools for management discipline rather than public accountability.\n\nThe sixth risk is **content-farm capture**. The same infrastructure that preserves attribution and corrections could be used to mass-produce SEO-shaped, minimally reported, syntactically well-provenanced articles. Googleâ€™s scaled-content and spam policies are a warning that large-scale publication can be manipulative even when individual pages look formally structured.\n\nThe seventh risk is **autonomy creep**. A drafting assistant becomes an assignment agent; an assignment agent becomes a publishing recommender; a recommender becomes an autopublisher. The distinction between editorial support software and autonomous publication must remain explicit.\n\n---\n\n## Prioritized roadmap\n\n### Study first\n\nStart with real correction workflows, headline workflows, standards desks, legal review, and source handling. These are the places where the theoryâ€™s invariants meet newsroom consequences. Also study failed corrections, misleading headlines, AI-assisted errors, syndication mistakes, and stories where official-source dependence distorted coverage.\n\n### Prototype first\n\nPrototype the **correction propagation graph**, **modality-preserving sentence plan**, **authority graph**, and **genre contract schema** before any autonomous drafting system. These artifacts support accountability even in human-written newsrooms.\n\n### Benchmark first\n\nBuild the **Accountable Newsroom Agent Benchmark** around hard cases: contested evidence, allegations, corrections, headline/body mismatch, source attribution loss, AI/tool provenance, structured-data drift, and feed/syndication updates. Existing AIS, FEVER, AVeriTeC, QAGS, and SummaC-style tasks can be components, but the benchmark must be newsroom-native rather than generic factuality testing.\n\n### Keep human-governed\n\nPublication approval, legal-risk overrides, source anonymity, harm minimization, newsworthiness, framing, final headline approval, correction wording, retraction decisions, and editorial stance must remain human-governed. Agents can surface conflicts, draft options, check constraints, and trace dependencies; they should not own public accountability.\n\n### Answer before publication automation is safe\n\nBefore any publication automation, the system must answer these questions reliably:\n\n- Who authorized this artifact?\n- Which claims does it assert?\n- Which sources support each claim?\n- Which claims are attributed rather than asserted?\n- What uncertainty was preserved?\n- What was omitted and why?\n- What gates passed or failed?\n- What legal or harm risks remain?\n- What downstream artifacts will update if this changes?\n- Who is accountable if it is wrong?\n\nUntil those questions are machine-checkable and institutionally governed, â€œagentic newsroomâ€ should mean **editorial support infrastructure**, not autonomous journalism.\n\n---\n\n## Reference links and starting bibliography\n\n### Journalism ethics, standards, and trust\n\n- SPJ Code of Ethics: <https://www.spj.org/spj-code-of-ethics/>\n- AP generative AI standards: <https://www.ap.org/the-definitive-source/behind-the-news/standards-around-generative-ai/>\n- Reuters Trust Principles: <https://www.thomsonreuters.com/en/about-us/trust-principles.html>\n- IFCN Code of Principles: <https://ifcncodeofprinciples.poynter.org/the-commitments>\n- Trust Project: <https://thetrustproject.org/about/>\n\n### News standards and structured publishing\n\n- IPTC NewsML-G2: <https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/>\n- IPTC ninjs: <https://iptc.org/standards/ninjs/>\n- IPTC Media Topics: <https://iptc.org/standards/media-topics/>\n- schema.org NewsArticle: <https://schema.org/NewsArticle>\n- schema.org ClaimReview: <https://schema.org/ClaimReview>\n- Google Article structured data: <https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/article>\n- Google spam policies: <https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies>\n\n### Provenance, metadata, and web standards\n\n- W3C PROV-O: <https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/>\n- W3C RDF Concepts: <https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/>\n- W3C SHACL: <https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/>\n- C2PA: <https://c2pa.org/>\n- ActivityPub: <https://www.w3.org/TR/activitypub/>\n- ActivityStreams Vocabulary: <https://www.w3.org/TR/activitystreams-vocabulary/>\n- Atom: <https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4287>\n- JSON Feed: <https://www.jsonfeed.org/version/1.1/>\n\n### Computational journalism, fact-checking, and NLG\n\n- AIS, â€œAttributable to Identified Sourcesâ€: <https://aclanthology.org/2023.cl-4.2/>\n- FEVER dataset: <https://fever.ai/dataset/fever.html>\n- AVeriTeC dataset: <https://fever.ai/dataset/averitec.html>\n- QAGS: <https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.450/>\n- SummaC: <https://aclanthology.org/2022.tacl-1.10/>\n- FactCC: <https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.750/>\n- WebNLG: <https://aclanthology.org/W17-3518/>\n- DART: <https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.37/>\n- ToTTo: <https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.89/>\n\n### Media theory and sociology\n\n- Entman, framing: <https://academic.oup.com/joc/article/43/4/51/4160153>\n- McCombs and Shaw, agenda-setting: <https://www.jstor.org/stable/2747787>\n- Harcup and Oâ€™Neill, news values: <https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/1461670X.2016.1150193>\n- Tuchman, objectivity as strategic ritual: <https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/225193>\n\n### AI governance and agent/tool systems\n\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: <https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/nist.ai.100-1.pdf>\n- MCP tools specification: <https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools>\n- OpenAPI Specification: <https://swagger.io/specification/>\n\n### Legal and safety\n\n- RCFP pre-publication review guide: <https://www.rcfp.org/resources/pre-publication-review-guide/>\n\n"},{"sourceId":"prior-art/news-topology","digest":"sha256:47c4df240540facb84d4eaab207223db7395c0bac68705ffbffb13f7f90888f4","content":"---\ntitle: Formal News Topology\ndescription: A skeptical formal account of news as a composite system â€” a site of reportable contexts carrying presheaf/sheaf-like assignments of attributed claim-provenance graphs, with closure operators for entailment, syndication reach, and freshness, categorical morphisms for editorial and syndication acts, bilattice/paraconsistent status for contested evidence, and event-calculus/belief-revision handling of correction. Argues a single topology of \"news\" is false; topology belongs to the claim-provenance layer only, embedded in workflow categories, temporal logic, provenance graphs, validation gates, and media theory. Includes a worked rail-yard incident with claim graph, JSON-LD/ninjs/PROV emissions, and correction propagation.\ndate-created: 2026-06-19\ndate-modified: 2026-06-19\ntags: [news-topology, claim-provenance-graph, sheaf, presheaf, closure-operator, paraconsistent, bilattice, event-calculus, belief-revision, correction, gatekeeping, editorial-morphism, schema-org, ninjs, prov-o]\n---\n\n# Toward a Formal News Topology: A Research Memo\n\n## Abstract\n\nA rigorous â€œnews topologyâ€ is **plausible only in a restricted sense**. The strongest candidate is not a single topology on â€œnews,â€ but a composite formal system: a site of reportable contexts, presheaf/sheaf-like assignments of locally supported claim graphs, closure operators for entailment, publication readiness, reach, and freshness, plus categorical morphisms for editorial and syndication transformations. It is **false** if it claims that all of news production, circulation, and interpretation is topological. Gatekeeping, framing, source power, platform incentives, and reader interpretation require order-theoretic, graph-theoretic, temporal, provenance, and sociological models alongside any topology.\n\nThe primitive should not be â€œarticleâ€ or â€œeventâ€ alone. The most stable primitive for formalization is an **attributed, time-indexed claim with provenance and editorial status**, embedded in a larger system of observations, sources, events, artifacts, feeds, corrections, circulation traces, and reader-demand signals.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem\n\nThe formal problem is to model how a situation in the world becomes a public, machine-readable, mutable news object under semantic-web and agentic-web conditions.\n\nLet a news system contain:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{N}=(E,O,S,C,A,G,P,F,R,D)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(E\\): events or event hypotheses;\n- \\(O\\): observations, documents, sensor records, interviews, official statements, scraped records;\n- \\(S\\): sources, including humans, institutions, instruments, databases, and agents;\n- \\(C\\): claims, assertions, denials, corrections, and retractions;\n- \\(A\\): editorial acts;\n- \\(G\\): gates, such as verification, legal review, rights clearance, publication approval;\n- \\(P\\): published artifacts;\n- \\(F\\): feeds, sitemaps, syndication, ActivityPub-like delivery, and search discovery traces;\n- \\(R\\): reader, platform, search, and social circulation signals;\n- \\(D\\): standards-level descriptions: RDF, JSON-LD, schema.org, IPTC, PROV, C2PA, SHACL, feeds.\n\nAgentic news changes the problem because agents may now be sources, reporters, editors, readers, syndicators, adversarial manipulators, or provenance evaluators. Existing semantic-web machinery can identify entities, express claims, validate shapes, query graphs, and preserve provenance, but it does not by itself define editorial obligation, truth, adversarial robustness, or task-grounded authority. MCP, for example, standardizes tool/resource/prompt exposure for model applications and explicitly treats tool descriptions as untrusted, with user consent required for tool invocation; A2A standardizes discovery and communication among opaque agents by way of Agent Cards, JSON-RPC over HTTP(S), and task exchange. Those protocols are useful for agentic affordances, not sufficient as epistemic guarantees. ([modelcontextprotocol.io](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18))\n\nThe core formal question is therefore:\n\n> Can news be modeled as transformations of attributed claim-provenance structures across reportable contexts, with mathematically meaningful covers, restrictions, gluing, closures, orders, and morphisms?\n\nThe answer is conditionally yes. But the topology belongs to **some layers** of the system, not all of it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Existing standards landscape\n\n### 2.1 News industry standards\n\nIPTC already treats news as structured exchange material rather than merely prose. NewsML-G2 is an IPTC standard for exchanging text, images, video, audio, event data, sports data, and packages with rich metadata; the News Architecture provides the conceptual and processing model behind NewsML-G2 and EventsML-G2. ([iptc.org](https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nninjs, â€œNews in JSON,â€ gives a JSON representation for news exchange, with schema support and versioned evolution; IPTC announced ninjs 3.1 in 2025, including a Digital Source Type property. This makes ninjs suitable as a serialization target for a computational news object, but not as the underlying formal primitive. ([iptc.org](https://iptc.org/standards/ninjs/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nIPTC NewsCodes and Media Topics provide controlled vocabularies. Media Topics is a multilingual taxonomy with more than 1,200 terms, and IPTC NewsCodes are expressed using SKOS, extended by IPTC mechanisms for retired and faceted concepts. These are important for topic fibers, beat classification, and concept lattices. ([iptc.org](https://iptc.org/standards/media-topics/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nrNews annotates news-specific metadata in HTML, while RightsML encodes machine-readable rights expressions. These are artifact-layer and rights-layer standards, not claim semantics. ([iptc.org](https://www.iptc.org/standards/rnews/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 2.2 Web semantic standards\n\nRDF represents information as graphs of subject-predicate-object triples using IRIs, blank nodes, and literals. JSON-LD serializes linked data in JSON. SPARQL queries RDF graphs, OWL supports richer ontology reasoning, SKOS represents concept schemes, SHACL validates RDF graphs against shapes, PROV-O expresses provenance in OWL, and DCAT describes datasets and data catalogs. These standards give a strong representational substrate for news metadata, claim graphs, source graphs, topic vocabularies, and validation gates. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nHowever, RDF/OWL are mostly monotonic and open-world; news correction, contradiction, and retraction are often nonmonotonic and closed-world at editorial gates. SHACL is therefore better suited to â€œis this publishable under this house schema?â€ than OWL is to â€œis this story true?â€\n\n### 2.3 schema.org, search, and discovery\n\nschema.org defines `NewsArticle` as an article reporting news or providing supporting background. It also defines `Claim` and `ClaimReview` for fact-checking contexts, and `CreativeWork` as a general supertype for creative artifacts. ([schema.org](https://schema.org/NewsArticle?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nGoogleâ€™s article structured-data documentation frames structured data as a way to help Search, Google News, and Assistant understand article pages; sitemaps and news sitemaps help crawlers discover pages, dates, and relationships. This means search visibility is a circulation/discovery layer, not a truth layer. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/article?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 2.4 Feeds, HTTP, and syndication\n\nAtom is an IETF-standard syndication format; JSON Feed is a pragmatic JSON syndication format. WebSub defines publisher-hub-subscriber notification. HTTP core semantics and caching define how resources are requested, represented, cached, and invalidated. Web Linking defines typed links among web resources. ([datatracker.ietf.org](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4287?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nFor news topology, these standards model **circulation traces**: publication, update, feed exposure, cache state, canonical links, alternate representations, and reachability. They do not model editorial sufficiency or truth.\n\n### 2.5 ActivityStreams, ActivityPub, and social circulation\n\nActivityStreams defines activities and objects; ActivityPub uses ActivityStreams for decentralized client-server and federated server-server delivery. This is relevant for modeling publication, update, boost/share, reply, correction, and retraction as activities. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/activitystreams-vocabulary/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nActivityPub-like systems are formally closer to event logs than to article schemas. They can record that an artifact was created, updated, announced, or deleted. They do not determine whether the artifactâ€™s claims are justified.\n\n### 2.6 Provenance and authenticity\n\nPROV-O models entities, activities, agents, derivation, attribution, generation, and use. It is a good basis for editorial provenance: an article was generated by an editing activity, used interviews and documents, and was associated with a reporter or editor. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nC2PA / Content Credentials aim to preserve origin and edit history for digital content using signed manifests, claims, assertions, credentials, and signatures. The C2PA manifest model is relevant for media authenticity, especially images, audio, and video. ([c2pa.org](https://c2pa.org/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nBut provenance is not truth. A cryptographically signed false claim is still false; an unsigned true observation may still be epistemically valuable. Recent security analyses of C2PA are also a warning that authenticity systems should not be treated as complete evidentiary systems without adversarial review; those specific references should be treated as needing ongoing verification because the security literature is still developing. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24890?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 2.7 Agentic protocols\n\nMCP and A2A matter because agentic news systems need machine-readable capabilities, tool calls, delegated actions, task state, and audit logs. MCP supplies a model in which servers expose tools, resources, and prompts to model clients; A2A supplies agent discovery and task collaboration among opaque agents. ([modelcontextprotocol.io](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18))\n\nFor news, these protocols require additional metadata:\n\n- source authority;\n- tool-use provenance;\n- human/agent authorship distinction;\n- task-specific epistemic contracts;\n- proof-carrying or evidence-carrying claims;\n- editorial gate results;\n- adversarial risk annotations;\n- citation graph requirements;\n- machine-checkable correction obligations.\n\nExisting semantic web standards are sufficient for representing many of these fields. They are not sufficient for enforcing them.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Existing theory landscape\n\nGatekeeping theory studies how possible information is selected, blocked, transformed, and passed through institutional channels. Formally, it suggests state transitions and partial orders over editorial admissibility: raw input, assigned lead, verified claim, edited draft, legal-cleared artifact, published artifact. Translation fails if â€œgateâ€ is reduced to a neutral function, because gatekeeping theory concerns power, institutional routines, ownership, source access, and professional norms. ([scholarcommons.scu.edu](https://scholarcommons.scu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1033&context=crt&utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nAgenda-setting theory concerns the relation between media salience and public salience. Formally, it suggests a time-indexed prominence function over topics, entities, and issues: \\(a(t,x)\\), with measurements such as placement, repetition, headline weight, homepage duration, push alerts, or feed rank. Translation fails at causal interpretation: salience correlation is not itself proof of public opinion formation. ([academic.oup.com](https://academic.oup.com/poq/article/36/2/176/1853310?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nFraming theory, especially Entmanâ€™s formulation, concerns selection and salience in presenting aspects of reality for problem definition, causal interpretation, moral evaluation, and treatment recommendation. Formally, framing can be modeled as a projection or weighting on a claim/event graph plus narrative role assignments. Translation fails because rhetoric, affect, genre, and political interpretation are not reducible to graph structure. ([onlinelibrary.wiley.com](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1993.tb01304.x?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nNews-values research, from Galtung and Ruge through Harcup and Oâ€™Neill, identifies criteria by which events become newsworthy. Formally, news values suggest scoring, ranking, and threshold functions over candidate events: negativity, proximity, elite actors, conflict, surprise, relevance, shareability, and similar attributes. Translation fails when cultural and institutional judgments are treated as universal metrics. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/002234336500200104?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nObjectivity studies, including Tuchmanâ€™s â€œobjectivity as strategic ritual,â€ suggest that objectivity can be modeled procedurally: attribution, balance conventions, quotation, documentary evidence, separation of news and opinion, and editorial verification. Translation fails if procedural objectivity is confused with metaphysical neutrality. ([journals.uchicago.edu](https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/225193?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nSource-dependence theory, including Gansâ€™s newsroom studies, suggests a bipartite or hypergraph between journalists, sources, institutions, beats, routines, and publishable stories. Formally, this supports source-reliance graphs and dependency measures. Translation fails where silence, intimidation, access journalism, and informal backgrounding are not recorded. ([semanticscholar.org](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Deciding-What%27s-News%3A-A-Study-of-CBS-Evening-News%2C-Gans/124fe5a22edb6cdc7e5a9c415c2b8c4fd658bdce?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nWire services and syndication suggest functors from newsroom packages to downstream artifacts, feeds, partner publications, and platform indexes. Corrections and updates suggest belief revision, truth maintenance, and versioned provenance. Computational journalism suggests pipelines for data extraction, document analysis, algorithmic accountability, and public-interest tooling. ([fredturner.stanford.edu](https://fredturner.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj27111/files/media/file/cohen-hamilton-turner-computational-journalism-cacm-10-111.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nPlatformization and SEO incentives belong to the circulation layer. They are modeled by reach, ranking, referral, and optimization traces, not by epistemic support. Work on platform power emphasizes that news organizations and platforms are structurally entangled; a formal news model must therefore distinguish editorial publication from platform discovery. ([reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/news/power-platforms?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nPropaganda, misinformation, and epistemic trust require adversarial modeling. Wardle and Derakhshanâ€™s â€œinformation disorderâ€ framework distinguishes mis-, dis-, and mal-information; Vosoughi, Roy, and Aralâ€™s Science study found that false news diffused farther, faster, deeper, and more broadly than true news on Twitter in their dataset. Formally, this motivates adversarial provenance, diffusion graphs, trust logics, and paraconsistent claim states. ([edoc.coe.int](https://edoc.coe.int/en/media/7495-information-disorder-toward-an-interdisciplinary-framework-for-research-and-policy-making.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n---\n\n## 4. Candidate primitives\n\n### 4.1 Event\n\nAn event is a hypothesized or observed occurrence:\n\n\\[\ne=(\\tau,\\lambda,\\mathsf{participants},\\mathsf{type},\\mathsf{uncertainty})\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\tau\\) is temporal extent, \\(\\lambda\\) is location or jurisdiction, and type comes from a controlled vocabulary when possible.\n\nEvents are not always primitive. In breaking news, the event is often inferred from claims: â€œthere was an explosion,â€ â€œthere was no explosion,â€ â€œofficials report a fire,â€ â€œwitnesses report smoke.â€ Therefore, event objects should usually be **constructed from claims**, not assumed as ground truth.\n\n### 4.2 Observation\n\nAn observation is a source-bound evidentiary item:\n\n\\[\no=(s,m,t,\\rho,d)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(s\\) is source, \\(m\\) is method, \\(t\\) is observation time, \\(\\rho\\) is raw record, and \\(d\\) is provenance.\n\nExamples: interview recording, agency statement, FOIA document, sensor reading, photograph, livestream capture, agent retrieval trace.\n\n### 4.3 Source\n\nA source is an agent, institution, instrument, document, dataset, or delegated computational process that can produce observations or assertions.\n\n\\[\ns=(id,type,authority,access,history,rights,risk)\n\\]\n\nThe model must separate **identity**, **authority**, **reliability history**, and **current assertion**. A source may be authentic and wrong, anonymous and right, automated and reliable, or human and adversarial.\n\n### 4.4 Claim\n\nA claim is the best central primitive:\n\n\\[\nc=(p,\\sigma,\\tau,\\alpha,\\pi,\\kappa)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(p\\): proposition-like content;\n- \\(\\sigma\\): scope: event/entity/place/topic/jurisdiction;\n- \\(\\tau\\): valid or asserted time;\n- \\(\\alpha\\): assertion force: alleges, confirms, denies, estimates, quotes, corrects;\n- \\(\\pi\\): provenance/support;\n- \\(\\kappa\\): editorial status: unverified, supported, contested, corrected, retracted.\n\nClaims are not just RDF triples. Some can be triples, but many are modal, temporal, vague, quoted, probabilistic, or jurisdiction-bound.\n\n### 4.5 Assertion\n\nAn assertion is a sourceâ€™s commitment to a claim under a communicative force:\n\n\\[\na=(source,claim,force,time,medium,context)\n\\]\n\nThis distinguishes â€œthe mayor said Xâ€ from â€œX is true.â€\n\n### 4.6 Story\n\nA story is an editorially ordered package of claims, entities, sources, context, narrative frame, and prominence choices. It is not merely a set of claims.\n\n\\[\nstory=(C',order,frame,headline,lede,background,omissions,gates)\n\\]\n\nThe formal model can track the claim set and provenance. It cannot fully formalize rhetorical force without additional discourse semantics.\n\n### 4.7 Published artifact\n\nA published artifact is a concrete representation:\n\n\\[\np=(uri,content,format,hash,version,metadata,rights,canonicalization)\n\\]\n\nExamples: article page, AMP page, PDF, video package, podcast, wire item, JSON-LD block, ninjs item, NewsML-G2 package.\n\n### 4.8 Feed item and syndication trace\n\nA feed item is a projection of a published artifact into a circulation channel:\n\n\\[\nf=(feed,id,item,updated,published,links,summary)\n\\]\n\nA syndication trace is a graph path from origin to downstream artifacts, partners, caches, search indexes, and social activities.\n\n### 4.9 Correction, update, retraction\n\nA correction is an editorial act modifying claim status or artifact content while preserving lineage.\n\n\\[\ncorr=(target,reason,oldClaims,newClaims,time,agent,visibility)\n\\]\n\nA correction is not just a new article. It is a transformation in the versioned claim-provenance graph.\n\n### 4.10 Reader demand signal\n\nA reader demand signal is a circulation-side observation: click, dwell, search query, share, subscription open, comment, referral, alert open. It should be modeled separately from truth and editorial value.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Formal constructions\n\n### 5.1 Categorical construction\n\nDefine a category \\(\\mathbf{NewsTrace}\\).\n\n**Objects.** Versioned news states:\n\n\\[\nX=(C,E,O,S,P,\\Pi,G)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C\\) is a claim graph, \\(E\\) an event hypothesis graph, \\(O\\) observations, \\(S\\) sources, \\(P\\) artifacts, \\(\\Pi\\) provenance, and \\(G\\) gate states.\n\n**Morphisms.** Editorial and circulation acts:\n\n\\[\nf:X\\to Y\n\\]\n\nsuch as ingest, assign, transcribe, normalize, entity-resolve, verify, edit, fact-check, package, publish, syndicate, correct, retract.\n\n**Identity.** The identity morphism preserves all claims, artifacts, provenance, and gate states.\n\n**Composition.** If \\(f:X\\to Y\\) and \\(g:Y\\to Z\\), then \\(g\\circ f:X\\to Z\\) is the sequential editorial trace. Composition is valid only when preconditions match: for example, `publish` requires a publishable gate state; `correct` requires an existing target.\n\nThis category models workflow and transformation well. It fails if morphisms are treated as pure deterministic functions. Editorial acts may be nondeterministic, lossy, probabilistic, legally constrained, and value-laden. More accurate versions use:\n\n- a **Kleisli category** for side effects, uncertainty, and audit logs;\n- a **double category** with editorial transformations horizontally and representation/projection transformations vertically;\n- a **fibration** over beats, topics, jurisdictions, publication surfaces, or standards.\n\nA functor\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{schema}}:\\mathbf{NewsTrace}\\to\\mathbf{RDFGraph}\n\\]\n\nmaps internal news states to public RDF/JSON-LD graphs. A functor\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{ninjs}}:\\mathbf{NewsTrace}\\to\\mathbf{JSON}\n\\]\n\nmaps them to ninjs items. A functor\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{feed}}:\\mathbf{NewsTrace}\\to\\mathbf{Feed}\n\\]\n\nmaps published artifacts to feed entries.\n\nA natural transformation between \\(F_{\\text{ninjs}}\\) and \\(F_{\\text{schema}}\\) would preserve selected invariants: URI, title/headline, publication time, modification time, authorship, source links, correction links, and topic identifiers. It will not preserve all semantics, because the target standards do not express exactly the same distinctions.\n\n**Adjunction candidate.** Let \\(U\\) forget editorial structure from a publishable story to its underlying claim graph. A left adjoint \\(L\\), if it exists, would construct the â€œfree publishable storyâ€ from a claim graph subject to editorial constraints. In real journalism this usually fails: there is no canonical minimal story because framing, lede choice, sourcing convention, legal risk, and audience relevance are not universal constructions.\n\n### 5.2 Sheaf/topological construction\n\nDefine a site \\((\\mathcal{C},J)\\).\n\nAn object \\(u\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) is a reportable context:\n\n\\[\nu=(T_u,L_u,Q_u,S_u,K_u)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(T_u\\) is a time interval, \\(L_u\\) a location/jurisdiction, \\(Q_u\\) a set of entities/topics, \\(S_u\\) an admissible source set, and \\(K_u\\) a standards/publication context.\n\nA morphism \\(v\\to u\\) is a refinement: narrower time, location, topic, source set, or publication surface.\n\nA covering family \\(\\{u_i\\to u\\}\\in J(u)\\) is valid when the \\(u_i\\) jointly satisfy a source-and-context coverage policy. Example: for a hazardous-material incident, a valid cover might require local official source, emergency response source, eyewitness or sensor evidence, and affected-area context.\n\nDefine a presheaf:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}:\\mathcal{C}^{op}\\to\\mathbf{Poset}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal{F}(u)\\) is the partially ordered set of claim graphs supported in context \\(u\\). The order may be informational refinement: \\(x\\leq y\\) if \\(y\\) contains all claims and support in \\(x\\), plus additional compatible support or status refinement.\n\nRestriction maps:\n\n\\[\n\\rho_{uv}:\\mathcal{F}(u)\\to\\mathcal{F}(v)\n\\]\n\nforget claims, sources, or provenance outside the smaller context \\(v\\), or weaken support when evidence leaves scope.\n\n**Separatedness.** If two local claim sections agree on all overlaps, they represent the same local news state modulo entity normalization.\n\n**Gluing.** A compatible family \\(\\{s_i\\in\\mathcal{F}(u_i)\\}\\) glues to \\(s\\in\\mathcal{F}(u)\\) when overlap restrictions agree and no hard incompatibility blocks synthesis.\n\nThis construction models local-to-global reporting, source-context compatibility, and editorial synthesis. It fails for full stories: two editors can construct different valid stories from the same compatible claim graph. Therefore, the sheaf candidate is strongest for **canonical claim-provenance graphs**, not for prose stories.\n\nContradiction can be handled two ways:\n\n1. Boolean claim sheaf: contradictions obstruct gluing.\n2. Paraconsistent claim sheaf: contradictions glue into a contested value rather than exploding.\n\nThe second is better for breaking news.\n\n### 5.3 Topology and closure systems\n\nSeveral closure operators are meaningful.\n\n**Semantic entailment closure.**\n\n\\[\ncl_{\\vdash}(C)=\\{d:C\\vdash d\\}\n\\]\n\nThis is extensive, monotone, and idempotent under a fixed entailment regime.\n\n**Publication-readiness closure.**\n\n\\[\ncl_G(C)=C\\cup\\{\\text{gate certificates derivable from }C\\}\n\\]\n\nThis is closure-like if gates are monotone. It fails when new evidence can remove readiness, as in legal risk, contradiction, or source withdrawal. Then \\(cl_G\\) must be indexed by time:\n\n\\[\ncl_{G,t}\n\\]\n\n**Syndication reach closure.**\n\nGiven a directed syndication graph \\(H\\), \\(cl_H(p)\\) is the set of artifacts, feeds, caches, and downstream items reachable from \\(p\\). This is a genuine reachability closure on a graph.\n\n**Freshness interior.**\n\nFreshness is usually not closure. It contracts with time. Define:\n\n\\[\nint_{\\Delta,t}(P)=\\{p\\in P:\\text{age}_t(p)<\\Delta \\text{ and no superseding correction exists}\\}\n\\]\n\nThis behaves more like a time-indexed interior or viability predicate.\n\n**Search visibility closure.** This is only weakly topological. Crawling and indexing have reachability aspects, but ranking, canonicalization, freshness, and policy interventions are opaque and nonmonotone.\n\n### 5.4 Order, lattice, and paraconsistent construction\n\nDefine an editorial state lattice:\n\n\\[\n\\mathsf{State}=\n\\mathsf{Completeness}\\times\n\\mathsf{Verification}\\times\n\\mathsf{Rights}\\times\n\\mathsf{Risk}\\times\n\\mathsf{Freshness}\n\\]\n\nA package is publishable if it lies above a threshold in required dimensions and below a risk bound. This models gates better than a linear workflow.\n\nFor truth status, use a bilattice:\n\n- \\(\\bot\\): neither supported nor refuted;\n- \\(T\\): supported true;\n- \\(F\\): supported false;\n- \\(\\top\\): both supported and refuted / contested.\n\nThere are two orders:\n\n- truth order: \\(F \\leq \\bot \\leq T\\), with contested handled separately;\n- knowledge order: \\(\\bot \\leq T,F \\leq \\top\\).\n\nThis models contested claims without trivial explosion. It fails to model source prestige, framing, and salience unless extended with weights and discourse structure.\n\n### 5.5 Formal concept analysis\n\nA formal context is:\n\n\\[\nK=(G,M,I)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(G\\) is a set of objects, \\(M\\) attributes, and \\(I\\subseteq G\\times M\\).\n\nFor news:\n\n- \\(G\\): claims, articles, sources, events, or reader queries;\n- \\(M\\): topics, entities, source types, news values, locations, jurisdictions, formats;\n- \\(I\\): incidence relation.\n\nThe resulting concept lattice groups extents and intents: for example, all claims involving â€œchemical spill,â€ â€œrail,â€ â€œpublic safety,â€ and â€œofficial source.â€ Formal concept analysis is useful for beat maps, coverage gaps, source dependence, entity-topic clusters, and audience-demand comparison. It fails for narrative sequence, temporal correction, and contradiction unless combined with temporal and paraconsistent structures. Ganter and Willeâ€™s work is the standard reference for FCA as a mathematical theory of concept lattices. ([philpapers.org](https://philpapers.org/rec/GANFCA-2?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 5.6 Graph, hypergraph, and provenance construction\n\nDefine a typed hypergraph:\n\n\\[\nH=(V,E,\\tau)\n\\]\n\nNodes include claims, events, sources, observations, artifacts, agents, topics, entities, and standards. Hyperedges include:\n\n- `supports(source, observation, claim)`;\n- `contradicts(claim_a, claim_b)`;\n- `quotes(article, assertion)`;\n- `derivedFrom(artifact, claims...)`;\n- `corrects(correction, artifact, claim)`;\n- `syndicates(origin, downstream_item)`;\n- `mentions(article, entity)`;\n- `uses(activity, entity)`.\n\nPROV supplies entity/activity/agent lineage. Provenance semirings supply algebra for how support accumulates: \\(+\\) for alternative support, \\(\\times\\) for joint derivation. Green, Karvounarakis, and Tannenâ€™s provenance semiring work shows how relational provenance, incomplete databases, probabilistic databases, bags, and why-provenance can be treated uniformly by semiring annotation. ([dl.acm.org](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1265530.1265535?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nThis construction is strong for auditability. It fails if semiring weights are interpreted as truth without epistemic theory.\n\n### 5.7 Temporal logic and event calculus\n\nNews has at least six relevant times:\n\n1. event occurrence time;\n2. observation time;\n3. assertion time;\n4. editorial decision time;\n5. publication/update/correction time;\n6. crawl, index, feed, and reader-exposure time.\n\nEvent calculus is appropriate for fluents such as:\n\n- `HoldsAt(Published(article), t)`;\n- `HoldsAt(Supported(claim, source), t)`;\n- `HoldsAt(Retracted(claim), t)`;\n- `Initiates(correction, Corrected(article), t)`;\n- `Terminates(correction, Current(article_v1), t)`.\n\nKowalski and Sergotâ€™s event calculus was designed for reasoning about events, time periods, and database updates, making it directly relevant to corrections and evolving news states. ([doc.ic.ac.uk](https://www.doc.ic.ac.uk/~rak/papers/event%20calculus.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nBelief revision and truth maintenance handle correction, contradiction, and retraction. AGM belief revision supplies a canonical tradition for rational belief change, while Doyleâ€™s truth-maintenance systems record and maintain reasons for beliefs. ([plato.stanford.edu](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-belief-revision/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 5.8 RDF, Datalog, SHACL, and validation\n\nA semantic-web layer should use named graphs:\n\n- one graph per source observation;\n- one graph per claim version;\n- one graph per article version;\n- one graph per correction;\n- one graph per syndication projection;\n- one graph per validation result.\n\nSHACL validates publication contracts. SPARQL queries support paths and correction lineage. Datalog handles recursive reachability, contradiction propagation, and rule-based gates.\n\nExample Datalog rules:\n\n```prolog id=\"w931cz\"\nsupported(C) :- assertion(A,C), source(A,S), trusted_for(S,Topic), about(C,Topic).\ncontested(C) :- contradicts(C,D), supported(C), supported(D).\nderived_artifact(P,C) :- artifact_uses(P,C).\nneeds_update(P) :- derived_artifact(P,C), corrected(C).\nreachable(X,Y) :- syndicates(X,Y).\nreachable(X,Z) :- syndicates(X,Y), reachable(Y,Z).\n```\n\nOWL/RDFS inference is useful for type hierarchies and vocabulary alignment, but correction and publication gates usually need closed-world validation and nonmonotonic update logic.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Worked example\n\n### 6.1 Scenario\n\nAt 10:05 America/Chicago on June 10, 2026, an incident is reported near the Riverbend rail yard.\n\nSources:\n\n- \\(S_1\\): city fire department statement at 10:20.\n- \\(S_2\\): transit agency statement at 10:25.\n- \\(S_3\\): eyewitness video uploaded at 10:30.\n\nClaims:\n\n- \\(c_1\\): â€œA chemical plume is visible near Riverbend rail yard.â€\n- \\(c_2\\): â€œOfficials ordered a one-mile evacuation.â€\n- \\(c_3\\): â€œNo railcar derailed.â€\n- \\(c_4\\): â€œTwo tank cars derailed.â€\n\nSupport:\n\n- \\(S_1\\) supports \\(c_1,c_2\\).\n- \\(S_2\\) supports \\(c_3\\).\n- \\(S_3\\) supports \\(c_1,c_4\\).\n\nContradiction:\n\n\\[\nc_3 \\perp c_4\n\\]\n\nbecause â€œno railcar derailedâ€ conflicts with â€œtwo tank cars derailed.â€\n\n### 6.2 Claim graph\n\n```json id=\"402cam\"\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim:c1\",\n      \"text\": \"A chemical plume is visible near Riverbend rail yard.\",\n      \"status\": \"supported\",\n      \"supportedBy\": [\"obs:fire-1020\", \"obs:video-1030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim:c2\",\n      \"text\": \"Officials ordered a one-mile evacuation.\",\n      \"status\": \"supported\",\n      \"supportedBy\": [\"obs:fire-1020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim:c3\",\n      \"text\": \"No railcar derailed.\",\n      \"status\": \"contested\",\n      \"supportedBy\": [\"obs:transit-1025\"],\n      \"contradicts\": [\"claim:c4\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim:c4\",\n      \"text\": \"Two tank cars derailed.\",\n      \"status\": \"contested\",\n      \"supportedBy\": [\"obs:video-1030\"],\n      \"contradicts\": [\"claim:c3\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nA Boolean sheaf of claims cannot glue \\(c_3\\) and \\(c_4\\) into a consistent global section. A paraconsistent claim sheaf can glue them into a global section with derailment status \\(\\top\\), meaning contested.\n\n### 6.3 Editorial synthesis\n\nThe editor publishes:\n\n> Officials order one-mile evacuation after chemical plume near Riverbend rail yard; cause remains disputed.\n\nThis story includes \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_2\\) as supported, and includes the derailment question as contested rather than resolved.\n\nCategorically:\n\n\\[\nX_0 \\xrightarrow{\\text{ingest}} X_1\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{verify/fire}} X_2\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{mark-contested}} X_3\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{publish}} X_4\n\\]\n\nThe invariant is that every public claim in \\(X_4\\) has either support, quotation attribution, or contested status.\n\n### 6.4 Standards emission\n\nThe JSON-LD layer is artifact metadata, not the full formal object:\n\n```json id=\"o36k8f\"\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n  \"@type\": \"NewsArticle\",\n  \"@id\": \"https://example.news/riverbend-plume#v1\",\n  \"headline\": \"Officials order one-mile evacuation after chemical plume near Riverbend rail yard\",\n  \"datePublished\": \"2026-06-10T10:55:00-05:00\",\n  \"dateModified\": \"2026-06-10T10:55:00-05:00\",\n  \"author\": { \"@type\": \"Person\", \"name\": \"Reporter A\" },\n  \"publisher\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Example News\" },\n  \"about\": [\n    { \"@id\": \"claim:c1\" },\n    { \"@id\": \"claim:c2\" },\n    { \"@id\": \"claim:c3\" },\n    { \"@id\": \"claim:c4\" }\n  ],\n  \"citation\": [\n    \"obs:fire-1020\",\n    \"obs:transit-1025\",\n    \"obs:video-1030\"\n  ],\n  \"creativeWorkStatus\": \"Published\"\n}\n```\n\nschema.org and Google structured-data conventions are appropriate for public web exposure and search interpretation, while IPTC formats are better suited for professional news exchange. ([schema.org](https://schema.org/NewsArticle?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nAn illustrative ninjs-like emission:\n\n```json id=\"q7dhh3\"\n{\n  \"uri\": \"https://example.news/riverbend-plume#v1\",\n  \"type\": \"text\",\n  \"title\": \"Officials order one-mile evacuation after chemical plume near Riverbend rail yard\",\n  \"versioncreated\": \"2026-06-10T10:55:00-05:00\",\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"body_text\": \"Officials ordered a one-mile evacuation after a chemical plume was reported near Riverbend rail yard. Accounts differ on whether railcars derailed.\",\n  \"subjects\": [\n    { \"code\": \"medtop:disaster-accident\", \"name\": \"accident and emergency incident\" }\n  ],\n  \"associations\": [\n    { \"type\": \"claimGraph\", \"uri\": \"urn:claimgraph:riverbend:v1\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative mapping, not a conformance specimen; exact field names and controlled values must be checked against the chosen ninjs version and IPTC vocabulary.\n\nA feed item exposes the publication/update state:\n\n```json id=\"qgeg4y\"\n{\n  \"id\": \"https://example.news/riverbend-plume#v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://example.news/riverbend-plume\",\n  \"title\": \"Officials order one-mile evacuation after chemical plume near Riverbend rail yard\",\n  \"date_published\": \"2026-06-10T10:55:00-05:00\",\n  \"date_modified\": \"2026-06-10T10:55:00-05:00\"\n}\n```\n\nFeed formats represent subscription and update delivery, not full claim semantics. ([datatracker.ietf.org](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4287?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\nPROV records derivation:\n\n```turtle id=\"tblkqv\"\n:article_v1 a prov:Entity ;\n  prov:wasGeneratedBy :publish_v1 ;\n  prov:wasDerivedFrom :claim_c1, :claim_c2, :claim_c3, :claim_c4 .\n\n:publish_v1 a prov:Activity ;\n  prov:used :obs_fire_1020, :obs_transit_1025, :obs_video_1030 ;\n  prov:wasAssociatedWith :editor_1 .\n\n:obs_fire_1020 a prov:Entity ;\n  prov:wasAttributedTo :fire_department .\n```\n\nPROV is strong for lineage but does not decide which claim is true. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 6.5 Later correction\n\nAt 11:15, the railroad confirms: one tank car derailed, not two. Create:\n\n- \\(c_5\\): â€œOne tank car derailed.â€\n- mark \\(c_3\\) false/corrected;\n- mark \\(c_4\\) over-specific and corrected;\n- update article to version \\(v_2\\);\n- emit correction notice;\n- propagate update to feed, sitemap, structured data, partner syndication, and downstream claim graph.\n\nBelief revision:\n\n\\[\nK' = (K \\setminus \\{c_3,c_4^{exact}\\}) \\cup \\{c_5,\\mathsf{corrects}(c_5,c_3),\\mathsf{corrects}(c_5,c_4)\\}\n\\]\n\nTruth maintenance records why the prior belief was held and why it was revised.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What is actually topological?\n\nThe following parts are genuinely topological or sheaf-like:\n\n1. **Reportable-context covers.** A breaking-news context can be covered by local source/time/entity neighborhoods.\n2. **Restriction.** Claims and support can be restricted to narrower contexts.\n3. **Compatibility.** Local claim graphs can agree or fail to agree on overlaps.\n4. **Gluing.** Compatible local claim graphs can form a larger claim graph.\n5. **Obstruction.** Contradiction, missing provenance, incompatible timestamps, unresolved entity identity, or rights restrictions can block gluing.\n6. **Closure.** Semantic entailment, syndication reach, and some validation saturations satisfy closure-like properties.\n7. **Interior/freshness.** Fresh currently valid artifacts can be treated by time-indexed interior-like operators.\n\nThe following parts are not inherently topological:\n\n1. **Prose story form.** Two stories can be valid over the same claim graph and still differ in frame, lede, emphasis, and omission.\n2. **Editorial judgment.** Gatekeeping is order-theoretic and institutional, not merely topological.\n3. **Truth.** Topology does not decide truth; it structures local support and compatibility.\n4. **Audience interpretation.** Reader uptake requires pragmatics, psychology, politics, platform studies, and media theory.\n5. **Search rank.** Crawling has graph structure, but ranking and visibility are policy-laden and nonmonotone.\n6. **Agent reliability.** Agent capability discovery is not epistemic warrant.\n\nThe disciplined conclusion: a â€œnews topologyâ€ can be defined as a **site of reportable contexts with sheaves or presheaves of supported claim-provenance graphs**, plus closure/interior operators for entailment, reach, validation, and freshness. It cannot replace provenance, temporal logic, paraconsistent logic, or media theory.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Implementation sketch\n\n### 8.1 TypeScript core\n\n```ts id=\"sfsoe0\"\ntype URI = string;\ntype ISODateTime = string;\n\ntype ClaimStatus =\n  | \"unassessed\"\n  | \"supported\"\n  | \"contested\"\n  | \"corrected\"\n  | \"retracted\"\n  | \"false\"\n  | \"unknown\";\n\ntype EditorialActType =\n  | \"ingest\"\n  | \"assign\"\n  | \"observe\"\n  | \"assert\"\n  | \"verify\"\n  | \"edit\"\n  | \"fact_check\"\n  | \"publish\"\n  | \"syndicate\"\n  | \"update\"\n  | \"correct\"\n  | \"retract\";\n\ninterface TimeInterval {\n  start?: ISODateTime;\n  end?: ISODateTime;\n}\n\ninterface Source {\n  id: URI;\n  kind: \"human\" | \"institution\" | \"document\" | \"dataset\" | \"sensor\" | \"agent\";\n  label: string;\n  authorityScope?: URI[];\n  reliabilityProfile?: URI;\n  rights?: URI;\n}\n\ninterface Observation {\n  id: URI;\n  source: URI;\n  observedAt: ISODateTime;\n  method: URI;\n  rawRecord?: URI;\n  provenanceGraph?: URI;\n  c2paManifest?: URI;\n}\n\ninterface Assertion {\n  id: URI;\n  source: URI;\n  claim: URI;\n  force: \"states\" | \"denies\" | \"estimates\" | \"quotes\" | \"confirms\" | \"alleges\";\n  assertedAt: ISODateTime;\n  observation?: URI;\n}\n\ninterface Claim {\n  id: URI;\n  text: string;\n  normalized?: {\n    subject?: URI;\n    predicate?: URI;\n    object?: unknown;\n  };\n  about: URI[];\n  validTime?: TimeInterval;\n  status: ClaimStatus;\n  assertions: URI[];\n  supports?: URI[];\n  contradicts?: URI[];\n  corrects?: URI[];\n  correctedBy?: URI[];\n}\n\ninterface EditorialGateResult {\n  gate: URI;\n  passed: boolean;\n  checkedAt: ISODateTime;\n  reasons: string[];\n}\n\ninterface EditorialAct {\n  id: URI;\n  type: EditorialActType;\n  agent: URI;\n  startedAt: ISODateTime;\n  endedAt?: ISODateTime;\n  inputs: URI[];\n  outputs: URI[];\n  gateResults?: EditorialGateResult[];\n  provActivity?: URI;\n}\n\ninterface Artifact {\n  id: URI;\n  canonicalUrl: URI;\n  version: string;\n  format: \"html\" | \"jsonld\" | \"ninjs\" | \"newsmlg2\" | \"atom\" | \"jsonfeed\";\n  generatedFromClaims: URI[];\n  publishedAt?: ISODateTime;\n  modifiedAt?: ISODateTime;\n  correctionOf?: URI;\n}\n```\n\n### 8.2 RDF / JSON-LD model\n\nUse named graphs:\n\n- `graph:source-observations`;\n- `graph:claims-v1`;\n- `graph:article-v1`;\n- `graph:prov-v1`;\n- `graph:validation-v1`;\n- `graph:correction-v2`.\n\nMap:\n\n- schema.org for public article metadata;\n- PROV-O for lineage;\n- SKOS/IPTC Media Topics for classification;\n- C2PA references for media authenticity;\n- custom claim ontology for assertion force, contradiction, correction, and gate state.\n\n### 8.3 Claim graph algorithms\n\nCore algorithms:\n\n1. normalize incoming artifacts to canonical claim graph;\n2. entity-resolve sources, places, institutions, and topics;\n3. compute support edges;\n4. detect contradiction candidates;\n5. assign bilattice truth states;\n6. run editorial gate validation;\n7. emit artifacts;\n8. propagate correction to derived artifacts and feed items.\n\nContradiction detection should not be only lexical. It needs temporal scope, modality, attribution, and entity resolution.\n\n### 8.4 Editorial gate algebra\n\nLet a gate be:\n\n\\[\ng:X\\to \\{\\mathsf{pass},\\mathsf{fail},\\mathsf{warn}\\}\\times Reasons\n\\]\n\nGate composition:\n\n- \\(g_1\\wedge g_2\\): both required;\n- \\(g_1\\vee g_2\\): alternative satisfaction;\n- \\(g_1;g_2\\): sequential dependency;\n- \\(\\neg g\\): generally unsafe for editorial gates unless used only as a diagnostic.\n\nExample gates:\n\n- every factual claim has support or attribution;\n- every anonymous source has editor approval;\n- every image has provenance or disclosure;\n- every correction has target artifact and explanation;\n- every agent-generated claim has tool-use trace and human-review status.\n\n### 8.5 Closure and freshness computation\n\n```ts id=\"zi3qcy\"\nfunction reachabilityClosure(start: URI, edges: Array<[URI, URI]>): Set<URI> {\n  const seen = new Set<URI>([start]);\n  const queue = [start];\n\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const x = queue.shift()!;\n    for (const [a, b] of edges) {\n      if (a === x && !seen.has(b)) {\n        seen.add(b);\n        queue.push(b);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return seen;\n}\n\nfunction freshnessScore(\n  publishedAt: Date,\n  now: Date,\n  halfLifeMinutes: number,\n  superseded: boolean\n): number {\n  if (superseded) return 0;\n  const ageMinutes = (now.getTime() - publishedAt.getTime()) / 60000;\n  return Math.pow(0.5, ageMinutes / halfLifeMinutes);\n}\n```\n\nTest closure operators for:\n\n- extensity: \\(A\\subseteq cl(A)\\);\n- monotonicity: \\(A\\subseteq B\\Rightarrow cl(A)\\subseteq cl(B)\\);\n- idempotence: \\(cl(cl(A))=cl(A)\\).\n\nDo not claim a process is topological unless these tests pass or the failure is explicit.\n\n### 8.6 SHACL validation\n\nUse SHACL for closed-world publication constraints:\n\n- `NewsArticle` must have headline, datePublished, author/publisher, canonical URI;\n- factual claims must have source attribution or explicit uncertainty;\n- corrections must point to corrected artifact and corrected claim;\n- generated media must have provenance or disclosure;\n- feed item must correspond to a published artifact;\n- agent-authored material must have agent identity and tool-use trace.\n\n### 8.7 SPARQL / Datalog query layer\n\nRepresentative questions:\n\n- Which claims in this article are contested?\n- Which downstream feed items derive from a corrected claim?\n- Which sources support this claim?\n- Which articles depend on a source later marked unreliable?\n- Which artifacts are stale under freshness threshold \\(\\Delta\\)?\n- Which agent-generated assertions lack tool-use provenance?\n\n### 8.8 Testing strategy\n\nUse four test classes:\n\n1. **Algebraic tests:** identity and composition of editorial morphisms; closure laws where asserted.\n2. **Sheaf tests:** compatible local claim graphs glue; contradictory Boolean sections fail; paraconsistent sections glue as contested.\n3. **Standards tests:** JSON-LD, ninjs, feeds, PROV, SHACL validation against versioned fixtures.\n4. **Adversarial tests:** missing provenance, malicious agent tool description, stripped C2PA metadata, forged source identity, late correction, platform cache stale state.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Open problems\n\n1. **Claim individuation.** Natural-language claims do not have canonical boundaries.\n2. **Event individuation.** Events can be split, merged, renamed, or retroactively reclassified.\n3. **Attribution semantics.** Quotation, paraphrase, allegation, confirmation, denial, and inference require different logics.\n4. **Correction semantics.** Corrections are not always simple replacement; they may narrow, weaken, contextualize, or retract.\n5. **Nonmonotonic semantic web.** RDF/OWL do not natively fit evolving correction states.\n6. **Formal covers.** Source diversity and evidentiary sufficiency are institutional norms, not obvious Grothendieck topologies.\n7. **Gluing uniqueness.** Claim graphs may glue uniquely; stories usually do not.\n8. **Framing formalization.** Formal models risk erasing ideology, rhetoric, and genre.\n9. **Agent accountability.** Agent protocols describe capabilities and tasks, but not epistemic responsibility.\n10. **Provenance security.** Signed provenance can be absent, stripped, misleading, or cryptographically valid but journalistically false.\n11. **Platform opacity.** Search and social visibility are partially observable and policy-dependent.\n12. **Rights and safety.** Publication gates include legal, ethical, privacy, and harm considerations that are not reducible to truth.\n13. **Benchmarks.** A test corpus would need source observations, claim graphs, article versions, corrections, feeds, provenance, and circulation traces.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Annotated bibliography\n\n### Primary standards and technical sources\n\n**IPTC NewsML-G2, News Architecture, ninjs, NewsCodes, Media Topics, RightsML, rNews.** Core professional-news exchange standards and vocabularies. Use these for package structure, JSON serialization, controlled topics, rights metadata, and newsroom exchange. They are not sufficient as claim logic. ([iptc.org](https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**schema.org `NewsArticle`, `Claim`, `ClaimReview`, `CreativeWork`.** Public web metadata vocabulary useful for article exposure, claim review markup, citations, authorship, and publication dates. It is artifact-centered and search-oriented. ([schema.org](https://schema.org/NewsArticle?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**RDF, JSON-LD, SPARQL, OWL, SKOS, SHACL, PROV-O, DCAT.** Semantic-web substrate for graph representation, linked-data serialization, query, ontology reasoning, concept vocabularies, validation, provenance, and dataset catalogs. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Atom, JSON Feed, WebSub, HTTP, Web Linking.** Circulation and web-architecture standards for feed items, update delivery, caching, resource semantics, and typed links. Use these for publication and syndication traces, not claim truth. ([datatracker.ietf.org](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4287?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**ActivityStreams and ActivityPub.** Useful for decentralized publication, update, announcement, and retraction activities. They model social circulation events more than editorial epistemology. ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/activitystreams-vocabulary/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**C2PA / Content Credentials.** Important for signed media provenance and authenticity manifests. Should be used as one evidentiary layer, not as a truth guarantee. ([c2pa.org](https://c2pa.org/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**MCP and A2A.** Agentic protocol sources for tool exposure, resources, prompts, agent discovery, and cross-agent task exchange. They motivate affordance profiles and audit requirements for agentic news. ([modelcontextprotocol.io](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18))\n\n### Scholarly and theoretical sources\n\n**McCombs and Shaw, agenda-setting.** Supports formal salience functions over topics and time, while warning that prominence is not truth. ([academic.oup.com](https://academic.oup.com/poq/article/36/2/176/1853310?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Entman, framing.** Supports modeling selection, salience, and interpretive role assignment, but not reducing meaning to graph structure. ([onlinelibrary.wiley.com](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1993.tb01304.x?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Tuchman, objectivity as strategic ritual.** Supports procedural modeling of attribution and verification, while preserving critique of objectivity as institutional practice. ([journals.uchicago.edu](https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/225193?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Gans, source dependence and newsroom routines.** Supports source-news bipartite graphs and institutional dependency analysis. ([semanticscholar.org](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Deciding-What%27s-News%3A-A-Study-of-CBS-Evening-News%2C-Gans/124fe5a22edb6cdc7e5a9c415c2b8c4fd658bdce?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Galtung and Ruge; Harcup and Oâ€™Neill, news values.** Supports formal ranking and selection functions for newsworthiness, with cultural and institutional limitations. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/002234336500200104?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Cohen, Hamilton, and Turner; Diakopoulos.** Computational journalism and algorithmic accountability sources for document analysis, data extraction, public-interest tooling, and investigation of computational power. ([fredturner.stanford.edu](https://fredturner.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj27111/files/media/file/cohen-hamilton-turner-computational-journalism-cacm-10-111.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Wardle and Derakhshan; Vosoughi, Roy, and Aral.** Information disorder and diffusion evidence motivating adversarial provenance, trust logic, and spread modeling. ([edoc.coe.int](https://edoc.coe.int/en/media/7495-information-disorder-toward-an-interdisciplinary-framework-for-research-and-policy-making.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Green, Karvounarakis, and Tannen, provenance semirings.** Formal basis for support accumulation and transformation annotations. ([dl.acm.org](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1265530.1265535?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Ganter and Wille, formal concept analysis.** Basis for concept lattices over topics, entities, sources, claims, and demand signals. ([philpapers.org](https://philpapers.org/rec/GANFCA-2?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Kowalski and Sergot, event calculus.** Basis for event, update, correction, and fluent reasoning over time. ([doc.ic.ac.uk](https://www.doc.ic.ac.uk/~rak/papers/event%20calculus.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**AGM belief revision and Doyle truth maintenance.** Basis for correction, retraction, contradiction management, and reason-tracking. ([plato.stanford.edu](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-belief-revision/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n---\n\nThe earned hypothesis is therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\text{News topology} =\n\\text{a site of reportable contexts}\n+\n\\text{presheaves/sheaves of claim-provenance graphs}\n+\n\\text{closure/interior operators}\n}\n\\]\n\nbut only when embedded in categorical workflow, temporal logic, paraconsistent status, provenance graphs, validation gates, and media-theoretic constraints.\n"},{"sourceId":"prior-art/claims-to-prose","digest":"sha256:fa467332dc400e66204a0e0ed7cdba9283e92af619e0126014fefd06fecd0a82","content":"---\ntitle: Claims to Prose â€” Faithful Editorial Generation\ndescription: A skeptical report arguing that prose generation from structured claims is not a function but a constrained licensed-realization relation, implemented as an auditable NLG pipeline under type/contract constraints, informed by argumentation and provenance, and optionally indexed by editorial context. Surveys NLG/data-to-text, discourse and pragmatics, argumentation and paraconsistency, journalism norms, and faithfulness evaluation; gives an error taxonomy (modality strengthening, attribution erasure, contradiction smoothing, headline overclaim) and a worked MCP example across wire/explainer/editorial/correction genres. Judges \"claims-to-prose fiber algebra\" justified only in a restricted indexed sense, not as a full theory.\ndate-created: 2026-06-19\ndate-modified: 2026-06-19\ntags: [claims-to-prose, licensed-realization, natural-language-generation, attribution-preservation, modality-preservation, faithfulness, hallucination, argumentation, paraconsistent, discourse-planning, error-taxonomy, headline-discipline, indexed-realization]\n---\n\n# From Structured Claims to Prose: A Skeptical Research Report on Faithful Editorial Generation\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe best-supported model is **not** a simple function from claims to prose. Existing research supports a **hybrid model**: a structured claim/evidence/provenance layer; a discourse and genre planning layer; a realization layer; and a verification/editorial gate layer. â€œClaims-to-prose fiber algebraâ€ is **not an established term** in the relevant literature; exact searches returned mathematical uses of â€œfiber algebra,â€ not a known NLG, journalism, or discourse model. At most, a **restricted fibered/indexed model** may help describe how different editorial contexts license different families of prose realizations. It does not replace NLG, discourse theory, argumentation, provenance, semantics, or human editorial judgment.\n\nThe central finding: prose generation from evidence should be treated as a **constrained relation and planning problem**, not as direct translation. The output must preserve attribution, modality, uncertainty, contradiction, provenance, correction state, and stance boundaries while allowing genre-appropriate selection, aggregation, ordering, and compression.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The neutral claims-to-prose problem\n\nThe neutral problem is:\n\n> Given a structured collection of source statements, observations, claims, evidence, provenance, uncertainty, contradictions, correction state, and editorial goals, produce a prose artifact that is readable and useful without asserting more than the evidence licenses.\n\nThis is adjacent to **natural language generation**, especially data-to-text, graph-to-text, RDF-to-text, document planning, discourse planning, and surface realization. Classic NLG already decomposes generation into tasks such as content determination, discourse planning, aggregation, lexicalization, referring-expression generation, and linguistic realization. Reiter and Daleâ€™s applied NLG work is still the clearest pipeline reference point. See: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/natural-language-engineering/article/building-applied-natural-language-generation-systems/FEB374A3FF652F06D8567A6FAB2EF36E\n\nThe input is **not just facts**. It contains different kinds of items:\n\n| Item | Meaning in this problem |\n|---|---|\n| **Source statement** | What a source said, wrote, measured, or published. |\n| **Claim** | A proposition abstracted from one or more source statements. |\n| **Assertion** | A commitment made by the publicationâ€™s prose. |\n| **Evidence** | Material bearing on a claimâ€™s support, refutation, or uncertainty. |\n| **Observation** | A measurement or direct report, often still source-bound. |\n| **Inference** | A derived claim licensed by rules, background, or editorial reasoning. |\n| **Editorial judgment** | A decision about salience, framing, genre, order, emphasis, or risk. |\n| **Interpretation** | A higher-level reading that may be plausible but not simply entailed. |\n| **Frame / stance** | The organizing perspective or evaluative posture of the artifact. |\n| **Prose sentence** | A linguistic realization that may assert, imply, presuppose, hedge, quote, or attribute. |\n| **Headline** | A high-risk compressed artifact with special pragmatic force. |\n| **Quotation** | Source wording reproduced under quotation norms. |\n| **Paraphrase** | Source content restated in publication wording. |\n| **Summary** | Selected compression of a larger evidence or claim set. |\n| **Correction** | A later update identifying and repairing a prior error. |\n| **Retraction** | Withdrawal of a prior publication or claim status. |\n| **Publication artifact** | Story, brief, explainer, editorial, liveblog, correction notice. |\n| **Metadata artifact** | Structured metadata describing the artifact. |\n| **Search/discovery artifact** | Headline, snippet, schema markup, sitemap, feed entry, social card. |\n\nWhat must be preserved is not only factual content. It includes **attribution**, **modality**, **evidentiality**, **uncertainty**, **contradiction**, **scope**, **time**, **source dependence**, **correction state**, and **the boundary between reported fact and editorial interpretation**.\n\nWhat may be transformed includes selection, ordering, aggregation, lexical choice, genre structure, explanatory background, and compression. But compression becomes distortion when it changes the publicationâ€™s commitments.\n\nA transformation error occurs when prose **asserts, implies, presupposes, or pragmatically suggests** something not licensed by the structured evidence and editorial rules.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Literature review\n\n### 2.1 Natural language generation and data-to-text\n\nClassic NLG treats generation as a pipeline: decide content, structure it, aggregate propositions, choose words, generate referring expressions, and realize surface syntax. This is directly relevant because claims-to-prose is not one operation; it is a sequence of decisions. Source: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/natural-language-engineering/article/building-applied-natural-language-generation-systems/FEB374A3FF652F06D8567A6FAB2EF36E\n\nModern data-to-text and graph-to-text research extends this to RDF triples, tables, and knowledge graphs. The WebNLG challenge maps RDF triples to text and explicitly tests choices such as lexicalization, aggregation, referring-expression generation, and sentence segmentation. Source: https://aclanthology.org/W17-3518/ DART and ToTTo provide large structured-data-to-text datasets; ToTToâ€™s authors emphasize controlled table-to-text generation and note hallucination of unsupported phrases as a problem. Sources: https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.37/ and https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.89/\n\nThe important lesson is that NLG can formalize **structured input â†’ discourse plan â†’ text**, but most systems do not fully preserve provenance, evidential status, contradiction, or correction history unless those are explicitly represented and constrained. Pipeline and hybrid systems often make these constraints more inspectable than end-to-end neural systems; comparative work on RDF-to-text found explicit intermediate planning could improve generalization and reduce hallucination. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09022\n\n### 2.2 Discourse theory, rhetoric, and pragmatics\n\nProse does not merely list claims. It organizes them using relations such as background, evidence, concession, elaboration, contrast, cause, consequence, and evaluation. Rhetorical Structure Theory models texts as relations among spans, often nucleus-satellite structures. Source: https://www.sfu.ca/rst/05bibliographies/bibs/Mann_Thompson_1988.pdf\n\nThis matters because discourse relations can themselves become **additional editorial assertions**. For example:\n\n> â€œThe protocol promises interoperability, but researchers warn of tool-poisoning risks.â€\n\nThe word â€œbutâ€ introduces a contrast relation. That contrast may be licensed if the evidence really supports a tension between promise and risk; otherwise it is editorial framing disguised as neutral connection.\n\nFormal semantics and pragmatics show why prose can accidentally strengthen claims. Presupposition theory studies information treated as taken for granted; implicature studies what a speaker suggests beyond literal content. Sources: https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2014/entries/presupposition/ and https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/implicature/ Evidentiality and modality mark how a speaker knows something and how strongly it is asserted. English often expresses evidential distinctions through verbs and adverbs such as â€œalleged,â€ â€œreported,â€ â€œapparently,â€ â€œmay,â€ and â€œwarned.â€ Source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1890020/\n\nTherefore, a claims-to-prose system must track not only proposition content but also **commitment strength**.\n\n### 2.3 Argumentation, evidence, contradiction, and provenance\n\nComputational argumentation distinguishes claims that support, attack, undercut, or defeat other claims. Dungâ€™s abstract argumentation frameworks model conflict relations among arguments as directed graphs; structured argumentation adds premises, conclusions, and strict or defeasible inference rules. Source: https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/33651/35806 Defeasible reasoning is especially relevant because journalistic claims are often provisional: later evidence can defeat or qualify earlier support. Source: https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/reasoning-defeasible/\n\nContradiction should not always be resolved. Paraconsistent logics are relevant because they permit reasoning with inconsistent information without trivializing the system. Source: https://iep.utm.edu/para-log/ Provenance semiring work is also relevant: it studies how query results can retain information about the source tuples and derivations that produced them. Source: https://web.cs.ucdavis.edu/~green/papers/pods07.pdf\n\nFact-checking pipelines usually decompose the task into claim detection, evidence retrieval, and claim verification. Source: https://direct.mit.edu/tacl/article/doi/10.1162/tacl_a_00454/109469/A-Survey-on-Automated-Fact-Checking FEVER labels claims as supported, refuted, or not enough information, with evidence sentences; AVeriTeC extends this with labels including conflicting evidence or cherry-picking. Sources: https://aclanthology.org/N18-1074/ and https://fever.ai/dataset/averitec.html\n\nThese labels are useful for claims-to-prose because prose often needs to say â€œevidence is mixed,â€ not force a binary verdict.\n\n### 2.4 Journalism and computational journalism\n\nJournalism already has professional norms for transforming facts and source statements into prose. The SPJ Code emphasizes verification, original sources, context, correction, and the continuing duty to update information. Source: https://www.spj.org/spj-code-of-ethics/ Reuters states principles of integrity, independence, and freedom from bias; its AI guidance stresses human input and disclosure where AI-generated content is involved. Source: https://www.thomsonreuters.com/en/about-us/trust-principles APâ€™s generative AI standards treat AI output as unvetted source material and say AP does not use tools like ChatGPT to create publishable content without editorial judgment and sourcing. Source: https://www.ap.org/the-definitive-source/behind-the-news/standards-around-generative-ai/\n\nThis literature implies that â€œfaithfulâ€ prose is not merely logically entailed prose. It must also satisfy professional norms: clear attribution, fair representation, source transparency, correction propagation, headline fairness, and genre-appropriate framing.\n\nAutomated journalism research distinguishes template/pipeline approaches from neural approaches. The key relevance here is auditability: a system used for publication must expose what was selected, omitted, attributed, and inferred. Source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445913/\n\n### 2.5 Factuality, faithfulness, and hallucination\n\nFaithfulness is a major open problem in NLG. Surveys define unfaithful generation as text containing information not supported by the input or source material. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05227\n\nSeveral evaluation lines matter:\n\n- **FactCC** checks whether summary claims are consistent with source documents and can identify support or inconsistency spans. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.750/\n- **QAGS** uses question answering to compare source and generated summary answers. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.450/\n- **SummaC** uses NLI over source and generated text with sentence-level decomposition and aggregation. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2022.tacl-1.10/\n- **Attributable to Identified Sources** asks whether generated output pertaining to the external world is verified against provided source material. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2023.cl-4.2/\n\nThese methods are useful but insufficient. NLI models can mishandle presuppositions and implicatures, sometimes treating pragmatic inferences as entailments. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.03066 That is a serious issue for journalism, where headlines, hedges, and attribution verbs can change commitments without changing obvious factual tokens.\n\n### 2.6 Standards and structured publication artifacts\n\nRelevant standards divide into several groups.\n\n**Publication and discovery metadata.** Schema.org `NewsArticle` models news articles and related metadata; `ClaimReview` models fact-checking reviews of claims. Sources: https://schema.org/NewsArticle and https://schema.org/ClaimReview Google has changed how ClaimReview appears in Search, but still supports it in Fact Check Explorer. Source: https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/factcheck\n\n**News exchange formats.** IPTC NewsML-G2 supports exchange of news items and packages with rich metadata; IPTC ninjs provides a JSON representation for news. Source: https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/ IPTC Media Topics provides a taxonomy for categorization. Source: https://iptc.org/standards/media-topics/\n\n**Provenance and validation.** W3C PROV-O represents provenance; SHACL validates RDF graphs against shapes; RDF represents information as triples and, in RDF 1.2, datasets with named graphs. Sources: https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/ and https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/ and https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/\n\n**Content provenance.** C2PA Content Credentials describe manifests, assertions, claims, and signatures for content provenance, but C2PAâ€™s technical â€œclaimâ€ is not the same as a journalistic claim. Source: https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.4/index.html\n\n**Syndication and distribution.** ActivityPub/ActivityStreams, Atom, JSON Feed, and Google structured data/news sitemap requirements help distribute artifacts but do not, by themselves, preserve claim-to-sentence alignment. Source: https://www.w3.org/TR/activitypub/\n\nThe standards landscape can represent articles, metadata, provenance, categories, and fact-check reviews. It does **not** provide a complete standard for claim-to-prose alignment, modality preservation, contradiction handling, or editorial stance tracking.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Candidate models\n\n| Model | What it is | What it models well | What it fails to model | Miniature example | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Function** | `claims â†’ prose` | Deterministic template generation in narrow domains. | Many valid outputs; genre variation; omission; editorial choice; uncertainty. | Same evidence can yield a wire brief, explainer, editorial, or correction. | Too simple except for constrained templates. |\n| **Relation** | `Licensed(claim_base, prose)` | Many possible faithful realizations; allows constraints. | Needs separate planning and checking machinery. | â€œResearchers warn of risksâ€ and â€œA paper reports risksâ€ may both be licensed. | Core formal view. |\n| **Pipeline** | Content selection â†’ discourse plan â†’ realization. | Matches classic NLG; inspectable intermediate choices. | Does not automatically preserve provenance or modality unless designed in. | Select claims C1, C3, C9; order as lede/background/risk. | Necessary but not sufficient. |\n| **Planning problem** | Choose content and rhetorical structure under constraints. | Genre, salience, readability, risk tradeoffs. | Objective functions encode editorial values; optimization can hide bias. | Maximize relevance while requiring uncertainty markers. | Useful when paired with contracts. |\n| **Type system / contract** | Every sentence plan carries support, attribution, modality, and allowed transformations. | Checkability and audit trail. | Cannot decide newsworthiness or fairness alone. | A sentence with modality `certain` cannot realize a claim with modality `possible`. | Strongly recommended. |\n| **Logic / entailment** | Generated assertions must be entailed by claims plus allowed background. | Good for simple factual consistency. | Too strict for summaries, attributions, explanations, and pragmatic effects. | â€œThe company said Xâ€ is entailed by a source statement; â€œX is trueâ€ may not be. | Use as one checker, not full model. |\n| **Argumentation framework** | Claims support, attack, undercut, or defeat others. | Contradiction, contested claims, corrections. | Does not generate prose structure by itself. | Security paper attacks companyâ€™s implied â€œsafe integrationâ€ framing. | Combine with discourse planning. |\n| **Probabilistic / decision-theoretic** | Optimize expected usefulness, faithfulness, readability, and risk. | Uncertainty and ranking among possible realizations. | Can launder editorial values as numbers. | Penalize unsupported additions and high-risk headlines. | Useful as ranking layer. |\n| **Category / fibration / indexed model** | Contexts index families of licensed realizations. | Genre/audience/platform variation; reindexing when context changes. | Not a surface realization theory; little direct empirical support in NLG. | The â€œwireâ€ fiber licenses neutral attribution; â€œeditorialâ€ fiber licenses explicit stance. | Restricted use only. |\n| **Sheaf / gluing model** | Local evidence contexts are checked for compatible global narratives. | Partial, local, contradictory evidence; source-context compatibility. | More plausible for claim graphs than for prose generation itself. | Local legal/security/company accounts may not glue into one settled narrative. | Interesting but mostly auxiliary. |\n\nThe best overall model is:\n\n> a **licensed-relation model** implemented as an **auditable NLG pipeline**, constrained by **type/contracts**, informed by **argumentation/provenance**, evaluated by **faithfulness and editorial review**, and optionally indexed by editorial context.\n\n---\n\n## 4. What must be preserved\n\nA faithful claims-to-prose system needs preservation obligations.\n\n1. **Attribution preservation**  \n   A sourceâ€™s statement must not become the publicationâ€™s unqualified assertion.\n\n2. **Modality preservation**  \n   â€œMay,â€ â€œcould,â€ â€œalleges,â€ â€œwarns,â€ â€œpreliminary,â€ â€œunconfirmed,â€ and â€œaccording toâ€ must not be strengthened into settled fact.\n\n3. **Evidentiality preservation**  \n   Observation, report, inference, estimate, expert interpretation, and company claim must remain distinguishable.\n\n4. **Uncertainty preservation**  \n   Unknowns must remain unknown; absence of evidence must not become evidence of absence.\n\n5. **Contradiction preservation**  \n   Conflicting evidence must not be smoothed into false consensus.\n\n6. **Provenance preservation**  \n   Claims must remain traceable to source records, derivations, and versions.\n\n7. **Correction and retraction state**  \n   Corrected or retracted claims must not be reused as current evidence without warning.\n\n8. **Temporal scope**  \n   â€œAs of November 2024,â€ â€œin the 2025-11-25 specification,â€ and â€œin a 2026 security paperâ€ are different commitments.\n\n9. **Stance boundary**  \n   Reported fact, analysis, and editorial recommendation must be separated.\n\n10. **Discourse relation licensing**  \n    Cause, contrast, concession, trend, and consequence relations must be supported, not smuggled in.\n\n11. **Headline discipline**  \n    A headline must not assert more strongly than the story body.\n\n12. **Distribution-layer separation**  \n    Search intent, audience fit, platform optimization, and ad suitability are publication constraints, not evidence. They should not alter evidential force.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Error taxonomy\n\n| Error | Definition | Example | Detection / prevention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Hallucination** | Generated content unsupported by source. | â€œMCP is a W3C standardâ€ when sources do not say that. | Source alignment, AIS, NLI/QA checks, human review. |\n| **Unsupported addition** | Plausible background added as fact. | â€œThe protocol is widely adoptedâ€ without adoption evidence. | Require background-claim records. |\n| **Omitted qualifier** | Dropping limiting language. | â€œResearchers found MCP unsafeâ€ from â€œsome clients may be vulnerable.â€ | Modality contract check. |\n| **False certainty** | Uncertain claim presented as settled. | â€œMCP creates vulnerabilitiesâ€ instead of â€œresearchers report risks.â€ | Modality/evidentiality checker. |\n| **Modality strengthening** | â€œMay/could/allegedâ€ becomes â€œis/did.â€ | â€œCould expose usersâ€ â†’ â€œexposes users.â€ | Typed modality lattice. |\n| **Attribution erasure** | Source claim becomes publication claim. | â€œMCP replaces fragmented integrationsâ€ instead of â€œAnthropic says it aims toâ€¦â€ | Attribution map required per sentence. |\n| **Quote distortion** | Quotation altered or context removed. | Pulling a phrase to imply a stronger claim. | Quote span verification; source context review. |\n| **Paraphrase drift** | Rewording changes meaning. | â€œSecurity principlesâ€ becomes â€œsecurity guarantees.â€ | Paraphrase entailment plus human review. |\n| **Causal overclaim** | Cause asserted from mere association or sequence. | â€œMCP caused new attacksâ€ from â€œresearchers identified risks.â€ | Discourse-relation license check. |\n| **Temporal overclaim** | Time scope broadened. | â€œThe latest spec saysâ€¦â€ from an older draft. | Versioned source records. |\n| **Entity conflation** | Distinct actors collapsed. | Anthropic, MCP maintainers, Linux Foundation, and implementers treated as one actor. | Entity resolution and role typing. |\n| **Contradiction smoothing** | Conflict removed for readability. | Company promise and security critique presented as harmonious. | Argumentation graph must expose attacks/undercuts. |\n| **Contested claim as settled** | Disputed proposition stated as fact. | â€œMCP is secureâ€ despite security critiques. | Claim status: supported/refuted/mixed/insufficient. |\n| **Source claim as fact** | A sourceâ€™s self-description becomes fact. | â€œOpen standardâ€ without independent standard-body context. | Source-type-aware attribution. |\n| **Correlation as causation** | Association becomes causal relation. | â€œAdoption increased because of security controlsâ€ without evidence. | Causal relation requires explicit support. |\n| **Isolated case as trend** | Single example generalized. | â€œMCP clients are vulnerableâ€ from one tested client. | Quantifier/scope checker. |\n| **Search-demand distortion** | SEO framing alters evidential emphasis. | â€œIs MCP dangerous?â€ headline when evidence supports narrower risks. | Distribution metadata separated from article assertions. |\n| **Editorial stance disguised as reported fact** | Opinion encoded as neutral report. | â€œMCP needs regulationâ€ in a news brief without attribution. | Stance labeling and genre contract. |\n| **Misleading headline** | Headline stronger than body. | â€œAI protocol exposes usersâ€ while article says â€œresearchers warn of possible risks.â€ | Headline-body entailment and pragmatic review. |\n| **Correction not propagated** | Old error remains in downstream artifacts. | Feed snippet still says â€œW3C standardâ€ after correction. | Correction propagation tests. |\n| **Provenance loss** | Claim no longer traceable. | Sentence has no source alignment. | Required claim alignment and PROV validation. |\n\nThe taxonomy is supported by NLG faithfulness literature, summarization consistency work, attribution-aware generation, fact-checking pipelines, and journalism correction/verification norms.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Worked example: agentic web technology\n\n### 6.1 Source records\n\nThe example uses Model Context Protocol, because it has a company announcement, a technical specification, and active security analysis.\n\n| Source | Relevant content |\n|---|---|\n| **S1: Company announcement** | Anthropic announced MCP in November 2024 as an open-source protocol for connecting AI assistants to systems where data lives, aiming to replace fragmented integrations with a standard protocol. Source: https://www.anthropic.com/news/model-context-protocol |\n| **S2: Technical specification** | The MCP specification describes an open protocol for integrating LLM applications with external data sources and tools, using hosts, clients, servers, JSON-RPC, resources, prompts, and tools; it also states security principles around consent, access controls, tool safety, and privacy. Source: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25 |\n| **S3: Critical/security analysis** | Security researchers have analyzed MCP risks including tool poisoning, prompt injection, capability attestation gaps, and trust propagation, while proposing mitigations such as metadata analysis, decision-path tracking, transparency, and other controls. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.22489 |\n\n### 6.2 Claim base\n\n| ID | Claim | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | Anthropic introduced MCP in November 2024. | Supported by S1. |\n| C2 | MCP is presented as an open protocol for connecting AI assistants or LLM applications to external data sources and tools. | Supported by S1/S2. |\n| C3 | MCP uses a host/client/server architecture. | Supported by S2. |\n| C4 | MCP uses JSON-RPC and exposes capabilities such as resources, prompts, and tools. | Supported by S2. |\n| C5 | The specification includes security principles around consent, access controls, privacy, and tool safety. | Supported by S2. |\n| C6 | The specificationâ€™s security principles require implementation choices; the protocol description itself is not equivalent to a security guarantee. | Inference from S2; must be marked as interpretation unless directly quoted from spec language. |\n| C7 | Security researchers report risks such as tool poisoning, prompt injection, and trust-propagation problems. | Supported by S3. |\n| C8 | These risks do not establish that every MCP deployment is unsafe. | Editorially important negative constraint; supported by scope of S3. |\n| C9 | MCP may become important infrastructure for agentic systems. | Plausible interpretation; requires attribution or evidence. |\n| C10 | Stronger governance/security controls may be needed. | Editorial judgment, not source fact. |\n\n### 6.3 Output A: wire brief\n\n**Prose**\n\n> Anthropic introduced the Model Context Protocol in November 2024 as an open protocol for connecting AI assistants to external data sources and tools. The current specification describes a host-client-server architecture using JSON-RPC and capabilities such as resources, prompts and tools. The specification also lists security principles including user consent, access controls, privacy and tool safety. Recent security analyses warn that MCP implementations can face risks such as tool poisoning and prompt-injection attacks. The available evidence supports the narrower claim that implementations need careful security controls; it does not show that all MCP deployments are unsafe.\n\n**Selected claims:** C1, C2, C3, C4, C5, C7, C8.  \n**Omitted claims:** C9 and C10, because a wire brief should avoid unneeded editorial projection.  \n**Discourse structure:** lede â†’ technical background â†’ security caveat â†’ scope limitation.  \n**Attribution/uncertainty:** â€œAnthropic introduced,â€ â€œspecification describes,â€ â€œsecurity analyses warn,â€ â€œdoes not show.â€  \n**Risk:** â€œcurrent specificationâ€ must be versioned in a real newsroom system.  \n**Licensed?** Yes, if â€œcurrentâ€ is bound to the retrieved specification version.\n\n### 6.4 Output B: explanatory article\n\n**Prose**\n\n> Model Context Protocol, or MCP, is a proposed common way for AI applications to connect with external data sources and tools. Anthropic introduced it in 2024, and the specification describes a system in which a host application connects through clients to MCP servers. Those servers can expose resources, prompts and tools over JSON-RPC.  \n>\n> The appeal is interoperability. Instead of each AI application building a separate connector for every database, repository or business tool, MCP offers a shared pattern for exposing capabilities to an AI system.  \n>\n> The same design also creates security questions. A tool interface can become a path for prompt injection, excessive permissions, misleading tool descriptions or other forms of trust abuse. The MCP specification includes security principles such as consent and access controls, but those principles still have to be implemented by hosts, clients and servers.  \n>\n> The safest summary is therefore mixed: MCP addresses a real integration problem, but the evidence does not justify describing it as either a security solution or a security failure. It is an integration protocol whose safety depends heavily on implementation, review and governance.\n\n**Selected claims:** C1â€“C8.  \n**Introduced background:** â€œinteroperabilityâ€ and â€œshared patternâ€ as explanatory framing, licensed by S1/S2 but still interpretive.  \n**Discourse structure:** definition â†’ motivation â†’ risk â†’ balanced synthesis.  \n**Risk:** The final paragraph is an interpretive synthesis; it should be labeled as analysis if published outside a straight explainer.  \n**Licensed?** Mostly yes, provided claim C6 is retained as interpretation rather than specification fact.\n\n### 6.5 Output C: editorial / critical article\n\n**Prose**\n\n> MCP should be treated as security-sensitive infrastructure, not as neutral plumbing. The protocolâ€™s goal is attractive: a common way for AI applications to reach tools and data sources. But a common interface also gives attackers a common target.  \n>\n> The company announcement and the specification frame MCP around interoperability. Security analyses complicate that framing by identifying risks such as tool poisoning, prompt injection and implicit trust propagation. Those risks do not prove that MCP is unsafe in every deployment, but they do show that implementation choices, permission boundaries and tool metadata deserve more scrutiny than a normal developer-integration story would suggest.  \n>\n> The editorial judgment is this: publications should avoid describing MCP as a â€œsafe standardâ€ unless they can separately verify the implementation, threat model and governance controls in question.\n\n**Selected claims:** C1, C2, C5, C7, C8.  \n**Introduced editorial claims:** â€œsecurity-sensitive infrastructure,â€ â€œnot neutral plumbing,â€ â€œdeserve more scrutiny.â€  \n**Discourse structure:** thesis â†’ evidence contrast â†’ policy/editorial recommendation.  \n**Risk:** Could overstate security risk if headline says â€œMCP is dangerous.â€  \n**Licensed?** Yes only as an explicitly labeled editorial/analysis piece. Not licensed as a neutral news brief.\n\n### 6.6 Output D: correction / update notice\n\n**Prose**\n\n> **Correction, June 10, 2026:** An earlier version described Model Context Protocol as a â€œW3C standardâ€ and said it â€œguarantees safe tool use.â€ That was inaccurate. The sources reviewed describe MCP as an open protocol introduced by Anthropic and specified by the MCP project; they do not establish W3C standard status. The specification includes security principles, but those principles do not by themselves guarantee safe implementations. The headline and two body sentences have been revised to restore attribution and add the missing security qualification.\n\n**Claims selected:** C1, C2, C5, C6, C8.  \n**Corrected false claims:** â€œW3C standard,â€ â€œguarantees safe tool use.â€  \n**Discourse structure:** error â†’ correction â†’ reason â†’ propagation notice.  \n**Risk:** The correction must propagate to headline, social card, feed item, schema metadata, and internal claim store.  \n**Licensed?** Yes, assuming the earlier error occurred.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evaluation methods\n\nA claims-to-prose system needs multiple evaluation layers.\n\n| Layer | What can be checked automatically | What needs human review |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Claim extraction** | Extract propositions from generated prose and map them to source claims. | Whether the extracted claim captures pragmatic force. |\n| **Source alignment** | Each sentence has source IDs, claim IDs, attribution, and modality. | Whether the chosen source mix is fair. |\n| **Factual consistency** | NLI, QA-based checks, AIS-style attribution, contradiction detection. | Whether entailment misses presupposition, implicature, or framing. |\n| **Citation verification** | Cited source contains support span; citation is not decorative. | Whether the source is authoritative enough. |\n| **Provenance validation** | PROV-O/SHACL/RDF graph constraints; version checks. | Whether provenance is editorially sufficient. |\n| **Discourse validation** | Check that causal/contrast/concession relations have licensed relation records. | Whether framing is fair. |\n| **Correction propagation** | Regression tests ensure corrected claims do not reappear in snippets, summaries, feeds, or schema. | Whether correction wording is adequate. |\n| **Adversarial testing** | Try contradictory sources, missing qualifiers, misleading headlines, SEO pressure, stale retractions. | Whether the system behaves responsibly in novel cases. |\n| **Readability/usefulness** | Basic readability, coverage, redundancy, genre fit. | Whether article serves the audience without distortion. |\n| **Editorial gate** | Checklist completion. | Final publication decision. |\n\nExisting benchmarks help but do not solve the newsroom case. WebNLG, DART, ToTTo, and E2E test structured-data-to-text generation. FEVER and AVeriTeC test claim verification. SummaC, FactCC, QAGS, and AIS test factuality or attribution.\n\nAn agentic newsroom would need a new benchmark with:\n\n- source records, not just clean facts;\n- versioned claims and corrections;\n- conflicting evidence;\n- required genre outputs;\n- sentence-level claim alignment;\n- headline and metadata artifacts;\n- provenance graphs;\n- human editorial judgments;\n- adversarial examples for modality, attribution, contradiction, and stance.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Implementation sketch\n\nA minimal architecture should not start with category theory. It should start with auditability.\n\n### 8.1 Components\n\n1. **Source store**  \n   Stores announcements, papers, interviews, legal records, datasets, corrections, retractions, and standards documents.\n\n2. **Claim store**  \n   Normalizes source statements into claim records with modality, attribution, scope, time, and status.\n\n3. **Provenance graph**  \n   Links claims to source spans, derivations, versions, corrections, and downstream prose artifacts.\n\n4. **Argumentation layer**  \n   Represents support, attack, undercutting, contradiction, defeaters, and unresolved disagreement.\n\n5. **Editorial status model**  \n   Tracks draft, verified, contested, corrected, retracted, embargoed, opinion, analysis, sponsored, or unsuitable-for-ad contexts.\n\n6. **Genre/discourse planner**  \n   Produces wire brief, explainer, editorial, liveblog, correction, headline, social summary, or metadata plan.\n\n7. **Sentence planner**  \n   Creates sentence-level obligations: claim IDs, attribution phrase, modality, discourse relation, source span, and risk flags.\n\n8. **Realization engine**  \n   Could be template-based, rule-based, neural, or hybrid. For high-risk domains, templates and constrained decoding should dominate.\n\n9. **Attribution and stance tracker**  \n   Ensures â€œsource says,â€ â€œevidence suggests,â€ â€œpublication concludes,â€ and â€œeditorial arguesâ€ remain distinct.\n\n10. **Generated-prose claim extractor**  \n    Re-extracts claims from output and compares them with planned claims.\n\n11. **Faithfulness checker**  \n    Uses entailment, QA, citation checking, source-span matching, modality checks, contradiction checks, and human review routing.\n\n12. **Correction propagator**  \n    Finds all dependent artifacts when a source or claim changes.\n\n13. **Standards emitter**  \n    Emits Schema.org, NewsML-G2/ninjs, PROV-O, C2PA, feeds, and sitemaps where appropriate.\n\n14. **Editorial gate evaluator**  \n    Requires checklist completion before publication.\n\n### 8.2 TypeScript-like interfaces\n\n```ts\ntype ID = string;\n\ntype Modality =\n  | \"certain\"\n  | \"probable\"\n  | \"possible\"\n  | \"alleged\"\n  | \"reported\"\n  | \"disputed\"\n  | \"unknown\";\n\ntype ClaimStatus =\n  | \"supported\"\n  | \"refuted\"\n  | \"mixed\"\n  | \"insufficient_evidence\"\n  | \"corrected\"\n  | \"retracted\"\n  | \"editorial_judgment\";\n\ntype SourceKind =\n  | \"company_announcement\"\n  | \"technical_standard\"\n  | \"research_paper\"\n  | \"legal_record\"\n  | \"interview\"\n  | \"dataset\"\n  | \"news_report\"\n  | \"correction\"\n  | \"retraction\";\n\ninterface SourceRecord {\n  id: ID;\n  kind: SourceKind;\n  title: string;\n  publisher: string;\n  publishedAt?: string;\n  retrievedAt: string;\n  version?: string;\n  url?: string;\n  provenance: ProvenanceNode[];\n  reliabilityNotes?: string[];\n}\n\ninterface SourceStatement {\n  id: ID;\n  sourceId: ID;\n  spanRef?: string;\n  text: string;\n  speaker?: string;\n  assertedAt?: string;\n}\n\ninterface Claim {\n  id: ID;\n  proposition: string;\n  sourceStatements: ID[];\n  modality: Modality;\n  status: ClaimStatus;\n  attribution?: string;\n  temporalScope?: string;\n  entities: string[];\n  support: Evidence[];\n  attacks: Evidence[];\n  qualifiers: string[];\n  correctionState?: \"current\" | \"superseded\" | \"corrected\" | \"retracted\";\n}\n\ninterface Evidence {\n  id: ID;\n  sourceId: ID;\n  claimId: ID;\n  relation: \"supports\" | \"refutes\" | \"qualifies\" | \"undercuts\";\n  strength?: \"weak\" | \"moderate\" | \"strong\";\n  notes?: string;\n}\n\ninterface EditorialStatus {\n  genre: \"wire\" | \"explainer\" | \"analysis\" | \"editorial\" | \"correction\";\n  stance: \"neutral_report\" | \"analysis\" | \"opinion\";\n  audience?: string;\n  publicationSurface: \"article\" | \"headline\" | \"feed\" | \"social\" | \"schema\";\n  riskLevel: \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n}\n\ninterface DiscourseRelation {\n  type:\n    | \"background\"\n    | \"elaboration\"\n    | \"evidence\"\n    | \"contrast\"\n    | \"concession\"\n    | \"cause\"\n    | \"consequence\"\n    | \"correction\";\n  licensedBy: ID[];\n}\n\ninterface SentencePlan {\n  id: ID;\n  realizesClaims: ID[];\n  sourceIds: ID[];\n  requiredAttribution?: string;\n  requiredModality: Modality;\n  discourseRelation?: DiscourseRelation;\n  introducedBackgroundClaims?: ID[];\n  riskFlags: string[];\n}\n\ninterface ProseArtifact {\n  id: ID;\n  genre: EditorialStatus[\"genre\"];\n  title?: string;\n  body: string;\n  sentencePlans: SentencePlan[];\n  metadataArtifacts: ID[];\n  createdAt: string;\n}\n\ninterface ClaimAlignment {\n  sentenceId: ID;\n  claimIds: ID[];\n  sourceSpanRefs: string[];\n  assertionStrength: Modality;\n  attributionPreserved: boolean;\n}\n\ninterface FaithfulnessReport {\n  artifactId: ID;\n  unsupportedClaims: string[];\n  modalityStrengthening: string[];\n  attributionLosses: string[];\n  contradictionSmoothing: string[];\n  correctionPropagationFailures: string[];\n  requiresHumanReview: boolean;\n}\n\ninterface CorrectionPlan {\n  correctedClaimIds: ID[];\n  affectedArtifacts: ID[];\n  affectedMetadata: ID[];\n  requiredNotices: string[];\n}\n```\n\nThe key design principle: prose generation should produce **sentence plans first**, not free text first. Free text should be checked against the plan and the source graph.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Is â€œfiber algebra of claims to proseâ€ justified?\n\n### 9.1 No: existing NLG/discourse theory is sufficient\n\nThis is partly true. NLG, discourse planning, argumentation, provenance, semantics, factuality evaluation, and journalism standards already provide most necessary concepts. Calling the whole system â€œfiber algebraâ€ risks obscuring established terms.\n\n### 9.2 Partially: fiber language helps model context variation\n\nThis is plausible. Different publication contexts license different prose:\n\n- wire brief;\n- explainer;\n- editorial;\n- headline;\n- correction;\n- social summary;\n- schema metadata;\n- search snippet;\n- ad-sensitive surface.\n\nA fibered or indexed view could model each context as having a family of allowed realizations and contracts.\n\n### 9.3 Yes, restricted: indexed licensed realizations over editorial contexts\n\nThis is the best-supported answer.\n\nA restricted formalization might be:\n\n- base objects: editorial contexts, genres, surfaces, audiences, legal/risk states;\n- fiber over a context: licensed discourse and sentence plans for a claim base in that context;\n- reindexing: changing from explainer to headline, article to feed, pre-correction to post-correction;\n- algebraic operations: select, aggregate, hedge, attribute, contrast, quote, correct, summarize;\n- invariants: no unsupported assertion, no modality strengthening, provenance retained, correction propagated, stance marked.\n\nThis could clarify **families of licensed outputs**, but it does not generate prose by itself.\n\n### 9.4 Yes, strong: genuine algebraic/fibered formalism gives new invariants\n\nThe literature reviewed here does not justify the strong claim. Category-theoretic and sheaf-theoretic work exists in data integration and local-to-global consistency, including functorial data migration and applied sheaf models. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.1166 But there is not enough evidence that a â€œclaims-to-prose fiber algebraâ€ provides new computable checks beyond existing provenance graphs, type contracts, argumentation frameworks, discourse planning, and validation constraints.\n\n### Final verdict\n\n**Restricted yes, but not strong yes.**\n\nâ€œClaims-to-prose fiber algebraâ€ is a useful name only if it means:\n\n> an indexed family of licensed prose/discourse realizations over editorial contexts, with preservation laws for attribution, modality, provenance, contradiction, correction state, and stance.\n\nIt is misleading if it means:\n\n> a complete formal theory of how claims become prose, a deterministic function from evidence to article, or a replacement for NLG, discourse theory, semantics, journalism practice, and human editorial review.\n\nThe practical name I would use in a research or engineering setting is:\n\n> **attribution-preserving, provenance-aware, discourse-planned data-to-text generation for editorial artifacts.**\n\nThe shorter technical name could be:\n\n> **licensed claim-to-prose realization.**\n\n---\n\n## 10. Annotated bibliography\n\n### Natural language generation and data-to-text\n\n- **Reiter & Dale, â€œBuilding applied natural language generation systems.â€** Classic account of applied NLG tasks including content determination, discourse planning, aggregation, lexicalization, referring-expression generation, and realization. Source: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/natural-language-engineering/article/building-applied-natural-language-generation-systems/FEB374A3FF652F06D8567A6FAB2EF36E\n- **Reiter & Dale, *Building Natural Language Generation Systems*.** Standard book-length treatment of document planning, microplanning, and surface realization. Source: https://books.google.com/books/about/Building_Natural_Language_Generation_Sys.html?id=qnWQU9C8bDkC\n- **WebNLG Challenge.** RDF-to-text benchmark focused on lexicalization, aggregation, referring expressions, and realization from triples. Source: https://aclanthology.org/W17-3518/\n- **DART.** Open-domain structured-data-to-text dataset. Useful for claim/table/record-to-text work but not sufficient for provenance-heavy journalism. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.37/\n- **ToTTo.** Controlled table-to-text benchmark; important because it explicitly identifies hallucination of unsupported phrases in structured-data generation. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.89/\n- **Ferreira et al., pipeline vs. end-to-end RDF-to-text.** Evidence that explicit intermediate representations can improve generalization and reduce hallucination. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09022\n\n### Discourse, semantics, and pragmatics\n\n- **Mann & Thompson, Rhetorical Structure Theory.** Foundational model of discourse relations among text spans. Source: https://www.sfu.ca/rst/05bibliographies/bibs/Mann_Thompson_1988.pdf\n- **RST and computational text planning work.** Shows how discourse relations can support text planning. Source: https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-009-3645-4_7\n- **Centering theory.** Relevant to coherence and reference tracking, though limited for full editorial reasoning. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14678\n- **Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Presupposition.** Useful for understanding hidden commitments in prose and headlines. Source: https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2014/entries/presupposition/\n- **Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Implicature.** Useful for distinguishing literal assertion from suggested meaning. Source: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/implicature/\n- **Evidentiality research.** Relevant to marking information source and strength. Source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1890020/\n\n### Argumentation, evidence, and contradiction\n\n- **Dung abstract argumentation frameworks.** Foundational graph model of arguments and attacks. Source: https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/33651/35806\n- **Structured computational argumentation surveys.** Relevant to premises, conclusions, strict and defeasible inference. Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2401.03454v1\n- **Defeasible reasoning.** Important for claims that can be overturned by later evidence. Source: https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/reasoning-defeasible/\n- **Paraconsistent logic.** Relevant for preserving contradiction without explosion. Source: https://iep.utm.edu/para-log/\n- **Provenance semirings.** Formal work on retaining derivation/source information in query results. Source: https://web.cs.ucdavis.edu/~green/papers/pods07.pdf\n- **FEVER.** Claim verification dataset with supported/refuted/not-enough-information labels and evidence. Source: https://aclanthology.org/N18-1074/\n- **AVeriTeC.** Fact-checking benchmark with labels including conflicting evidence and cherry-picking. Source: https://fever.ai/dataset/averitec.html\n\n### Journalism and computational journalism\n\n- **SPJ Code of Ethics.** Verification, source clarity, context, and correction duties. Source: https://www.spj.org/spj-code-of-ethics/\n- **Reuters Trust Principles and AI guidance.** Integrity, independence, freedom from bias, human input, and disclosure norms. Source: https://www.thomsonreuters.com/en/about-us/trust-principles\n- **AP generative AI standards.** Treats AI output as unvetted source material requiring editorial judgment and sourcing. Source: https://www.ap.org/the-definitive-source/behind-the-news/standards-around-generative-ai/\n- **Automated journalism surveys.** Useful background on template, pipeline, and neural approaches to news automation. Source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445913/\n\n### Faithfulness, factuality, and hallucination\n\n- **Faithfulness in NLG survey.** Frames unfaithful generation as a central NLG problem. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05227\n- **Hallucination in NLG survey.** Broad taxonomy of hallucination across generation tasks. Source: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3571730\n- **FactCC.** Factual consistency checking for abstractive summarization. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.750/\n- **QAGS.** QA-based factual consistency evaluation. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.450/\n- **SummaC.** NLI-based summarization consistency benchmark and method. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2022.tacl-1.10/\n- **Attributable to Identified Sources.** Directly relevant to source-grounded generation and attribution-preserving prose. Source: https://aclanthology.org/2023.cl-4.2/\n\n### Standards and metadata\n\n- **Schema.org `NewsArticle` and `ClaimReview`.** Publication and fact-check metadata, not full claim-to-prose alignment. Sources: https://schema.org/NewsArticle and https://schema.org/ClaimReview\n- **IPTC NewsML-G2, ninjs, and Media Topics.** News exchange and classification standards. Source: https://iptc.org/standards/newsml-g2/\n- **W3C PROV-O.** Provenance representation. Source: https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/\n- **RDF and SHACL.** Graph representation and validation constraints. Sources: https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/ and https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/\n- **C2PA Content Credentials.** Content provenance infrastructure; useful but not a complete claim-veracity model. Source: https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.4/index.html\n- **ActivityPub/ActivityStreams, Atom, JSON Feed, Google article metadata.** Distribution and discovery standards, not evidence-preservation systems. Source: https://www.w3.org/TR/activitypub/\n\n### Category-theoretic and sheaf-adjacent work\n\n- **Spivak, functorial data migration.** Shows how category theory can model schemas and structure-preserving data migration. Relevant by analogy to provenance and context-indexed artifacts, not directly to prose generation. Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.1166\n- **Applied sheaf theory for data integration.** Useful for local-to-global consistency and incompatible local evidence contexts; less directly useful for surface prose. Source: https://people.cs.uchicago.edu/~ericschmid/schmid-applied-sheaf-theory.pdf\n- **Exact search for â€œclaims-to-prose fiber algebra.â€** No established literature result was found under that phrase; search results concerned unrelated mathematical uses of fibers and fiber algebras. Example: https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/2506909/what-is-the-original-source-of-fiber-in-mathematics\n"},{"sourceId":"prior-art/accountable-attention","digest":"sha256:97b285a974b8a51b283c49678f1a1a26f80c11c68f53fad821e2d191c38c5c61","content":"---\ntitle: Against Clickbait, For Accountable Attention\ndescription: A memo arguing clickbait's wrongness is unaccountable compression, an attention surface that compresses a complex claim into a more clickable version while deleting the conditions that make the claim warranted. Proposes accountable attention design that maximizes curiosity and salience subject to truthfulness, proportionality, uncertainty preservation, source preservation, correction propagation, and visible provenance metadata, under the slogan do not abolish hooks, make hooks answerable.\ntags: [clickbait, accountable-attention, warrant, correction, provenance, headline-ethics, attention-design, uncertainty-preservation]\n---\n\n# Against Clickbait, For Accountable Attention  \n**Research memo â€” June 2026**\n\n## Abstract\n\nâ€œClickbaitâ€ is too small a word for the system it names. The problem is not merely bad headlines. It is a production economy in which attention surfacesâ€”headlines, thumbnails, social cards, push alerts, search snippets, newsletter subject lines, RSS summaries, ActivityPub postsâ€”are optimized for measurable reaction while the warrant for the underlying claim is stripped away. That system includes precarious writing labor, content mills, SEO operations, search and recommendation algorithms, programmatic advertising, affiliate arbitrage, syndication, plagiarism, CMS templates, AI generation, and weak correction infrastructure.\n\nThe constructive claim of this memo is that attention design **can** be attractive, even spicy, without becoming clickbait in the pejorative sense. But it cannot be governed by raw CTR alone. The ethical replacement is **accountable attention design**: maximize curiosity and salience subject to truthfulness, proportionality, uncertainty preservation, source preservation, correction propagation, and visible commercial/provenance metadata.\n\nThe slogan is: **do not abolish hooks; make hooks answerable.**\n\n---\n\n## Core thesis\n\nClickbaitâ€™s wrongness is not â€œbeing interesting.â€ Public-interest media needs attraction. Service journalism needs packaging. Emergency alerts need urgency. Investigations need compelling framing. The problem is **unaccountable compression**: an attention surface compresses a complex claim into a more clickable version while deleting the conditions that make the claim warranted.\n\nA headline becomes unethical when it converts:\n\n- uncertainty into certainty;\n- allegation into fact;\n- correlation into causation;\n- anecdote into trend;\n- paid/sponsored interest into editorial judgment;\n- low-stakes curiosity into high-stakes panic;\n- source-bound knowledge into contextless assertion;\n- public-interest attention into ad-arbitrage inventory.\n\nThe redeemable alternative is **warrant-preserving attention design**: every teaser should remain attractive while preserving enough of the claimâ€™s evidence, scope, uncertainty, source, and update status that the user is not tricked into a false epistemic posture.\n\n---\n\n## Terminology: separating adjacent systems\n\n| Term | Main unit | What it is | Typical failure mode | Not identical to |\n|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| **Clickbait** | Headline, teaser, thumbnail, card, alert | A rhetorical/packaging tactic that exploits curiosity, emotion, ambiguity, suspense, or surprise to motivate a click. Recent scholarship often treats curiosity-gap omission as central. ([ojcmt.net](https://www.ojcmt.net/article/clickbait-research-challenges-and-opportunities-a-systematic-literature-review-15267)) | Deception, disappointment, overclaiming, affective manipulation, missing payoff. | Content farms, MFA, junk news. |\n| **Content farm** | Organization/site network | A production organization designed to generate large volumes of low-cost content against search, social, or ad demand. Historically: algorithmically chosen topics, freelance churn, SEO targeting. ([wired.com](https://www.wired.com/2011/02/google-clamp-down-content-factories?utm_source=chatgpt.com)) | Volume over care; duplication; thin articles; search pollution. | A labor arrangement alone. |\n| **Content mill** | Labor arrangement | Piece-rate or low-paid writing/editing/translation work, often outsourced or freelance, sometimes producing SEO or affiliate content. | Exploits precarity; separates writers from editorial judgment; rewards speed over verification. | Necessarily fraud; many workers are simply under bad incentives. |\n| **MFA site** | Advertising/arbitrage infrastructure | â€œMade-for-advertisingâ€ sites whose practical purpose is to harvest ad impressions, often with high ad density, paid traffic, refresh loops, duplicated/automated/AI content. DoubleVerify cites ANA findings that MFA represented a large share of programmatic impressions and spend. ([doubleverify.com](https://doubleverify.com/company/newsroom/doubleverify-to-protect-advertisers-against-made-for-advertising-content-with-industry-first-holistic-solution)) | Turns content into a thin wrapper around ad inventory. | All ad-supported media. High ad density alone is not sufficient. |\n| **SEO spam** | Search manipulation | Tactics aimed at manipulating search ranking or search systems rather than serving users. Googleâ€™s spam policies include deceptive practices and now explicitly reference attempts to manipulate generative AI responses in Search. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies)) | Search-result pollution; doorway pages; cloaking; scaled abuse. | Legitimate search optimization. |\n| **Junk news** | News-like publishing | Low-quality, unreliable, misleading, or hyperpartisan news-like output, often with weak sourcing and strong affective style. | Confuses news cues with evidence; gains legitimacy by format mimicry. | Satire, partisan journalism, tabloids per se. |\n| **AI slop** | Synthetic content stream | Mass synthetic low-quality text/images/video, often generic, derivative, unverifiable, or nonsensical, produced cheaply by generative models. NewsGuard reported thousands of AI content-farm news/information sites by March 2026. ([newsguardtech.com](https://www.newsguardtech.com/special-reports/ai-tracking-center)) | Scale, hallucination, laundering, weak accountability, displacement of editorial labor. | Any AI-assisted journalism. |\n| **Disinformation-for-profit** | Political attention arbitrage | False or misleading politically consequential content monetized through ads, influencers, affiliate schemes, donations, or list-building. | Converts democratic disorder into revenue. Research frames disinformation as a market outcome, not merely a platform accident. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/14614448231207644)) | Ideological propaganda alone. |\n| **Platform gaming** | General incentive response | Any optimization of content, metadata, posting cadence, format, or distribution to exploit platform ranking systems. | Incentive capture: creators adapt to what platforms measure, not what publics need. | Always malicious behavior; all media systems invite adaptation. |\n\n---\n\n## The production system: not â€œbad headlines,â€ but an attention economy stack\n\n### 1. Labor and production\n\nThe low-quality information economy depends on cheap, flexible labor: freelancers, editors, translators, contractors, moderators, SEO writers, newsletter operators, thumbnail designers, social media managers, and increasingly AI â€œoperatorsâ€ who prompt, rewrite, summarize, or lightly humanize machine output. This is not a simple villain story. Many workers are precarious professionals trying to survive in a degraded market.\n\nResearch on freelance journalism shows structural insecurity around income, social protections, housing, childcare, and dependence on work outside journalism or sponsored/native content. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/20594364241230434)) Content mills and farms exploit this condition by turning editorial judgment into piece-rate throughput: produce fast, fit the template, hit the keyword, answer the â€œPeople also askâ€ box, keep the client happy.\n\nAI intensifies this system by making draft generation cheap while pushing human labor toward lower-status cleanup: fact-checking after the fact, rewriting slop, adding â€œvoice,â€ producing variants, or managing publication pipelines. A 2025 audit of U.S. newspapers estimated nontrivial AI use in local journalism and found disclosure uneven and rare, which suggests that synthetic production is moving faster than public accountability norms. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18774?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n### 2. Discovery and ranking\n\nSearch engines, social feeds, recommendation systems, email inboxes, notification trays, and browser start pages all convert editorial work into ranked surfaces. This shifts power from the article to the preview. The headline, thumbnail, snippet, and card become the economically decisive object.\n\nGoogle Discoverâ€™s policy explicitly warns against misleading or exaggerated preview content, including titles, snippets, and images that withhold crucial information or sensationalize outrage, titillation, or morbid curiosity. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/google-discover)) YouTubeâ€™s spam policy similarly prohibits content, metadata, or behavior designed to mislead users or manipulate platform engagement. ([support.google.com](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2801973?hl=en)) These policies matter because platform surfaces are where â€œthe articleâ€ becomes a behavioral prompt.\n\nThe risk is amplified by headline-only circulation. A large study of public Facebook posts found that sharing without clicking was commonâ€”around three quarters of forwarded linksâ€”suggesting that many users act on headlines and blurbs rather than articles. ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39562798/)) If the preview is epistemically distorted, the distortion can travel farther than the evidence.\n\n### 3. Monetization and arbitrage\n\nProgrammatic advertising makes it possible to monetize attention at scale even when the publisher has little durable audience relationship. MFA sites exploit this by buying traffic, producing cheap content, maximizing ad density, and routing human attention through pages designed less for reading than for impression generation. DoubleVerify describes MFA traits such as high ad density, paid traffic dependence, refresh behavior, endless scroll or click loops, and duplicated or automated content, while also warning that no single trait alone should define MFA. ([doubleverify.com](https://doubleverify.com/company/newsroom/doubleverify-to-protect-advertisers-against-made-for-advertising-content-with-industry-first-holistic-solution))\n\nAd-tech transparency systemsâ€”ads.txt, sellers.json, and the OpenRTB SupplyChain objectâ€”try to reveal who is authorized to sell inventory and which intermediaries participate in a bid request. ([iabtechlab.com](https://iabtechlab.com/sellers-json/)) But the incentive remains: if a page can cheaply attract a human impression, the ad market may reward it before any human institution evaluates its quality.\n\n### 4. Syndication, plagiarism, and metadata loss\n\nContent circulates through RSS, social cards, embeds, newsletters, ActivityPub posts, push notifications, search snippets, screenshot memes, and scraped summaries. Each hop can lose context. Open Graph metadata can specify title, description, image, and canonical object for rich social display; RSS supports channel/item syndication and extension through namespaces; ActivityPub supports decentralized creation, updating, deleting, and delivery of objects. ([ogp.me](https://ogp.me/))\n\nThe problem is that most preview systems carry **display metadata** better than **warrant metadata**. They know title, image, summary, and URL. They usually do not know: What claim is being made? Who supports it? Is it alleged, confirmed, estimated, modeled, satirical, sponsored, corrected, or retracted? Was the thumbnail captured at the relevant time and place? Was AI involved? Has the teaser been updated everywhere it traveled?\n\n---\n\n## What exactly is wrong with clickbait?\n\nClickbait has several distinct wrongs. Confusing them makes the debate too blunt.\n\n| Wrong | Description | Example failure | Ethical repair |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Deception** | The teaser asserts or strongly implies something false. | â€œDoctor banned after miracle cureâ€ when no ban occurred. | Entailment check: every claim atom must be supported by the article and sources. |\n| **Disappointment** | The teaser promises a payoff the article does not deliver. | â€œYou wonâ€™t believe what happened nextâ€ followed by generic filler. | Payoff rule: answer the promised question early and specifically. |\n| **Warrant deletion** | The teaser removes the evidence relation. | â€œCity admits water is unsafeâ€ when only one council member alleged it. | Preserve source: â€œCouncil member allegesâ€¦â€ |\n| **Uncertainty deletion** | The teaser upgrades a tentative claim. | â€œCoffee prevents dementiaâ€ from a small observational study. | Preserve modality: â€œStudy finds an association; causation unknown.â€ |\n| **Scope inflation** | A narrow case becomes a general trend. | â€œTeachers are quitting en masseâ€ from three anecdotes. | State scope: â€œIn one districtâ€¦â€ |\n| **Affective manipulation** | The teaser uses fear, outrage, disgust, or sexualization beyond the public value of the story. | Disaster thumbnail for an unrelated policy article. | Proportionality test: affect must match evidence and stakes. |\n| **Incentive corruption** | The teaser exists mainly to maximize ad impressions, not to inform. | MFA article split into 20 pages with paid-traffic loops. | Evaluate satisfied, informed attentionâ€”not raw clicks. |\n| **Correction failure** | False previews persist after article correction. | Old social card keeps circulating after retraction. | Versioned cards, correction propagation, cache refresh, retraction flags. |\n\nThe deepest wrong is not â€œcuriosity.â€ It is **misdirected epistemic attention**: the user is made to allocate time, emotion, trust, or sharing behavior under a distorted representation of what is known.\n\n---\n\n## Can a headline be spicy without lying?\n\nYes. A headline can be vivid, funny, urgent, elegant, suspenseful, or provocative while remaining warranted. â€œSpicyâ€ becomes unethical when it requires deleting the conditions of knowledge.\n\nA useful distinction:\n\n| Bad clickbait | Ethical attention design |\n|---|---|\n| â€œThis common food is killing youâ€ | â€œThe evidence on processed meat risk is stronger than it was ten years agoâ€”but still easy to overstate.â€ |\n| â€œPolitician caught in shocking scandalâ€ | â€œAudit says the mayorâ€™s office broke contracting rules; mayor disputes the finding.â€ |\n| â€œScientists finally prove aliens existâ€ | â€œA telescope signal is interesting enough for follow-up, not enough for an alien claim.â€ |\n| â€œYouâ€™re charging your phone wrongâ€ | â€œThree charging habits can age your battery faster.â€ |\n| â€œDonâ€™t leave home before reading thisâ€ | â€œFlash-flood warning until 6 p.m.; avoid the Route 18 underpass.â€ |\n\nThe ethical headline does not have to be boring. It can still create a curiosity gap. But the gap should concern **explanation**, not **basic truth conditions**. Users may click to learn why, how, what evidence, what exceptions, what to do next. They should not have to click merely to discover that the headlineâ€™s apparent claim was exaggerated.\n\n---\n\n## Tabloid history and the ambivalence of attention\n\nTabloid style shows that popular packaging is not inherently illegitimate. Tabloids historically used compression, sensationalism, crime, scandal, photography, vernacular voice, and working-class accessibility. Some of that tradition democratized readability; some exploited fear, spectacle, misogyny, racism, class contempt, or crime panic. The lesson is not â€œtabloid bad, broadsheet good.â€ It is that **popular address and evidentiary accountability are separable**.\n\nService journalism also depends on strong packaging: â€œwho qualifies,â€ â€œwhat changes tomorrow,â€ â€œhow to apply,â€ â€œwhat this means for your bill.â€ Constructive journalism research suggests that adding solutions, nuance, and matter-of-fact framing can empower audiences and sometimes sustain engagement without becoming fluff, though effects on trust and attention remain mixed. ([constructiveinstitute.org](https://constructiveinstitute.org/research-overview-effects/))\n\nSo the ethical project is not anti-popular. It is anti-unaccountable.\n\n---\n\n## The constructive alternative: accountable attention design\n\n**Definition:** Accountable attention design is the design of headlines, thumbnails, teasers, cards, snippets, alerts, and summaries that maximizes legitimate audience interest while preserving the warrant, uncertainty, provenance, scope, and correction status of the underlying content.\n\nIt has five principles.\n\n### 1. Entailment\n\nEvery factual implication in the attention surface must be entailed by the article and its cited evidence. If the article says â€œX alleges Y,â€ the headline cannot say â€œY happened.â€ If the study shows association, the headline cannot imply causation.\n\nThis is especially important for automated headline generation, where recent NLP research treats headline hallucinationâ€”headlines not supported by their sourcesâ€”as a concrete detection problem. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05852))\n\n### 2. Warrant preservation\n\nThe teaser should preserve the source relation when that relation matters.\n\nBad: â€œCity water unsafe.â€  \nBetter: â€œInternal memo warned of water-test failures.â€  \nBetter still, when contested: â€œInternal memo warned of water-test failures; city says supply remains safe.â€\n\n### 3. Uncertainty preservation\n\nA headline must not silently upgrade:\n\n- â€œmayâ€ to â€œdoesâ€;\n- â€œrisk factorâ€ to â€œcauseâ€;\n- â€œearly evidenceâ€ to â€œproofâ€;\n- â€œpreprintâ€ to â€œstudy provesâ€;\n- â€œallegedâ€ to â€œcaughtâ€;\n- â€œmodeled estimateâ€ to â€œmeasured fact.â€\n\n### 4. Proportionality\n\nThe emotional intensity of the surface should match the public stakes and evidentiary strength. A public-safety alert may be urgent. A minor product rumor should not mimic emergency language. A thumbnail should not import unrelated disaster imagery to intensify a weak story.\n\n### 5. Correction propagation\n\nA teaser is not a disposable wrapper. It is part of the publication. If the article is corrected, retracted, substantially updated, or legally challenged, the headline, social card, RSS summary, push alert landing page, cached snippets, and syndicated copies should have update paths.\n\n---\n\n## Comparison table: from clickbait to accountable attention\n\n| Surface | Common clickbait form | Accountable form | Metadata needed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Headline** | â€œDoctors hate this simple trickâ€ | â€œA small trial found modest benefits from X; doctors warn evidence is early.â€ | Claim type, evidence type, uncertainty, source. |\n| **Thumbnail** | Shocked face, red arrow, unrelated image | Relevant image or document excerpt; synthetic/illustrative status disclosed. | Image origin, date/place, synthetic flag, rights/provenance. |\n| **Social card** | Strong claim with no source relation | Source-preserving summary: â€œCourt filing allegesâ€¦â€ | Canonical URL, version, claim atoms, correction status. |\n| **Push alert** | â€œYou need to see this nowâ€ | â€œTornado warning until 7:15 p.m. for X County.â€ | Severity, location, expiry, source agency. |\n| **Newsletter subject** | â€œThe scandal no one is talking aboutâ€ | â€œWhy the X audit mattersâ€”and what it does not prove.â€ | Topic, claim scope, evidence category. |\n| **Search snippet** | Keyword-stuffed answer bait | Concise answer plus limitations. | Last updated, author/source, structured claim. |\n| **RSS summary** | Truncated suspense | Complete enough summary to decide whether to read. | Stable GUID, update timestamp, correction flag. |\n| **ActivityPub post** | Viral claim detached from update trail | Post object with preview, source, update/delete propagation. | Activity ID, preview attachment, update status. |\n\n---\n\n## Worked examples\n\n### Example 1: health study\n\n**Unaccountable clickbait:**  \nâ€œCommon sleep habit causes dementia, scientists warn.â€\n\n**Why it fails:**  \nIt likely upgrades association to causation, universalizes risk, and uses fear disproportionate to the evidence.\n\n**Accountable attention version:**  \nâ€œLarge study links irregular sleep with higher dementia risk, but it cannot prove cause.â€\n\n**Why it works:**  \nStill attractive. Still important. But it preserves study scale, relation type, and uncertainty.\n\n**Metadata note:**  \nClaim type: association. Evidence: observational study. Certainty: moderate/limited. Prohibited verbs: â€œcauses,â€ â€œproves,â€ â€œprevents.â€\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: political investigation\n\n**Unaccountable clickbait:**  \nâ€œMayor caught stealing public money.â€\n\n**Accountable version:**  \nâ€œAudit alleges the mayorâ€™s office steered contracts; mayor denies wrongdoing.â€\n\n**Why it works:**  \nIt preserves the institutional source, the nature of the claim, and the response. It can still attract attention because the stakes are clear.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: service journalism\n\n**Unaccountable clickbait:**  \nâ€œYour rent bill changes tomorrow. Donâ€™t ignore this.â€\n\n**Accountable version:**  \nâ€œRent-credit applications close tomorrow. Hereâ€™s who qualifies and what proof you need.â€\n\n**Why it works:**  \nUrgency is justified. The user knows the topic, deadline, relevance test, and expected payoff.\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: AI slop article\n\n**Unaccountable clickbait:**  \nâ€œExperts reveal the one fruit you should never eat after 40.â€\n\n**Accountable version:**  \nNo publication unless there is a real expert source, a specific claim, disclosed evidence, and an editor willing to stand behind it.\n\n**Why it matters:**  \nAI content farms can cheaply produce health, finance, travel, and lifestyle articles that imitate service journalism while lacking accountable sourcing. Studies show that AI-generated news-like text can be difficult for readers to detect, and monitoring organizations have documented rapid growth in AI content-farm sites. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12128?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: thumbnail\n\n**Unaccountable thumbnail:**  \nA crying child, flames, a red arrow, and the word â€œEXPOSED,â€ attached to a zoning-board story.\n\n**Accountable thumbnail:**  \nA crop of the zoning map, highlighted parcel, and text: â€œNew industrial zone proposed near two schools.â€\n\n**Why it works:**  \nVisual drama comes from relevance, not imported affect.\n\n---\n\n## A protocol sketch: Accountable Attention Card 0.1\n\nExisting metadata systems already cover pieces of this. Open Graph supports rich previews; RSS supports syndication and extensions; ActivityPub supports decentralized object delivery and updates; IPTC standards support news metadata; C2PA Content Credentials aim to describe origin and edits; Schema.org ClaimReview encodes fact-checking claims. ([ogp.me](https://ogp.me/))\n\nThe missing layer is a standard for **warrant-bearing previews**.\n\n```json id=\"zoo5ku\"\n{\n  \"@context\": [\n    \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"https://example.org/accountable-attention-card/0.1\"\n  ],\n  \"canonical_url\": \"https://publisher.example/story\",\n  \"surface_type\": \"headline | social_card | push_alert | rss_item | activitypub_note | search_snippet\",\n  \"display_text\": \"Audit alleges the mayorâ€™s office steered contracts; mayor denies wrongdoing.\",\n  \"summary\": \"A city audit says three contracts violated procurement rules. The mayor's office disputes the findings.\",\n  \"claim_atoms\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"A city audit alleges contract steering.\",\n      \"claim_type\": \"reported_claim\",\n      \"source_relation\": \"audit_alleges\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"public_document\",\n      \"certainty\": \"alleged\",\n      \"scope\": {\n        \"place\": \"City X\",\n        \"time_period\": \"2024 contracts\",\n        \"actors\": [\"mayor's office\", \"city auditor\"]\n      },\n      \"support\": [\n        {\n          \"source_title\": \"City Auditor Report\",\n          \"source_url\": \"https://city.example/audit.pdf\",\n          \"source_date\": \"2026-06-08\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"publisher\": \"Example Newsroom\",\n    \"byline\": \"Reporter Name\",\n    \"editorial_review\": true,\n    \"ai_assistance\": {\n      \"used\": true,\n      \"role\": \"headline variants generated; final edited by human\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"media\": {\n    \"thumbnail_url\": \"https://publisher.example/image.jpg\",\n    \"is_synthetic\": false,\n    \"is_illustrative\": false,\n    \"origin_time\": \"2026-06-08T14:00:00-05:00\",\n    \"origin_place\": \"City Hall\",\n    \"content_credentials\": \"present\"\n  },\n  \"commercial_context\": {\n    \"sponsored\": false,\n    \"affiliate_links\": false,\n    \"paid_distribution\": false\n  },\n  \"revision\": {\n    \"version\": 3,\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-06-10T09:00:00-05:00\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-06-11T11:30:00-05:00\",\n    \"status\": \"active | corrected | retracted | killed\",\n    \"correction_notice_url\": null\n  },\n  \"distribution\": {\n    \"cache_ttl\": \"PT10M\",\n    \"rss_guid\": \"example-story-123\",\n    \"activitypub_id\": \"https://publisher.example/ap/story/123\",\n    \"allow_platform_summary\": true,\n    \"required_update_propagation\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe goal is not to create paperwork for its own sake. The goal is to make the attention surface auditable. A platform, browser, RSS reader, Mastodon instance, search engine, newsletter client, or fact-checking tool should be able to ask: **What claim does this teaser make, what supports it, what changed, and who is accountable?**\n\n---\n\n## Rubric: warrant-preserving attention design\n\nScore each item 0â€“2.\n\n| Criterion | 0 | 1 | 2 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **Truth entailment** | Implies unsupported or false claim. | Mostly supported but ambiguous. | Every factual implication is supported. |\n| **Source preservation** | Removes crucial â€œwho says?â€ context. | Source appears only after click. | Source relation visible in surface when material. |\n| **Uncertainty preservation** | Upgrades weak evidence to certainty. | Some hedging retained. | Evidence strength and uncertainty are preserved. |\n| **Scope accuracy** | Inflates anecdote/local case into trend. | Scope partly visible. | Scope is clear: who, where, when, how broad. |\n| **Proportionality** | Emotional intensity exceeds evidence/stakes. | Some sensational excess. | Tone matches stakes and evidence. |\n| **Payoff sufficiency** | Click required to learn basic topic or claim. | Topic clear, payoff delayed. | User can make an informed click/no-click decision. |\n| **Image integrity** | Misleading, unrelated, synthetic undisclosed. | Relevant but context thin. | Relevant, contextualized, synthetic/illustrative status clear. |\n| **Commercial transparency** | Sponsored/affiliate/paid traffic hidden. | Disclosed only after click. | Commercial context visible when relevant. |\n| **Correction readiness** | No durable update path. | Article can update, previews may not. | Cards/snippets/feeds have version and correction propagation. |\n| **User-benefit metric** | Optimized for raw CTR only. | Some quality metric considered. | Optimized for informed satisfaction, retention of accuracy, and public value. |\n\n**Interpretation:**  \n0â€“9: clickbait or high-risk teaser.  \n10â€“14: publish only after revision.  \n15â€“18: acceptable.  \n19â€“20: strong accountable attention design.\n\n---\n\n## Automated headline generation: avoiding overclaiming\n\nA safer headline-generation system should not simply optimize for predicted CTR. It should use a constrained pipeline:\n\n1. **Extract claim atoms** from the article: actors, actions, evidence, dates, quantities, uncertainty markers.\n2. **Generate candidate headlines** with style constraints: service, investigative, explanatory, urgent, playful, etc.\n3. **Run source-faithfulness checks** using natural language inference or claim-verification models: does the article entail the headline?\n4. **Run overclaiming checks:** allegation-to-fact, correlation-to-causation, possibility-to-certainty, local-to-general, preprint-to-proof.\n5. **Require source and uncertainty preservation** for high-stakes categories: health, elections, crime, finance, war, disaster, identity-based claims.\n6. **A/B test against ethical metrics:** not just click-through, but bounce, complaint rate, correction rate, reader satisfaction, comprehension, and whether users can accurately predict article content from the teaser.\n7. **Human sign-off** for high-stakes or contested claims.\n8. **Attach metadata** to the chosen surface: source relation, evidence type, version, correction status, AI role.\n\nThe key rule: generated headlines should be selected from the set of **entailed, uncertainty-preserving, source-aware candidates**. Within that safe set, optimize for attraction.\n\n---\n\n## â€œEthical idle clickbaitâ€\n\nThere is a narrow category worth redeeming: **ethical idle clickbait**. This is low-stakes curiosity design that is playful, attractive, and perhaps suspenseful, but not deceptive or consequentially misleading.\n\nPermissible example:\n\n> â€œThe tiny design choice that makes old diners feel cozyâ€\n\nThis can be ethical if the article actually identifies the design choice, does so early, and does not pretend to reveal a public-health, political, or financial secret.\n\nLess permissible:\n\n> â€œThe tiny design choice in your kitchen that could be making you sickâ€\n\nNow the surface invokes health risk. The burden of evidence rises. The teaser must specify the risk, evidence, and actionability.\n\nSo the ethical license for curiosity gaps depends on stakes. The higher the stakes, the less acceptable it is to hide the basic claim.\n\n---\n\n## Risks and limits\n\n1. **Metadata can be forged, stripped, ignored, or gamed.** C2PA-style provenance is promising, but critics argue it is not sufficient for high-stakes trust on its own, and platforms may fail to preserve metadata consistently. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24890?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n2. **Rubrics can become performative compliance.** A bad actor can mimic â€œallegedly,â€ â€œexperts say,â€ or â€œaccording to documentsâ€ without meaningful verification.\n\n3. **Small publishers may face unequal burden.** Metadata and review workflows could help large institutions while overloading independent journalists unless built into CMS tools.\n\n4. **Truthful surfaces can still manipulate salience.** A teaser can be technically accurate but misleading through image choice, ordering, omission, or emotional framing.\n\n5. **Ad markets may reward borderline behavior faster than governance can punish it.** MFA and junk-news systems thrive because the economic signal arrives immediately, while accountability is delayed.\n\n6. **Labor reform is part of epistemic reform.** Accountable attention requires time: to verify, edit, contextualize, correct, and document. If writers are paid per unit at impossible speeds, ethics becomes decorative.\n\n---\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **Can platforms rank by warrant quality?** Search and social systems already detect spam and engagement bait. Could they also rank teasers by source preservation, correction readiness, and uncertainty accuracy?\n\n2. **What is the right metadata minimum?** Too little metadata is useless; too much becomes burdensome. The practical minimum may be: claim type, source relation, certainty, version, correction status, media provenance, commercial disclosure.\n\n3. **How should correction propagation work across screenshots and reposts?** Protocols can update cards and feeds, but screenshots are culturally powerful and technically detached.\n\n4. **Can ad exchanges price down epistemic risk?** Ads.txt, sellers.json, and SupplyChain address seller transparency; similar infrastructure could mark MFA, undisclosed AI farms, correction failures, and provenance gaps.\n\n5. **What counts as user satisfaction?** Dwell time can reward outrage or confusion. Better metrics might include â€œarticle matched preview,â€ â€œreader learned what they expected,â€ â€œlow regret,â€ and â€œaccurate recall.â€\n\n6. **Can AI help repair what AI worsens?** AI can generate slop, but it can also check entailment, detect overclaiming, classify uncertainty, compare headline to body, and flag missing provenance.\n\n7. **What is the public-interest equivalent of CTR?** A newsroom might optimize for â€œattention converted into warranted understanding,â€ not merely â€œattention captured.â€\n\n---\n\n## Annotated bibliography\n\n**JÃ¡cobo-Morales & Marino-JimÃ©nez, systematic review of clickbait research.**  \nUseful for mapping clickbait scholarship after 2018 across detection, semantic techniques, curiosity, and credibility. It supports treating clickbait as a multi-dimensional phenomenon rather than just â€œannoying headlines.â€ ([ojcmt.net](https://www.ojcmt.net/article/clickbait-research-challenges-and-opportunities-a-systematic-literature-review-15267))\n\n**Nickl et al., â€œThe evolution of online news headlines.â€**  \nA large-scale study of roughly 40 million headlines showing growth in features such as negativity and length across outlets and countries, consistent with adaptation to digital attention pressures. ([nature.com](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-025-04514-7))\n\n**Sundar et al., â€œSharing without clicking on news in social media.â€**  \nImportant evidence that social users often share links without clicking, which makes preview surfaces epistemically powerful in their own right. ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39562798/))\n\n**Google Search spam policies.**  \nAuthoritative platform policy source defining spam as deceptive or manipulative behavior and explicitly including attempts to manipulate generative AI responses in Google Search. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies))\n\n**Google Discover content policies.**  \nUseful because it treats clickbait at the preview layer: titles, snippets, images, exaggeration, withheld information, sensationalism, outrage, and morbid curiosity. ([developers.google.com](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/google-discover))\n\n**YouTube spam, deceptive practices, and scams policy.**  \nShows how metadata and engagement manipulation are governed on video platforms, not just text pages. ([support.google.com](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2801973?hl=en))\n\n**DoubleVerify / ANA discussion of MFA sites.**  \nUseful current industry framing of MFA as ad-arbitrage infrastructure with traits including high ad density, paid traffic, refresh behavior, click loops, duplicated content, and AI-generated material. ([doubleverify.com](https://doubleverify.com/company/newsroom/doubleverify-to-protect-advertisers-against-made-for-advertising-content-with-industry-first-holistic-solution))\n\n**IAB Tech Lab: ads.txt, sellers.json, and SupplyChain object.**  \nKey infrastructure documents for understanding how ad-tech tries to identify authorized sellers and trace intermediaries in programmatic advertising. ([iabtechlab.com](https://iabtechlab.com/sellers-json/))\n\n**NewsGuard AI Tracking Center.**  \nCurrent monitoring source documenting the growth of AI-generated news/information content farms across languages, useful for the â€œAI slopâ€ section. ([newsguardtech.com](https://www.newsguardtech.com/special-reports/ai-tracking-center))\n\n**Puccetti et al., â€œAI â€˜Newsâ€™ Content Farms Are Easy to Make and Hard to Detect.â€**  \nShows how easily fine-tuned language models can produce news-like text that native speakers struggle to identify, underscoring the detection/accountability problem. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12128?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Russell et al., audit of AI use in American newspapers.**  \nImportant for nuance: AI generation is not only a spam-farm issue; it is also entering mainstream/local news production, often without clear disclosure. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18774?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\n\n**Carlos Diaz Ruiz, disinformation markets.**  \nFrames disinformation as a market outcome produced by engagement monetization, programmatic advertising, moderation markets, influencer monetization, and algorithmic optimization. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/14614448231207644))\n\n**McCain Institute / Carter Center, â€œThe Disinformation Economy.â€**  \nUseful empirical and policy framing for how misinformation traffic can be monetized through display advertising and why reducing monetization can reduce incentives. ([mccaininstitute.org](https://www.mccaininstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TheDisinfoEcon_DRAFT64.pdf))\n\n**Gollmitzer, freelance journalism and labor precarity.**  \nGrounds the memoâ€™s labor section: low-quality content economies are not just about bad actors; they depend on insecure media labor and shrinking protections. ([journals.sagepub.com](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/20594364241230434))\n\n**C2PA and Content Credentials.**  \nCore provenance standard for describing content origin and edits, especially relevant for thumbnails, synthetic media, and AI-assisted production. ([c2pa.org](https://c2pa.org/))\n\n**IPTC standards.**  \nImportant for existing news metadata infrastructure: photo metadata, video metadata, media topics, RightsML, and NewsML-G2. ([iptc.org](https://iptc.org/))\n\n**Schema.org ClaimReview.**  \nRelevant to claim-level metadata and fact-checking representation, though not sufficient by itself for ordinary teasers. ([schema.org](https://schema.org/ClaimReview))\n\n**Open Graph, RSS, ActivityPub, and Fediverse preview proposals.**  \nThese show that preview infrastructure already exists; the missing piece is warrant-bearing metadata and correction propagation across those surfaces. ([ogp.me](https://ogp.me/))\n\n**Headline hallucination detection research.**  \nUseful for automated headline generation: generated headlines can be unsupported by source text, and recent datasets/modeling work directly targets that problem. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05852))\n\n**Constructive journalism research.**  \nShows that public-interest packaging need not be grim, boring, or purely negative; solutions-oriented and matter-of-fact approaches can empower readers while preserving journalistic seriousness. ([constructiveinstitute.org](https://constructiveinstitute.org/research-overview-effects/))\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\n\nâ€œClickbaitâ€ should not be redeemed as deception with better manners. But the underlying desireâ€”to make people look, care, and actâ€”can be redeemed.\n\nThe ethical target is **maximal attraction under warrant constraints**:\n\n> Make the surface as compelling as possible, but never more certain, more sourced, more dramatic, more general, more visual, or more urgent than the evidence allows.\n\nThat is the difference between stealing attention and earning it.\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/researching-skill","digest":"sha256:07403ddf4dd07e4f7a7521d4c617f7ce975b928f65d9f82e3991e5273d0c9703","content":"---\nname: researching\ndescription: Find the day's story â€” an under-discussed current event that is a window onto collapse â€” and fix its single SEO term. Run this first; its brief feeds writing.\n---\n\n# Researching a Koios.News story\n\nThe job is not to cover the biggest headline.\nIt is to find the event that is **under-discussed relative to how much it reveals** â€” the small fracture that is a clear window onto the larger emergent phenomenon.\n\n## Process\n\n1. **Scan the last 24 hours to 7 days** of news.\n  Only use events you can confirm happened within the last week.\n2. **Pick for revelation, not size.** Favour an event that (a) is not being widely discussed, and (b) connects cleanly to the collapse frame â€” climate breakdown as upstream cause, a compounding feedback rather than an isolated incident.\n3. **Find the primary sources.** Get title, author, and permalink for each.\n  You will cite these, not paraphrase them away.\n4. **Name the synthetic frame** â€” the one sentence that says what larger system this event is a window onto.\n5. **Choose the single SEO term.** One term the whole post will align to; it must read naturally in a first paragraph and in a subheader.\n6. **Feasibility-check** any future the event implies: can the world actually support that adaptation at that point?\n\n## Output: a research brief\n\nWrite a brief to `library/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md` (use the script).\nIt carries: the event, the confirmed primary sources, why it is under-discussed, the chosen SEO term, and the collapse-connection angle.\nThe brief is what `working/writing/` consumes.\n\n## Working from a specific source\n\nSometimes the story starts the other way round: someone hands you ONE source â€” a think-tank dispatch, an investigation, a report â€” and asks whether it belongs in Koios.\nThe **submission desk** is for that.\nIt does not paraphrase the source into a post; it appraises beat fit, then *finds more* â€” pulling related items out of the beat's own wires (and any sources you already found by hand) â€” and synthesizes original, multi-source analysis anchored on the seed, through the same cultivation cycle every article runs through.\n\n- A source outside every Koios beat is witnessed as out-of-frame and never drafted (the same lawful shape as unsupported demand).\n- The seed leads; related wire matches and your discoveries are the supporting context the analysis draws on.\n  Aim for several real sources cited, not one.\n- The draft candidate is a starting point, not the post.\n  A single-source LLM rewrite is slop; the editor's job is the original synthesis across the gathered sources, with every figure and claim warranted.\n\n```sh\n# JSON on stdin (url + title required; summary/text/beat/term optional):\necho '{\"url\":\"â€¦\",\"title\":\"â€¦\",\"summary\":\"â€¦\",\"beat\":\"collapse-conflict\"}' \\\n  | pnpm --filter @emsenn/koios.news run source:submit\n```\n\nIn code, `submitSource(seed, { mode: \"expand\", discoveredSources })` from `@emsenn/koios.news/researching/sourcing`; `gatherRelatedSources(...)\n` exposes the \"find more\" step on its own.\n\n## Scripts\n\n- `scripts/new-brief.mjs \"<title>\" <seo-term>` â€” scaffold a dated brief in `library/` with the sections above, ready to fill in.\n- `pnpm --filter @emsenn/koios.news run source:submit` â€” submit one specific source (JSON on stdin) for appraisal and multi-source expansion.\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/researching-script","digest":"sha256:86672f49839e2f1a2710ca71790c87f5daee5a6b8d40b057b12cbcd028444773","content":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// new-brief.mjs â€” scaffold a dated Koios.News research brief.\n// Usage: node new-brief.mjs \"<title>\" \"<seo term>\"\n// Writes working/researching/library/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md (won't overwrite).\n\nimport { writeFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { dirname, resolve } from \"node:path\";\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n\nconst [, , title, seoTerm] = process.argv;\nif (!title) {\n  console.error('usage: new-brief.mjs \"<title>\" \"<seo term>\"');\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst library = resolve(here, \"..\", \"library\");\nconst today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);\nconst slug = title\n  .toLowerCase()\n  .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\")\n  .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, \"\");\nconst file = resolve(library, `${today}-${slug}.md`);\n\nif (existsSync(file)) {\n  console.error(`refusing to overwrite ${file}`);\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst brief = `---\ntitle: \"${title}\"\ndate: ${today}\nseo_term: \"${seoTerm ?? \"\"}\"\nstatus: brief\n---\n\n# ${title}\n\n## The event\n<!-- what happened, confirmed within the last 7 days -->\n\n## Primary sources\n<!-- title â€” author â€” permalink, one per line -->\n-\n\n## Why it is under-discussed\n<!-- what the coverage is missing -->\n\n## The collapse-connection angle\n<!-- one sentence: what larger system this is a window onto -->\n\n## Feasibility check\n<!-- can the world support the future this implies, at that point? -->\n`;\n\nmkdirSync(library, { recursive: true });\nwriteFileSync(file, brief);\nconsole.log(file);\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/writing-skill","digest":"sha256:52e20f1bf6b318b20f389cfb93ae41fa4cf308f0e6337c939848255d709d2e40","content":"---\nname: writing\ndescription: Draft a Koios.News post from a research brief â€” the article plus its excerpt, meta description, and tweet â€” in the site's frontmatter shape. Consumes a researching brief; hands off to editing.\n---\n\n# Writing a Koios.News post\n\nTake a brief from `working/researching/library/` and turn it into a publishable draft in `content/posts/`.\nWrite in Nik's voice (see `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Shape of the article\n\n- **Lede (first paragraph):** state the event plainly and land the **SEO term** in it.\n  Then the turn â€” why this is a fracture, not an incident.\n- **Subheaders (`##`):** the SEO term appears in at least one.\n  Each section reads the event through the collapse frame; no section treats it as isolated.\n- **Length:** â‰¥ 800 words.\n- **Citations:** primary sources by title, author, permalink â€” linked inline.\n  Never paraphrase a source's argument away.\n- **Close:** tie the event into the larger compounding pattern.\n  A vision, never a prophecy â€” \"might\", not \"will\".\n\n## The three extra outputs (frontmatter, not body)\n\nEvery post also carries, in its frontmatter â€” never as trailing sections in the body:\n\n- `summary` â€” a ~50-word excerpt, SEO term included.\n- `description` â€” a ~120-character meta description for link previews.\n- `koios.tweet` â€” one share sentence with an emoji, no hashtags, SEO term included.\n\n## Frontmatter shape\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"...\"            # the descriptive headline\ndate: YYYY-MM-DD\ndraft: true             # editing clears this when the post ships\nauthor: \"Nik Koios\"\nslug: \"...\"\ndescription: \"...\"      # ~120 char meta\nsummary: \"...\"         # ~50 word excerpt\nkeywords: [\"<seo term>\"]\ncategories: [\"Climate Breakdown\"]\ntags: [\"...\", \"...\"]\nkoios:\n  seo_term: \"<seo term>\"\n  tweet: \"...\"\n```\n\n## Scripts\n\n- `scripts/new-post.mjs \"<title>\" <seo-term>` â€” scaffold the post file in `content/posts/<slug>.md` with this frontmatter and section stubs, dated today and marked `draft: true`.\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/writing-script","digest":"sha256:2bf832c717e9a46ae9eacf4d6655841e89e4b98df74ff23de18957b1b3aa959f","content":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// new-post.mjs â€” scaffold a Koios.News post in the site's frontmatter shape.\n// Usage: node new-post.mjs \"<title>\" \"<seo term>\"\n// Writes content/posts/<slug>.md (won't overwrite), marked draft: true.\n\nimport { writeFileSync, existsSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { dirname, resolve } from \"node:path\";\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n\nconst [, , title, seoTerm] = process.argv;\nif (!title) {\n  console.error('usage: new-post.mjs \"<title>\" \"<seo term>\"');\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n// here = working/writing/scripts -> up 3 to package root, then into content.\nconst posts = resolve(here, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"content\", \"posts\");\nconst today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);\nconst slug = title\n  .toLowerCase()\n  .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\")\n  .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, \"\");\nconst file = resolve(posts, `${slug}.md`);\nconst term = seoTerm ?? \"\";\n\nif (existsSync(file)) {\n  console.error(`refusing to overwrite ${file}`);\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst post = `---\ntitle: \"${title}\"\ndate: ${today}\ndraft: true\nauthor: \"Nik Koios\"\nslug: \"${slug}\"\ndescription: \"\"\nsummary: \"\"\nkeywords: [\"${term}\"]\ncategories: [\"Climate Breakdown\"]\ntags: []\nkoios:\n  seo_term: \"${term}\"\n  tweet: \"\"\n---\n\n<!-- Lede: state the event and land \"${term}\" here. Then the turn. -->\n\n## <!-- a subheader; \"${term}\" should appear in at least one -->\n\n<!-- read the event through the collapse frame; cite primary sources inline -->\n\n## What this is a window onto\n\n<!-- tie it into the larger compounding pattern â€” a vision, never a prophecy -->\n`;\n\nwriteFileSync(file, post);\nconsole.log(file);\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/editing-skill","digest":"sha256:9cd73e9e1236d8fd01d280dd20738f4a676fc324bb38feb6e12eaae9ef890276","content":"---\nname: editing\ndescription: Audit a draft before it ships â€” voice, SEO-term placement, citations, length, and the no-prophecy rule. Mostly mechanical checks via lint-post; the judgment calls are voice and citations.\n---\n\n# Editing a Koios.News post\n\nNothing ships until it passes here.\nTwo layers: the mechanical checks (run the linter) and the judgment checks (you, reading as Nik).\n\n## Mechanical â€” run the linter\n\n`scripts/lint-post.mjs content/posts/<slug>.md` checks:\n\n- body length â‰¥ 800 words;\n- the SEO term (`koios.seo_term`) appears in the **first paragraph**;\n- the SEO term appears in **at least one `##` subheader**;\n- `summary` (~50 words), `description` (~120 chars), and `koios.tweet` are all present and non-empty;\n- reports the `draft` state.\n\nIt exits non-zero if a hard check fails.\nFix and re-run until clean.\n\n## Judgment â€” read it as Nik\n\n- **Voice.** Confident-but-comfortable, content-farm register, concise.\n  No hedging filler, no academic throat-clearing.\n  One or two examples, not a pile.\n- **Citations.** Every claim that needs a source has one, by title/author/ permalink, linked inline.\n  No source's argument paraphrased away.\n- **No prophecy.** Scan for \"will happen\" / certainty about the future.\n  A vision is \"might\", not \"will\".\n  Rewrite anything that predicts.\n- **Not isolated.** Every section connects the event to the larger compounding phenomenon.\n  Cut any paragraph that treats it as a one-off.\n\n## Scripts\n\n- `scripts/lint-post.mjs <post.md>` â€” the mechanical checks above.\n"},{"sourceId":"newsroom/editing-script","digest":"sha256:6ddeff9a3bd002ec65f3d7a4842e90b7ada8f70353c387ecc87515654c22e7e0","content":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// lint-post.mjs â€” mechanical pre-publish checks for a Koios.News post.\n// Usage: node lint-post.mjs <path/to/post.md>\n// Exit 0 if all hard checks pass, 1 otherwise. Soft checks print warnings.\n\nimport { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\n\nconst path = process.argv[2];\nif (!path) {\n  console.error(\"usage: lint-post.mjs <post.md>\");\n  process.exit(2);\n}\n\nconst raw = readFileSync(path, \"utf8\");\nconst m = raw.match(/^---\\n([\\s\\S]*?)\\n---\\n([\\s\\S]*)$/);\nif (!m) {\n  console.error(\"âœ— no YAML frontmatter found\");\n  process.exit(1);\n}\nconst [, fm, body] = m;\n\n// --- tiny frontmatter field readers (good enough for our flat + koios shape) ---\nconst top = (k) => {\n  const r = fm.match(new RegExp(`^${k}:\\\\s*\"?(.*?)\"?\\\\s*$`, \"m\"));\n  return r ? r[1] : null;\n};\nconst koios = (k) => {\n  const r = fm.match(new RegExp(`^\\\\s{2,}${k}:\\\\s*\"?(.*?)\"?\\\\s*$`, \"m\"));\n  return r ? r[1] : null;\n};\n\nconst seoTerm = koios(\"seo_term\") || (top(\"keywords\") || \"\").replace(/[[\\]\"]/g, \"\");\nconst summary = top(\"summary\");\nconst description = top(\"description\");\nconst tweet = koios(\"tweet\");\nconst draft = top(\"draft\");\n\nconst words = body.trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;\nconst firstPara = body.trim().split(/\\n\\s*\\n/)[0] || \"\";\nconst subheads = [...body.matchAll(/^##\\s+(.*)$/gm)].map((x) => x[1]);\nconst has = (hay, needle) =>\n  needle && hay.toLowerCase().includes(needle.toLowerCase());\n\nconst fails = [];\nconst warns = [];\n\nif (!seoTerm) fails.push(\"no seo_term / keywords set\");\nif (words < 800) fails.push(`body is ${words} words (need â‰¥ 800)`);\nif (seoTerm && !has(firstPara, seoTerm))\n  fails.push(`SEO term \"${seoTerm}\" not in the first paragraph`);\nif (seoTerm && !subheads.some((h) => has(h, seoTerm)))\n  fails.push(`SEO term \"${seoTerm}\" not in any ## subheader`);\nif (!summary) fails.push(\"summary (excerpt) is empty\");\nif (!description) fails.push(\"description (meta) is empty\");\nif (!tweet) fails.push(\"koios.tweet is empty\");\n\nif (summary) {\n  const sw = summary.split(/\\s+/).length;\n  if (sw < 35 || sw > 65) warns.push(`summary is ${sw} words (aim ~50)`);\n}\nif (description && description.length > 160)\n  warns.push(`description is ${description.length} chars (aim ~120)`);\nif (draft === \"true\") warns.push(\"draft: true â€” not yet publishable\");\n\nconst name = path.split(\"/\").pop();\nfor (const w of warns) console.log(`  âš  ${name}: ${w}`);\nfor (const f of fails) console.log(`  âœ— ${name}: ${f}`);\nif (fails.length === 0) console.log(`  âœ“ ${name}: ${words} words, checks pass`);\n\nprocess.exit(fails.length ? 1 : 0);\n"},{"sourceId":"production/cultivate","digest":"sha256:0c08569512741ffad8f097b843707fde991ee032dcec3defc065ba5fc3e29cd2","content":"/**\n * cultivate â€” one witnessed cultivation cycle for one property: demand\n * creates the assignment; the term-as-lens selects ONE source story from\n * the witnessed intake; the draft is asked for, judged by the gates, and\n * revised at most twice; the candidate is projected into the property's\n * content store ALWAYS draft: true; the run is recorded on the property's\n * append-only run trace; and an editorial work item is deposited to the\n * swarm hub when one is configured. Recurrence is the farm tick's (or\n * cron's) job â€” one cycle is one invocation, and time enters as the `at`\n * parameter.\n *\n * Subsumes the former sites/koios.news/src/mill.ts runMill (2026-06-12). The\n * unsupported-demand obstruction semantics are kept exactly: demand\n * creates assignments, never evidence â€” when the intake supports no term\n * in the beat, the lawful outcome is no article, witnessed. Withheld\n * candidates and obstructed runs ARE the compost: the run trace plus the\n * draft-true store retain every subpar output as learning substrate, and\n * `compost()` reads them back with their gate evidence.\n */\n\nimport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\nimport { appendFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { dirname, join } from \"node:path\";\n\nimport { encodeFrontMatterDocument, type FrontMatterDocument } from \"../codecs/frontmatter.mjs\";\nimport { copyedit, type CopyEdit } from \"../topology/copyedit.mjs\";\nimport { newsSlug } from \"../topology/news/index.mjs\";\nimport { makeAsk, type ContentProviderUsage } from \"../computer/ask.mjs\";\nimport { syndicationAdapters, type FetchLike, type SyndicationAdapter, type SyndicationItem } from \"../syndication/index.mjs\";\n\nimport {\n  draftPrompt, parseDraftReply, selectStory, termSupportCount, judgeDraft,\n  type PostDraft, type FarmGate, type JudgeContext, type ArticleVoice,\n} from \"../topology/article.mjs\";\nimport { extractLinks } from \"../topology/gates.mjs\";\nimport { demandTable, readSearchConsole, type DemandEntry, type ViewCountsReading } from \"./demand.mjs\";\nimport { contentFarmDeskObstruction, editorialWorkItem, type EditorialOffice, type EditorialWorkItem } from \"./desks.mjs\";\nimport { isJsonRecord, parseLines, requireJsonValue, serializeLines } from \"@emsenn/relating/writing\";\nimport { candidateDocument, loadCandidates, runsPath, searchConsolePath, type FarmProperty } from \"./property.mjs\";\n\nexport type Ask = (prompt: string) => Promise<string> | string;\n\n// ---------- the cultivation seams (the fiber over the cycle) ----------\n// content-farm is the BASE engine; a specialization (e.g. the newsroom) is a\n// FIBER over it â€” its special functions injected as these seam parameters. The\n// cycle skeleton (demand â†’ assignment, witnessed intake, the support-walk\n// obstruction semantics, gate judging, usage accounting) is the base; `select`\n// and `compose` are the two seams that vary by specialization. The defaults\n// below reproduce the farm's single-story-markdown behavior EXACTLY, so the\n// generalization is behavior-preserving for every existing caller.\n// (The third seam â€” `record`, where the output carrier itself varies, e.g.\n// markdown candidate vs claim-state assertion trace â€” is the deeper one and is\n// staged separately; see dev/docs/notes/2026-06-15-newsroom-as-fiber-over-\n// content-farm.md.)\n\n/** The story-selection seam: from the assigned term (the lens) and the\n * witnessed intake, choose the subject story and its supporting context. The\n * farm default is the term-as-lens `selectStory` (ONE story); the newsroom\n * fiber clusters intake into N angles. */\nexport type SelectStory = (input: Readonly<{\n  term: string;\n  items: readonly SyndicationItem[];\n  genericWords: ReadonlySet<string>;\n}>) => Readonly<{ story: SyndicationItem; context: readonly SyndicationItem[] }>;\n\n/** The composition seam: turn the selected story + context into a draft. The\n * LLM is reached through the injected `ask`; the result carries the draft and\n * the local copyedits applied to it. The farm default is a single `draftPrompt`\n * call followed by the deterministic copyedit closure; the newsroom fiber\n * synthesizes a brief over the clustered papers. */\nexport type ComposeDraft = (input: Readonly<{\n  voice: ArticleVoice;\n  term: string;\n  story: SyndicationItem;\n  context: readonly SyndicationItem[];\n  ask: Ask;\n  intakeTitles: readonly string[];\n}>) => Promise<Readonly<{\n  draft: PostDraft;\n  copyedits: readonly CopyEdit[];\n  composition?: Readonly<{ prompt: string; promptDigest: string; promptCharacters: number }>;\n}>>;\n\n/** The farm's default selection: the term-as-lens `selectStory`. */\nconst defaultSelectStory: SelectStory = ({ term, items, genericWords }) =>\n  selectStory(term, items, { genericWords });\n\n/** The farm's default composition: one `draftPrompt` LLM call, then the\n * deterministic editorial closure (floor-guarded at the length gate's â‰¥ 800).\n * No re-prompt â€” the LLM is spent ONCE. */\nconst defaultCompose: ComposeDraft = async ({ voice, term, story, context, ask, intakeTitles }) => {\n  const prompt = draftPrompt({ voice, term, story, context });\n  const rawDraft = parseDraftReply(await ask(prompt));\n  // The story leads the sources so the closure always links it (the gate\n  // requires the subject story cited); context follows as supporting sources.\n  const intakeSources = [story, ...context].map((item) => ({ title: item.title, url: item.url }));\n  const edited = copyedit(rawDraft.body, { intakeTitles, intakeSources, floor: 800 });\n  const draft = edited.edits.length > 0 ? Object.freeze({ ...rawDraft, body: edited.body }) : rawDraft;\n  return Object.freeze({\n    draft,\n    copyedits: edited.edits,\n    composition: Object.freeze({\n      prompt,\n      promptDigest: `sha256:${createHash(\"sha256\").update(prompt).digest(\"hex\")}`,\n      promptCharacters: prompt.length,\n    }),\n  });\n};\n\n// ---------- the editorial desk deposit ----------\n\ntype HubFetchLike = (url: string, init?: { headers?: Record<string, string>; method?: string; body?: string }) => Promise<{\n  ok: boolean;\n  status: number;\n  json(): Promise<unknown>;\n}>;\n\nexport type FarmHub = Readonly<{\n  base: string;\n  token: string;\n  fetchImpl?: HubFetchLike;\n}>;\n\nexport type DeskWitness = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.desk:witness\";\n  at: string;\n  workId: string;\n  deposited: boolean;\n  skipped: boolean;\n}>;\n\nexport type DeskObstruction = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.desk:obstruction\";\n  at: \"hub-read\" | \"hub-deposit\";\n  reason: string;\n}>;\n\nexport type DeskJudgment = DeskWitness | DeskObstruction;\n\n/** Deposit one editorial work item, unless the hub already holds it open.\n * An unreachable hub is an obstruction value, never a crash. */\nexport async function depositEditorialWork(hub: FarmHub, item: EditorialWorkItem, options: Readonly<{ at: string; by?: string }>): Promise<DeskJudgment> {\n  const fetchImpl: HubFetchLike = hub.fetchImpl ?? (fetch as unknown as HubFetchLike);\n  const base = hub.base.replace(/\\/$/, \"\");\n  const board = item.fields.requiredSkill;\n  let open: ReadonlyArray<{ workId: string }>;\n  try {\n    const response = await fetchImpl(`${base}/work-board/${encodeURIComponent(board)}/open`, {\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${hub.token}` },\n    });\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    const body = (await response.json()) as { open?: ReadonlyArray<{ workId: string }> };\n    open = Array.isArray(body.open) ? body.open : [];\n  } catch (error) {\n    return contentFarmDeskObstruction({ at: \"hub-read\", reason: message(error) });\n  }\n  if (open.some((held) => held.workId === item.workId)) {\n    return Object.freeze({ kind: \"content.farm.desk:witness\" as const, at: options.at, workId: item.workId, deposited: false, skipped: true });\n  }\n  try {\n    const response = await fetchImpl(`${base}/work-board/${encodeURIComponent(board)}/deposit`, {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${hub.token}`, \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        workId: item.workId,\n        fields: item.fields,\n        options: { by: options.by ?? `${item.fields.office}-farm`, at: options.at },\n      }),\n    });\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n  } catch (error) {\n    return contentFarmDeskObstruction({ at: \"hub-deposit\", reason: message(error) });\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({ kind: \"content.farm.desk:witness\" as const, at: options.at, workId: item.workId, deposited: true, skipped: false });\n}\n\n// ---------- the cycle ----------\n\nexport type CultivateOptions<W = CultivationResult> = Readonly<{\n  /** The day stamp recorded on the candidate and the run trace. Time is a\n   * parameter: the caller (CLI, farm tick, cron) owns the clock. */\n  at: string;\n  ask?: Ask;\n  fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n  adapters?: Readonly<Record<string, SyndicationAdapter>>;\n  dryRun?: boolean;\n  /** Force the assignment to this term (the human's assignment; the\n   * support walk is bypassed). */\n  term?: string;\n  beat?: string;\n  provider?: string;\n  model?: string;\n  env?: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;\n  /** Earned-attention reading folded into the demand table. */\n  views?: ViewCountsReading;\n  /** When configured, the produced candidate is deposited as an editorial\n   * work item on the swarm hub. */\n  hub?: FarmHub;\n  /** Pre-fetched intake. When provided, the cycle skips its own fetch and\n   * uses these items â€” so an orchestration can fetch once and run many cycles\n   * (e.g. one per angle) over the same witnessed intake. */\n  items?: readonly SyndicationItem[];\n  /** The story-selection seam (default: the term-as-lens `selectStory`). A\n   * specialization (the newsroom fiber) injects its own selection here. */\n  select?: SelectStory;\n  /** The composition seam (default: one `draftPrompt` call + the copyedit\n   * closure). A specialization injects its own draft synthesis here. */\n  compose?: ComposeDraft;\n  /** The recording seam (default: write a markdown candidate + JSONL run trace\n   * + hub work item, returning a CultivationResult). A specialization injects\n   * its own output carrier here â€” e.g. the newsroom fiber emits a claim-state\n   * assertion trace instead. When provided, it determines the cycle's witness\n   * type `W`. */\n  record?: RecordCycle<W>;\n}>;\n\nexport type CultivationResult = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.cultivation:witness\";\n  property: string;\n  assignment: DemandEntry;\n  story: SyndicationItem;\n  draft: PostDraft;\n  gates: readonly FarmGate[];\n  /** The local post-production edits applied to the draft after the one\n   * LLM call, in order (empty when the draft needed no repair). */\n  copyedits: readonly CopyEdit[];\n  composition?: Readonly<{ prompt: string; promptDigest: string; promptCharacters: number }>;\n  shipped: boolean;\n  slug: string;\n  path: string | null;\n  document: FrontMatterDocument;\n  usage: Readonly<{ calls: number; tokens: number }>;\n  /** Sources that obstructed during intake, as values. */\n  intakeObstructions: readonly string[];\n  desk?: DeskJudgment;\n}>;\n\n/** The obstruction carrier for a run demand could not warrant: demand\n * creates assignments, never evidence â€” when today's intake supports no\n * term in the beat, the lawful outcome is no article, witnessed. */\nexport type CultivationObstruction = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.cultivation-obstruction\";\n  property: string;\n  obstruction: \"unsupported-demand\";\n  assignment: DemandEntry;\n  evidence: string;\n  usage: Readonly<{ calls: number; tokens: number }>;\n  intakeObstructions: readonly string[];\n}>;\n\nexport type CultivationJudgment = CultivationResult | CultivationObstruction;\n\nexport function isCultivationObstruction(result: CultivationJudgment): result is CultivationObstruction {\n  return \"obstruction\" in result;\n}\n\n// ---------- the recording seam (the output-carrier fiber) ----------\n// The cycle skeleton settles a story, a draft, and the gate verdict; HOW that\n// settlement is carried out into the world â€” markdown candidate vs claim-state\n// assertion trace â€” is the `record` seam. Everything the seam needs is gathered\n// into a carrier-independent CycleSettlement; the side-effect policy (clock,\n// whether to persist, the editorial hub) is the RecordContext.\n\n/** Everything the cycle settles before recording, independent of the output\n * carrier â€” the input every record seam receives. */\nexport type CycleSettlement = Readonly<{\n  property: FarmProperty;\n  /** The settled assignment (term + beat) the cycle warranted. */\n  assignment: DemandEntry;\n  story: SyndicationItem;\n  context: readonly SyndicationItem[];\n  draft: PostDraft;\n  copyedits: readonly CopyEdit[];\n  composition?: Readonly<{ prompt: string; promptDigest: string; promptCharacters: number }>;\n  gates: readonly FarmGate[];\n  shipped: boolean;\n  /** The URLs the draft may lawfully cite (the cycle's intake). */\n  citableUrls: readonly string[];\n  usage: Readonly<{ calls: number; tokens: number }>;\n  intakeObstructions: readonly string[];\n}>;\n\n/** The side-effect policy for recording: the clock stamp, whether to persist,\n * and the optional editorial hub. */\nexport type RecordContext = Readonly<{\n  at: string;\n  dryRun: boolean;\n  hub?: FarmHub;\n}>;\n\n/** The recording seam: carry the settled cycle out into the world as the\n * output type `W`, persisting unless `ctx.dryRun`. The farm default writes a\n * markdown candidate + JSONL run trace + hub work item and returns a\n * CultivationResult; a specialization (the newsroom fiber) emits its own\n * carrier (a claim-state assertion trace). */\nexport type RecordCycle<W> = (settlement: CycleSettlement, ctx: RecordContext) => Promise<W> | W;\n\n/** The farm's default recording: a markdown candidate (always draft: true),\n * the append-only run trace, and â€” when a hub is configured â€” an editorial\n * work-item deposit. `shipped` records only the gate verdict; publication is\n * the orchestrator's editorial read. */\nconst defaultRecord: RecordCycle<CultivationResult> = async (s, ctx) => {\n  const slug = newsSlug(s.draft.title).split(\"-\").slice(0, 9).join(\"-\") || `${s.property.name}-${ctx.at}`;\n  const sameUrl = (left: string, right: string): boolean => left.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\") === right.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\");\n  const cited = extractLinks(s.draft.body).filter((link) => s.citableUrls.some((url) => sameUrl(link, url)));\n  const document = candidateDocument({\n    draft: s.draft,\n    term: s.assignment.term,\n    beat: s.assignment.beat,\n    ...(s.property.beatCategory?.[s.assignment.beat] === undefined ? {} : { category: s.property.beatCategory[s.assignment.beat]! }),\n    author: s.property.author,\n    termKey: s.property.termKey ?? \"farm\",\n    slug,\n    day: ctx.at,\n    shipped: s.shipped,\n    cited: [...new Set(cited)],\n  });\n  let path: string | null = null;\n  let desk: DeskJudgment | undefined;\n  if (!ctx.dryRun) {\n    mkdirSync(s.property.contentDir, { recursive: true });\n    path = join(s.property.contentDir, `${slug}.md`);\n    writeFileSync(path, encodeFrontMatterDocument(document), \"utf8\");\n    recordRun(s.property, {\n      property: s.property.name,\n      term: s.assignment.term,\n      beat: s.assignment.beat,\n      story: { title: s.story.title, url: s.story.url },\n      slug,\n      shipped: s.shipped,\n      gates: s.gates.map((gate) => ({ gate: gate.gate, charge: gate.charge, evidence: gate.evidence })),\n      // The copyedit audit: provenance grows monotonically â€” every local\n      // repair is recorded with what it removed and what replaced it.\n      copyedits: s.copyedits.map((change) => ({ op: change.op, before: change.before.slice(0, 80), after: change.after.slice(0, 80) })),\n      ...(s.composition === undefined ? {} : { composition: s.composition }),\n      usage: s.usage,\n      t: ctx.at,\n    });\n    if (ctx.hub !== undefined) {\n      desk = await depositEditorialWork(ctx.hub, editorialWorkItem(s.property, { slug, title: s.draft.title, summary: s.draft.summary }), { at: ctx.at });\n    }\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"content.farm.cultivation:witness\" as const,\n    property: s.property.name,\n    assignment: s.assignment,\n    story: s.story,\n    draft: s.draft,\n    gates: s.gates,\n    copyedits: s.copyedits,\n    ...(s.composition === undefined ? {} : { composition: s.composition }),\n    shipped: s.shipped,\n    slug,\n    path,\n    document,\n    usage: s.usage,\n    intakeObstructions: s.intakeObstructions,\n    ...(desk === undefined ? {} : { desk }),\n  });\n};\n\n/** Witnessed intake for a beat: fetch every configured source through its\n * adapter, collecting items and (as values, never throws) the obstructions.\n * Exported so an orchestration (the newsroom's N-angle cycle) can fetch the\n * intake ONCE and feed it to many per-angle cultivate calls via `options.items`. */\nexport async function intakeItems(\n  property: FarmProperty,\n  beat: string,\n  io: Readonly<{ fetchImpl?: FetchLike; adapters?: Readonly<Record<string, SyndicationAdapter>> }>,\n): Promise<Readonly<{ items: readonly SyndicationItem[]; obstructions: readonly string[] }>> {\n  const registry = io.adapters ?? syndicationAdapters;\n  const items: SyndicationItem[] = [];\n  const obstructions: string[] = [];\n  for (const source of property.beatSources[beat] ?? []) {\n    const adapter = registry[source.adapter];\n    if (!adapter) {\n      obstructions.push(`unknown adapter \"${source.adapter}\"`);\n      continue;\n    }\n    try {\n      const fetched = await adapter.fetch(source, io.fetchImpl === undefined ? {} : { fetchImpl: io.fetchImpl });\n      items.push(...fetched);\n    } catch (error) {\n      obstructions.push(`${source.url ?? source.query ?? source.adapter}: ${message(error)}`);\n    }\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({ items: Object.freeze(items), obstructions: Object.freeze(obstructions) });\n}\n\nfunction recordRun(property: FarmProperty, line: Record<string, unknown>): void {\n  const path = runsPath(property);\n  mkdirSync(dirname(path), { recursive: true });\n  appendFileSync(path, serializeLines([requireJsonValue(line, \"farm run record\")]), \"utf8\");\n}\n\n/** One cultivation cycle for one property. See the module header. */\nexport function cultivate(property: FarmProperty, options: CultivateOptions): Promise<CultivationJudgment>;\nexport function cultivate<W>(property: FarmProperty, options: CultivateOptions<W> & { record: RecordCycle<W> }): Promise<W | CultivationObstruction>;\nexport async function cultivate<W>(property: FarmProperty, options: CultivateOptions<W>): Promise<W | CultivationObstruction> {\n  const genericWords = new Set(property.genericTermWords ?? []);\n  const usage = { calls: 0, tokens: 0 };\n\n  // 1. Demand creates the assignment.\n  const posts = loadCandidates(property.contentDir);\n  const demand = demandTable({\n    posts,\n    ...(options.views === undefined ? {} : { views: options.views }),\n    searchConsole: readSearchConsole(searchConsolePath(property)),\n    seeds: property.seeds,\n    termKey: property.termKey ?? \"farm\",\n  });\n  const defaultBeat = options.beat ?? Object.keys(property.beatSources)[0] ?? \"\";\n  const assignment = options.term !== undefined\n    ? demand.find((entry) => entry.term === options.term)\n      ?? Object.freeze({ beat: defaultBeat, term: options.term, score: 0, evidence: Object.freeze({ priority: 0, views: 0, impressions: 0, clicks: 0, covered: 0 }) })\n    : (options.beat !== undefined ? demand.filter((entry) => entry.beat === options.beat) : demand)[0];\n  if (assignment === undefined) throw new TypeError(\"demand table is empty; nothing to assign\");\n\n  // 2. Witnessed intake for the beat â€” pre-fetched items if the caller\n  //    injected them, otherwise fetched here. Transport failures are\n  //    obstruction values inside the cycle, never throws.\n  const intake = options.items !== undefined\n    ? Object.freeze({ items: options.items, obstructions: Object.freeze([]) as readonly string[] })\n    : await intakeItems(property, assignment.beat, {\n      ...(options.fetchImpl === undefined ? {} : { fetchImpl: options.fetchImpl }),\n      ...(options.adapters === undefined ? {} : { adapters: options.adapters }),\n    });\n\n  const obstruct = (settled: DemandEntry, evidence: string): CultivationObstruction => {\n    if (options.dryRun !== true) {\n      recordRun(property, {\n        property: property.name,\n        obstruction: \"unsupported-demand\",\n        term: settled.term,\n        beat: settled.beat,\n        evidence,\n        usage,\n        t: options.at,\n      });\n    }\n    return Object.freeze({\n      kind: \"content.farm.cultivation-obstruction\" as const,\n      property: property.name,\n      obstruction: \"unsupported-demand\" as const,\n      assignment: settled,\n      evidence,\n      usage: Object.freeze({ ...usage }),\n      intakeObstructions: intake.obstructions,\n    });\n  };\n\n  if (intake.items.length === 0) {\n    return obstruct(assignment, `no witnessed intake for beat ${assignment.beat}${intake.obstructions.length > 0 ? ` (${intake.obstructions.join(\"; \")})` : \"\"}`);\n  }\n\n  // 3. Demand meets supply: demand ranks the candidates, but the assignment\n  //    settles on the highest-ranked term today's intake can actually\n  //    support, judged on the term's distinctive words (beat-generic words\n  //    prove nothing). An unsupported term stays in the table for the day\n  //    its event arrives. A forced term is the human's assignment; the\n  //    walk is bypassed.\n  const threshold = property.supportThreshold ?? 3;\n  let settled = assignment;\n  if (options.term === undefined && termSupportCount(assignment.term, intake.items, genericWords) < threshold) {\n    const supported = demand.find((entry) => entry.beat === assignment.beat && termSupportCount(entry.term, intake.items, genericWords) >= threshold);\n    if (supported === undefined) {\n      return obstruct(assignment, `no term in beat ${assignment.beat} reaches support â‰¥ ${threshold} in today's ${intake.items.length}-article intake (top term \"${assignment.term}\" supports ${termSupportCount(assignment.term, intake.items, genericWords)})`);\n    }\n    settled = supported;\n  }\n\n  // 3b. The term is the lens; the story is the subject. One article covers\n  //     ONE source story â€” the rest of the intake is supporting context only.\n  // Stories already cited in the content store are excluded from candidacy:\n  // the same source event covered twice is a repetition, not a new article.\n  const coveredUrls = new Set(posts.flatMap((post) => {\n    const sources = post.document.frontMatter[\"sources\"];\n    return Array.isArray(sources) ? (sources as unknown[]).map(String) : [];\n  }));\n  const freshItems = intake.items.filter((article) => !coveredUrls.has(article.url));\n  const select = options.select ?? defaultSelectStory;\n  const { story, context } = select({ term: settled.term, items: freshItems.length > 0 ? freshItems : intake.items, genericWords });\n  const cycleIntake = [story, ...context];\n  const citableUrls = cycleIntake.map((article) => article.url);\n  const judgeContext: JudgeContext = Object.freeze({\n    storyUrl: story.url,\n    intakeTitles: Object.freeze(cycleIntake.map((article) => article.title)),\n    intakeTexts: Object.freeze(cycleIntake.map((article) => `${article.title} ${article.summary ?? \"\"}`)),\n  });\n\n  // 4. Compose the draft through the composition seam. The farm default is\n  //    ONE draft call followed by the deterministic editorial closure (the LLM\n  //    is spent once; the closure repairs form defects locally, no re-prompt,\n  //    floor-guarded at the length gate's â‰¥ 800 so length-reducing operators\n  //    never flip a passing length gate to failing â€” see defaultCompose and\n  //    @emsenn/content/topology/copyedit for the operators and the closure\n  //    laws). A specialization (the newsroom fiber) injects its own synthesis.\n  const ask = options.ask ?? makeAsk({\n    provider: options.provider ?? property.provider ?? \"nim\",\n    ...(options.model ?? property.model ? { model: options.model ?? property.model } : {}),\n    temperature: property.temperature ?? 0.65,\n    maxTokens: property.maxTokens ?? 3000,\n    ...(options.env === undefined ? {} : { env: options.env }),\n    onUsage: (entry: ContentProviderUsage) => {\n      usage.calls += 1;\n      usage.tokens += entry.totalTokens ?? 0;\n    },\n  });\n  const voice = property.voice;\n  const compose = options.compose ?? defaultCompose;\n  const { draft, copyedits, composition } = await compose({\n    voice, term: settled.term, story, context, ask,\n    intakeTitles: judgeContext.intakeTitles ?? [],\n  });\n  const gates = judgeDraft(draft, settled.term, citableUrls, judgeContext);\n\n  // 5. Record the settlement through the recording seam. The cycle settles a\n  //    story, a draft, and the gate verdict; HOW that is carried into the world\n  //    â€” markdown candidate (the farm default) vs claim-state assertion trace\n  //    (the newsroom fiber) â€” is the seam's job. `shipped` records only the\n  //    gate verdict; publication is the orchestrator's editorial read.\n  const shipped = gates.every((gate) => gate.charge === \"pass\");\n  const settlement: CycleSettlement = Object.freeze({\n    property,\n    assignment: settled,\n    story,\n    context,\n    draft,\n    copyedits,\n    ...(composition === undefined ? {} : { composition }),\n    gates,\n    shipped,\n    citableUrls,\n    usage: Object.freeze({ ...usage }),\n    intakeObstructions: intake.obstructions,\n  });\n  const record = (options.record ?? defaultRecord) as RecordCycle<W>;\n  return await record(settlement, {\n    at: options.at,\n    dryRun: options.dryRun === true,\n    ...(options.hub === undefined ? {} : { hub: options.hub }),\n  });\n}\n\n// ---------- the compost reading ----------\n\n/** One replayed run-trace line, as recorded. */\nexport type FarmRunRecord = Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n\n/** Replay a property's run trace. Unparseable lines are held aside, not folded. */\nexport function replayRuns(path: string): readonly FarmRunRecord[] {\n  if (!existsSync(path)) return Object.freeze([]);\n  const runs: FarmRunRecord[] = [];\n  for (const parsed of parseLines(readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"))) {\n    if (isJsonRecord(parsed)) runs.push(Object.freeze(parsed));\n  }\n  return Object.freeze(runs);\n}\n\n/** One compost entry: a withheld or obstructed run, with its evidence.\n * Subpar output is retained as learning substrate â€” the run trace and the\n * withheld draft-true candidates ARE the compost; this is the reading. */\nexport type CompostEntry = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.compost.entry\";\n  property?: string;\n  term: string;\n  beat: string;\n  slug?: string;\n  obstruction?: string;\n  evidence: readonly string[];\n  t?: string;\n}>;\n\n/** Read the compost out of a replayed run trace: every obstructed run\n * (with its obstruction evidence) and every withheld run (with the\n * evidence of each failing gate). Shipped runs are not compost. */\nexport function compost(runs: readonly FarmRunRecord[]): readonly CompostEntry[] {\n  return Object.freeze(runs.flatMap((run): CompostEntry[] => {\n    const term = typeof run.term === \"string\" ? run.term : null;\n    const beat = typeof run.beat === \"string\" ? run.beat : null;\n    if (term === null || beat === null) return [];\n    const base = {\n      kind: \"content.farm.compost.entry\" as const,\n      ...(typeof run.property === \"string\" ? { property: run.property } : {}),\n      term,\n      beat,\n      ...(typeof run.t === \"string\" ? { t: run.t } : {}),\n    };\n    if (typeof run.obstruction === \"string\") {\n      return [Object.freeze({\n        ...base,\n        obstruction: run.obstruction,\n        evidence: Object.freeze([typeof run.evidence === \"string\" ? run.evidence : \"(no recorded evidence)\"]),\n      })];\n    }\n    if (run.shipped === false) {\n      const gates = Array.isArray(run.gates) ? run.gates : [];\n      const evidence = gates.flatMap((gate): string[] => {\n        if (!gate || typeof gate !== \"object\") return [];\n        const record = gate as Record<string, unknown>;\n        if (record.charge !== \"fail\" || typeof record.gate !== \"string\") return [];\n        return [`${record.gate}: ${typeof record.evidence === \"string\" ? record.evidence : \"(no recorded evidence)\"}`];\n      });\n      return [Object.freeze({\n        ...base,\n        ...(typeof run.slug === \"string\" ? { slug: run.slug } : {}),\n        evidence: Object.freeze(evidence),\n      })];\n    }\n    return [];\n  }));\n}\n\nfunction message(error: unknown): string {\n  return error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"production/newsroom","digest":"sha256:76483a04858a4f94efefeb4afb8a9fb3edcd7c88fcf05cce7cc67d636b9ea33f","content":"/**\n * newsroom â€” the NEWSROOM FIBER over the content-farm cycle.\n *\n * Per emsenn (2026-06-15): \"newsroom is meant to be a specialization of\n * content-farm, where its special functions are parameters into a content-farm.\"\n * This module harvests those special functions out of the (retiring) standalone\n * `@emsenn/newsroom` package and exposes them as content-farm SEAM parameters â€”\n * `select`/`compose`/`record` â€” so a newsroom is literally\n * `cultivate(property, { ...options, ...newsroomProfile })`, not a parallel\n * engine. See dev/docs/notes/2026-06-15-newsroom-as-fiber-over-content-farm.md.\n *\n * This file currently carries the COMPOSE seam (multi-paper brief synthesis,\n * the newsroom reporter's voice); the SELECT seam (angle clustering) and the\n * RECORD seam (claim-state assertion trace) fold in next. The synthesis\n * functions are kept exported in their own right â€” they are real capabilities\n * (grounded multi-source briefing) and must not be lost.\n */\n\nimport { mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { join } from \"node:path\";\n\nimport type { SyndicationItem, FetchLike, SyndicationAdapter } from \"../syndication/index.mjs\";\nimport type { PostDraft, FarmGate } from \"../topology/article.mjs\";\nimport {\n  write, clearGate, assertionsFromWrites, claimSetsFromTrace, newsSlug,\n  type NewsClaimWrite, type NewsAssertionTrace, type NewsClaimSet,\n} from \"../topology/news/index.mjs\";\nimport { cultivate, intakeItems } from \"./cultivate.mjs\";\nimport type { Ask, ComposeDraft, SelectStory, RecordCycle, CycleSettlement, CultivationObstruction } from \"./cultivate.mjs\";\nimport type { DemandEntry } from \"./demand.mjs\";\nimport type { FarmProperty } from \"./property.mjs\";\n\nconst unique = <T,>(xs: readonly T[]): T[] => [...new Set(xs)];\n\n// ---------- prose normalization (harvested from @emsenn/newsroom) ----------\n\n/** Strip the tics an LLM leaves in a brief: bold runs, \"headline:\"/\"report:\"\n * prefixes, empty `(Source: )` stubs, bare citation brackets, and the spacing\n * those removals leave behind. */\nexport function cleanProse(value: unknown): string {\n  return String(value ?? \"\")\n    .replace(/\\*\\*([^*]+)\\*\\*/g, \"$1\")\n    .replace(/^\\s*(?:headline|report):\\s*/i, \"\")\n    .replace(/\\s*\\(Source:\\s*\\)\\.?/gi, \".\")\n    .replace(/\\[[\\d,\\s]+\\]/g, \" \")\n    .replace(/\\s+([.!?,;:])/g, \"$1\")\n    .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n    .trim();\n}\n\n/** Split a reply into clean, single-idea sentences; blanks are dropped. */\nexport function sentences(value: unknown): readonly string[] {\n  const text = cleanProse(value);\n  if (!text) return Object.freeze([]);\n  return Object.freeze(text.split(/(?<=[.!?])\\s+/).map(cleanProse).filter((line) => line.length > 1));\n}\n\n// ---------- brief synthesis (harvested from @emsenn/newsroom) ----------\n\n/** A reporter's brief: one headline sentence and the coverage findings. The\n * claim-state-first shape â€” prose is a lossy projection of these. */\nexport type StructuredBrief = Readonly<{ headline: string; findings: readonly string[] }>;\n\n/** The reporter prompt: ground a short brief ONLY in the bundled sources. */\nexport function synthesisPrompt(systemPrompt: string, topic: string, papers: readonly SyndicationItem[]): string {\n  const bundle = papers.map((paper, index) => `[${index + 1}] ${paper.title}: ${paper.summary ?? \"\"}`).join(\"\\n\\n\");\n  return `${systemPrompt}\\n\\nTopic: \"${topic}\". Sources:\\n\\n${bundle}\\n\\n` +\n    \"Write a short news brief grounded only in the sources above: one headline sentence, then 3 to 6 short, single-idea sentences of coverage.\";\n}\n\n/** Read a reply into a brief: the first sentence is the headline, the rest are\n * findings. A reply with no prose is an error â€” synthesis produced nothing. */\nexport function structureBrief(reply: unknown): StructuredBrief {\n  const parts = sentences(reply);\n  if (parts.length === 0) throw new Error(\"synthesis returned no prose\");\n  return Object.freeze({ headline: parts[0]!, findings: Object.freeze(parts.slice(1)) });\n}\n\n/** Synthesize a brief over the bundled papers through the injected `ask`. */\nexport async function synthesizeBrief(\n  ask: Ask,\n  params: Readonly<{ systemPrompt?: string; topic: string; papers: readonly SyndicationItem[] }>,\n): Promise<StructuredBrief> {\n  return structureBrief(await ask(synthesisPrompt(params.systemPrompt ?? \"\", params.topic, params.papers)));\n}\n\n// ---------- the newsroom COMPOSE seam ----------\n\n/** The newsroom's composition: synthesize a brief over the selected story and\n * its context (the cycle's intake = the reporter's papers), grounding every\n * claim in those sources, and project the brief into a PostDraft. Unlike the\n * farm default (one freeform draft prompt), the newsroom reporter writes a\n * source-bounded brief â€” headline + single-idea findings. */\nexport const newsroomCompose: ComposeDraft = async ({ voice, term, story, context, ask }) => {\n  const papers = [story, ...context];\n  const systemPrompt = [...voice.persona, ...(voice.laws ?? [])].join(\" \");\n  const brief = await synthesizeBrief(ask, { systemPrompt, topic: term, papers });\n  const body = brief.findings.join(\"\\n\\n\");\n  const draft: PostDraft = {\n    kind: \"producing-content.article.draft\" as const,\n    title: brief.headline,\n    description: brief.headline,\n    summary: brief.findings[0] ?? brief.headline,\n    tweet: brief.headline,\n    tags: [],\n    body: body.length > 0 ? body : brief.headline,\n  };\n  return Object.freeze({ draft, copyedits: [] });\n};\n\n// ---------- angle clustering (harvested from @emsenn/newsroom) ----------\n\nconst STOP_WORDS = new Set((\"the a an of to in on for and or with from is are be been by as at into over under \" +\n  \"new using based via this that these those their its it can will more most toward across within news agent agents web\").split(\" \"));\n\n/** The content words of a string (4+ letters, stop-words removed, de-duped) â€”\n * the basis for keyword-overlap clustering. */\nexport function keywordsOf(value: unknown): readonly string[] {\n  return unique((String(value ?? \"\").toLowerCase().match(/[a-z]{4,}/g) ?? []).filter((word) => !STOP_WORDS.has(word)));\n}\n\n/** The assignment-editor prompt: from today's intake, name the N strongest\n * distinct story angles worth assigning. */\nexport function assignmentPrompt(voice: string, topic: string, articles: readonly SyndicationItem[], max = 3): string {\n  const publication = articles.map((article, index) => `${index + 1}. ${article.title}`).join(\"\\n\");\n  return `${voice.replace(/\\{topic\\}/g, topic)}\\n\\nHere is today's source intake. Name the ${max} strongest distinct story angles worth assigning, one per line.\\n\\nSource observations:\\n${publication}`;\n}\n\n/** Read the assignment editor's reply into clean angle lines, dropping\n * preamble, empty headers, and numbering. */\nexport function parseAngles(reply: unknown): readonly string[] {\n  return Object.freeze(String(reply ?? \"\").split(\"\\n\")\n    .map((line) => cleanProse(line.replace(/^[\\s>*\\-\\d.)]+/, \"\")))\n    .filter((line) => line.length > 4 && /[a-z]/i.test(line))\n    .filter((line) => !/:\\s*$/.test(line))\n    .filter((line) => !/^(here are|the following|below are|these are|sure|certainly|okay|here's)\\b/i.test(line)));\n}\n\n/** Cluster the intake by keyword overlap with an angle, returning the matching\n * source subjects in descending relevance (the newsroom's source-clustering\n * capability; used by the angle-assignment layer). */\nexport function clusterSources(angle: string, articles: readonly SyndicationItem[], sourceSubjects: readonly string[], max = 5): readonly string[] {\n  const angleKeywords = keywordsOf(angle);\n  return Object.freeze(articles\n    .map((article, index) => ({\n      source: sourceSubjects[index],\n      score: keywordsOf(article.title).filter((word) => angleKeywords.includes(word)).length,\n    }))\n    .filter((candidate): candidate is { source: string; score: number } => candidate.score > 0 && !!candidate.source)\n    .sort((left, right) => right.score - left.score)\n    .slice(0, max)\n    .map((candidate) => candidate.source));\n}\n\n// ---------- the newsroom SELECT seam ----------\n\n/** The newsroom's selection: the assigned term IS the angle. Rather than the\n * farm's term-as-lens single-story pick, the newsroom clusters the intake by\n * keyword overlap with the angle â€” the best-matching item leads, the rest of\n * the cluster is the reporter's context (the papers the brief is grounded in).\n * With no keyword match it falls back to the intake order, so the cycle always\n * settles on a story. */\nexport const newsroomSelect: SelectStory = ({ term, items }) => {\n  const angleKeywords = keywordsOf(term);\n  const scored = items\n    .map((item) => ({ item, score: keywordsOf(`${item.title} ${item.summary ?? \"\"}`).filter((word) => angleKeywords.includes(word)).length }))\n    .sort((left, right) => right.score - left.score);\n  const matched = scored.filter((entry) => entry.score > 0);\n  const ranked = matched.length > 0 ? matched : scored;\n  const story = ranked[0]?.item ?? items[0]!;\n  const context = ranked.slice(1, 6).map((entry) => entry.item);\n  return { story, context };\n};\n\n// ---------- claim-state writes (harvested from @emsenn/newsroom) ----------\n\n/** The subject atom for a source observation: prefix + the article's id (arXiv\n * abs ids stripped to their bare form), or its url/title. */\nexport function articleSubject(prefix: string, article: Pick<SyndicationItem, \"id\" | \"url\" | \"title\">): string {\n  const rawId = article.id ?? article.url ?? article.title;\n  const arxivId = typeof article.id === \"string\" ? article.id.replace(/^https?:\\/\\/arxiv\\.org\\/abs\\//, \"\") : rawId;\n  return newsSlug(`${prefix} ${arxivId}`);\n}\n\n/** The source observation as a claim write: title and url, plus abstract,\n * publication date, authors, and categories when present. */\nexport function sourceWrites(article: SyndicationItem, opts: Readonly<{ prefix?: string; citation?: string }> = {}): readonly NewsClaimWrite[] {\n  const prefix = opts.prefix ?? article.source ?? \"source\";\n  const subject = articleSubject(prefix, article);\n  const citation = opts.citation ?? article.url;\n  const claims: Record<string, unknown> = { title: article.title, url: article.url };\n  if (article.summary) claims[\"abstract\"] = article.summary;\n  if (article.published) claims[\"published\"] = article.published;\n  if (article.authors && article.authors.length > 0) claims[\"author\"] = [...article.authors];\n  if (article.categories && article.categories.length > 0) claims[\"category\"] = [...article.categories];\n  return Object.freeze([write(subject, claims, citation)]);\n}\n\n/** A brief as claim writes: the headline cell and (when present) the findings\n * cell, both cited to the reporter. */\nexport function briefWrites(subject: string, brief: StructuredBrief, citation = \"newsroom:reporter\"): readonly NewsClaimWrite[] {\n  const writes: NewsClaimWrite[] = [write(subject, { headline: brief.headline }, citation)];\n  if (brief.findings.length > 0) writes.push(write(subject, { finds: [...brief.findings] }, citation));\n  return Object.freeze(writes);\n}\n\n// ---------- the newsroom RECORD seam ----------\n\n/** The newsroom's witness: instead of a markdown candidate, the cycle's\n * settlement is carried out as a claim-state ASSERTION TRACE (the canonical\n * carrier â€” every source observation, the angle and its draws-on links, the\n * brief, and each editorial gate, charged) and the CLAIM SETS folded back out\n * of it. Per the news-topology theory the trace is canonical and prose is the\n * lossy projection. */\nexport type NewsroomCultivation = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.newsroom-cultivation:witness\";\n  property: string;\n  assignment: DemandEntry;\n  story: SyndicationItem;\n  draft: PostDraft;\n  gates: readonly FarmGate[];\n  shipped: boolean;\n  assertionTrace: NewsAssertionTrace;\n  claimSets: readonly NewsClaimSet[];\n  path: string | null;\n  usage: Readonly<{ calls: number; tokens: number }>;\n  intakeObstructions: readonly string[];\n}>;\n\n/** The newsroom's recording: fold the settlement into claim writes â€” source\n * observations for the story and its context, the angle and its draws-on\n * links, the brief, the editorial gates (a failed gate is a denial), and the\n * published status when shipped â€” assert them into a trace, and fold the trace\n * back into per-subject claim sets. Persists the trace + claim sets as JSON\n * under the property's data dir unless dry-run. */\nexport const newsroomRecord: RecordCycle<NewsroomCultivation> = (settlement: CycleSettlement, ctx) => {\n  const { property, assignment, story, context, draft, gates, shipped, usage, intakeObstructions } = settlement;\n  const papers = [story, ...context];\n  const subject = newsSlug(draft.title).split(\"-\").slice(0, 9).join(\"-\") || `${property.name}-${ctx.at}`;\n  const citation = `newsroom:${property.name}`;\n  const sourceSubjects = unique(papers.map((item) => articleSubject(item.source ?? \"source\", item)));\n  const findings = sentences(draft.body);\n  const writes: NewsClaimWrite[] = [\n    ...papers.flatMap((item) => sourceWrites(item, { prefix: item.source ?? \"source\" })),\n    write(subject, { angle: assignment.term, \"draws-on\": sourceSubjects }, citation),\n    ...briefWrites(subject, { headline: draft.title, findings }, citation),\n    ...gates.map((gate) => clearGate(subject, gate.gate, gate.charge === \"pass\", `desk:${gate.gate}`)),\n    ...(shipped ? [write(subject, { status: \"published\", \"published-at\": ctx.at }, citation)] : []),\n  ];\n  const assertionTrace = assertionsFromWrites(writes, { transaction: ctx.at });\n  const claimSets = claimSetsFromTrace(assertionTrace);\n  let path: string | null = null;\n  if (!ctx.dryRun) {\n    const dir = join(property.dataDir, \"newsroom\");\n    mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });\n    path = join(dir, `${subject}.json`);\n    writeFileSync(path, `${JSON.stringify({ subject, shipped, assertionTrace, claimSets })}\\n`, \"utf8\");\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"content.farm.newsroom-cultivation:witness\" as const,\n    property: property.name,\n    assignment,\n    story,\n    draft,\n    gates,\n    shipped,\n    assertionTrace,\n    claimSets,\n    path,\n    usage,\n    intakeObstructions,\n  });\n};\n\n// ---------- the newsroom CYCLE (the N-angles-per-run orchestration) ----------\n\n/** Ask the assignment editor for the strongest distinct angles in today's\n * intake. The assignment is the OUTER layer of the newsroom fiber: it chooses\n * WHICH angles to run; the per-angle cultivation (selection â†’ composition â†’\n * recording) is one base cycle each. With no `ask`, the lead titles stand in;\n * an empty result still yields a roundup angle so a non-empty intake always\n * produces at least one story. */\nexport async function assignAngles(\n  voice: string,\n  topic: string,\n  items: readonly SyndicationItem[],\n  max = 3,\n  ask?: Ask,\n): Promise<readonly string[]> {\n  const reply = ask ? await ask(assignmentPrompt(voice, topic, items, max)) : items.slice(0, max).map((item) => item.title).join(\"\\n\");\n  const seen = new Set<string>();\n  const angles: string[] = [];\n  for (const angle of parseAngles(reply)) {\n    const key = newsSlug(angle).split(\"-\").slice(0, 8).join(\"-\");\n    if (!key || seen.has(key)) continue;\n    seen.add(key);\n    angles.push(angle);\n    if (angles.length >= max) break;\n  }\n  if (angles.length === 0 && items.length > 0) angles.push(`What's new in ${topic}`);\n  return Object.freeze(angles);\n}\n\n/** Options for the newsroom cycle: the clock stamp, the beat to draw intake\n * from, the assignment topic and story bound, and the cultivation I/O. */\nexport type NewsroomCycleOptions = Readonly<{\n  at: string;\n  beat?: string;\n  topic?: string;\n  maxStories?: number;\n  ask?: Ask;\n  fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n  adapters?: Readonly<Record<string, SyndicationAdapter>>;\n  dryRun?: boolean;\n  provider?: string;\n  model?: string;\n  env?: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;\n}>;\n\n/** One newsroom cycle for a property: fetch the beat's intake ONCE, assign the\n * day's angles over it, then run the base cultivation cycle once per angle with\n * the newsroom seams (angle selection, brief composition, assertion-trace\n * recording). The witnessed stories and the per-angle obstructions are both\n * returned as values. */\nexport type NewsroomCycleResult = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"content.farm.newsroom-cycle:witness\";\n  property: string;\n  angles: readonly string[];\n  intakeObstructions: readonly string[];\n  stories: readonly NewsroomCultivation[];\n  obstructions: readonly CultivationObstruction[];\n}>;\n\n/** Run one newsroom cycle. See NewsroomCycleResult. */\nexport async function runNewsroomCycle(property: FarmProperty, options: NewsroomCycleOptions): Promise<NewsroomCycleResult> {\n  const beat = options.beat ?? Object.keys(property.beatSources)[0] ?? \"\";\n  const intake = await intakeItems(property, beat, {\n    ...(options.fetchImpl === undefined ? {} : { fetchImpl: options.fetchImpl }),\n    ...(options.adapters === undefined ? {} : { adapters: options.adapters }),\n  });\n  const topic = options.topic ?? property.termKey ?? property.name;\n  const voice = [...property.voice.persona, ...(property.voice.laws ?? [])].join(\" \");\n  const angles = await assignAngles(voice, topic, intake.items, options.maxStories ?? 3, options.ask);\n  const stories: NewsroomCultivation[] = [];\n  const obstructions: CultivationObstruction[] = [];\n  for (const angle of angles) {\n    const result = await cultivate(property, {\n      at: options.at,\n      term: angle,\n      beat,\n      items: intake.items,\n      ...(options.ask === undefined ? {} : { ask: options.ask }),\n      ...(options.dryRun === undefined ? {} : { dryRun: options.dryRun }),\n      ...(options.provider === undefined ? {} : { provider: options.provider }),\n      ...(options.model === undefined ? {} : { model: options.model }),\n      ...(options.env === undefined ? {} : { env: options.env }),\n      select: newsroomSelect,\n      compose: newsroomCompose,\n      record: newsroomRecord,\n    });\n    if (result.kind === \"content.farm.newsroom-cultivation:witness\") stories.push(result);\n    else obstructions.push(result);\n  }\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"content.farm.newsroom-cycle:witness\" as const,\n    property: property.name,\n    angles,\n    intakeObstructions: intake.obstructions,\n    stories: Object.freeze(stories),\n    obstructions: Object.freeze(obstructions),\n  });\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"production/article-topology","digest":"sha256:79f3758c4fdb5e9bede4457719540bd54a5904b3e110fa77fb1b79d80742b85f","content":"ï»¿/**\n * article Ã¢â‚¬â€ the news-document production surface: the voice-parameterized\n * article prompt, the structured-reply parser, story selection from an\n * intake, and the mechanical editorial gates.\n *\n * This is the news specialization of document production Ã¢â‚¬â€ the sibling of\n * ./documentation (documents about CODE) turned on NEWS. It is pure over\n * its inputs: the prompt is built from a voice value and a source story,\n * the reply is parsed, the draft is gated. The I/O (calling the model,\n * fetching the intake, writing the candidate, recording the run) belongs\n * to the cycle that owns it Ã¢â‚¬â€ content-farm Ã¢â‚¬â€ exactly as documentation's\n * I/O belongs to its app. News Ã¢Å â€š document Ã¢Å â€š content: the parse/gate\n * primitives live here in the content library, beside the doc ones, never\n * in the service.\n *\n * Following documentation.mts's discipline, the source story enters as a\n * structural subset (`SourceStory`), so this module never depends on the\n * intake transport (syndication-codecs); the cycle passes its richer items\n * in and `selectStory` returns them unchanged via the element type.\n */\n\nimport { carrierFrom, carrierToTerm, type Tm, type FromSchema } from \"@emsenn/relating\";\nimport { jsonResource } from \"@emsenn/relating/writing\";\nimport {\n  GateChargeSchema,\n  gateCharge,\n  normalizeTerm,\n  wordCount,\n  extractLinks,\n  prophecyFindings,\n  repetitionFindings,\n  proseLintErrors,\n  extractNumbers,\n  numberFaithfulnessFindings,\n  type GateCharge,\n} from \"./gates.mjs\";\n\n// ---------- the source story (a structural subset of an intake item) ----------\n\nexport const SourceStorySchema = { $id: \"producing-content/topology/article/source-story\", title: \"SourceStory\", additionalProperties: true, type: \"object\", required: [\"title\", \"url\"], properties: { title: { minLength: 1, type: \"string\" }, url: { minLength: 1, type: \"string\" }, summary: { type: \"string\" }, published: { type: \"string\" } } } as const;\n\nexport const PostDraftSchema = { $id: \"producing-content/topology/article/post-draft\", title: \"PostDraft\", additionalProperties: false, type: \"object\", required: [\"kind\", \"title\", \"description\", \"summary\", \"tweet\", \"tags\", \"body\"], properties: { kind: { const: \"producing-content.article.draft\" }, title: { minLength: 1, type: \"string\" }, description: { type: \"string\" }, summary: { type: \"string\" }, tweet: { type: \"string\" }, tags: { type: \"array\", items: { type: \"string\" } }, body: { type: \"string\" } } } as const;\n\nexport const ArticleVoiceSchema = { $id: \"producing-content/topology/article/voice\", title: \"ArticleVoice\", additionalProperties: false, type: \"object\", required: [\"persona\"], properties: { persona: { type: \"array\", items: { type: \"string\" } }, exemplar: { type: \"array\", items: { type: \"string\" } }, laws: { type: \"array\", items: { type: \"string\" } } } } as const;\n\nexport const ArticleGateReportSchema = { $id: \"producing-content/topology/article/gate-report\", title: \"ArticleGateReport\", additionalProperties: false, type: \"object\", required: [\"kind\", \"draft\", \"term\", \"gates\"], properties: { kind: { const: \"producing-content.article.gate-report\" }, draft: PostDraftSchema, term: { minLength: 1, type: \"string\" }, gates: { type: \"array\", items: GateChargeSchema } } } as const;\n\nexport type SourceStoryCarrier = FromSchema<typeof SourceStorySchema>;\nexport type PostDraftCarrier = FromSchema<typeof PostDraftSchema>;\nexport type ArticleVoiceCarrier = FromSchema<typeof ArticleVoiceSchema>;\nexport type ArticleGateReportCarrier = FromSchema<typeof ArticleGateReportSchema>;\n\nexport const sourceStoryCarrier = carrierFrom(SourceStorySchema);\n\nexport const postDraftCarrier = carrierFrom(PostDraftSchema);\n\nexport const articleVoiceCarrier = carrierFrom(ArticleVoiceSchema);\n\nexport const articleGateReportCarrier = carrierFrom(ArticleGateReportSchema);\n\n/** The minimal shape of a source story the article surface needs Ã¢â‚¬â€ a\n * structural subset of a SyndicationItem, so this module never depends on\n * the intake transport (mirrors documentation.mts's DocumentableSymbol). */\nexport type SourceStory = Readonly<{\n  title: string;\n  url: string;\n  summary?: string;\n  published?: string;\n  authors?: readonly string[];\n  source?: string;\n}>;\n\n// ---------- the draft and the voice ----------\n\n/**\n * A produced news draft, parsed from a structured model reply: the headline,\n * the meta description, the summary, the share tweet, the topic tags, and the\n * markdown body.\n */\nexport type PostDraft = Readonly<{\n  kind: \"producing-content.article.draft\";\n  title: string;\n  description: string;\n  summary: string;\n  tweet: string;\n  tags: readonly string[];\n  body: string;\n}>;\n\n/** A property's editorial voice, as a value. `persona` opens the prompt;\n * `laws` are property craft laws spliced into the universal ones;\n * `exemplar` replaces the default form exemplar. */\nexport type ArticleVoice = Readonly<{\n  persona: readonly string[];\n  exemplar?: readonly string[];\n  laws?: readonly string[];\n}>;\n\n/** The default FORM exemplar: shape guidance (a lede that names the event,\n * concrete numbers, a three-sentence summary), never words to reuse. */\nexport const FORM_EXEMPLAR_DEFAULT: readonly string[] = Object.freeze([\n  \"FORM EXEMPLAR (match the SHAPE Ã¢â‚¬â€ a lede that names the event, sections that build one analysis, concrete numbers Ã¢â‚¬â€ never reuse its words):\",\n  \"  Lede: \\\"In a move that's rattling global markets, Thailand has abruptly halted rice exports due to an escalating water crisis, prompting fears of a new wave of food price surges and shortages. This isn't just a Thai issue. It's a warning flare for the whole world. The Thailand rice export ban is a fracture in the illusion of a globally integrated food system.\\\"\",\n  \"  Description: \\\"Thailand's rice export ban amid drought signals a global food system in breakdown. Here's how it affects us all.\\\" (113 characters)\",\n  \"  Summary (three full sentences, 53 words Ã¢â‚¬â€ the event, the numbers, the frame): \\\"Thailand's rice export ban due to historic drought is triggering global price spikes and exposing the fragility of our food systems. This collapse in rice trade reflects a deeper crisis: climate volatility is unraveling global agriculture. The illusion of stable food supply chains is breakingÃ¢â‚¬â€one drought, one policy, at a time.\\\"\",\n]);\n\n// ---------- the prompt ----------\n\n/**\n * The inputs to draftPrompt: the property's voice, the SEO lens term, the\n * single source story the article is about, and optional secondary stories\n * carried as supporting context.\n */\nexport type DraftPromptInput = Readonly<{\n  voice: ArticleVoice;\n  term: string;\n  story: SourceStory;\n  context?: readonly SourceStory[];\n}>;\n\nfunction sourceText(story: SourceStory, maximum: number): string {\n  const text = String(story.summary ?? \"\")\n    .replaceAll(\"&nbsp;\", \" \")\n    .replaceAll(\"&amp;\", \"&\")\n    .replaceAll(\"&quot;\", '\"')\n    .replaceAll(\"&#39;\", \"'\")\n    .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n    .trim();\n  if (text.length <= maximum) return text;\n  return `${text.slice(0, maximum).trimEnd()}\\n    [SOURCE TEXT TRUNCATED AFTER ${maximum} CHARACTERS]`;\n}\n\nfunction sourceMetadata(story: SourceStory): readonly string[] {\n  return Object.freeze([\n    ...(story.authors === undefined || story.authors.length === 0 ? [] : [`    BYLINE: ${story.authors.join(\", \")}`]),\n    ...(story.published === undefined ? [] : [`    PUBLISHED: ${story.published}`]),\n    ...(story.source === undefined ? [] : [`    CARRIER: ${story.source}`]),\n  ]);\n}\n\n/** The article prompt: one story, one developed argument, judged by the\n * gates the laws below describe. The lead craft laws end at repetition;\n * the property's own laws splice in before the closing (prophecy, voice,\n * ending, no-h1) laws Ã¢â‚¬â€ matching the koios mill's original order. */\nexport function draftPrompt(input: DraftPromptInput): string {\n  const supporting = (input.context ?? []).map((article, index) =>\n    `[CONTEXT ${index + 1}] ${article.title}\\n    URL: ${article.url}\\n${sourceMetadata(article).join(\"\\n\")}\\n    SOURCE TEXT:\\n    ${sourceText(article, 6_000)}`).join(\"\\n\\n\");\n  const head = [\n    ...input.voice.persona,\n    \"\",\n    `SEARCH-DEMAND LENS (a subject signal, not mandatory copy): \"${input.term}\"`,\n    \"Express the lens's material ideas in natural language. Do not force its exact wording into a sentence, headline, subheading, description, summary, or share copy when that wording is awkward or unsupported.\",\n    \"If the lens contains a geographic, demographic, or identity category, use that category only for people and places the reporting explicitly places within it. Never stretch the category across every example merely to satisfy search demand.\",\n    \"\",\n    ...(input.voice.exemplar ?? FORM_EXEMPLAR_DEFAULT),\n    \"\",\n    \"THE STORY (the single subject Ã¢â‚¬â€ the WHOLE article is about this one event):\",\n    \"\",\n    `${input.story.title}\\n    URL: ${input.story.url}`,\n    ...sourceMetadata(input.story),\n    \"    SOURCE TEXT:\",\n    `    ${sourceText(input.story, 16_000)}`,\n    ...(() => {\n      const nums = [...extractNumbers(`${input.story.title} ${String(input.story.summary ?? \"\")}`)];\n      return nums.length > 0 ? [`    Factual numbers (faithfulness gate Ã¢â‚¬â€ reproduce these exactly, never invent others): ${nums.join(\", \")}`] : [];\n    })(),\n    ...(supporting === \"\" ? [] : [\n      \"\",\n      \"SUPPORTING CONTEXT (separate reporting, not evidence that its people, region, causes, or conclusions are the same as THE STORY):\",\n      \"\",\n      supporting,\n      \"\",\n      \"A context item may supply one explicitly attributed fact or comparison only when its own text warrants that use. Never fuse contexts, transfer an attribution between them, infer a shared identity or causal connection, or give a context item a section of its own. Ignore context that does not sharpen the subject story safely.\",\n    ]),\n    \"\",\n    \"If this reporting packet cannot support a specific, fully attributed article without invented connective tissue, output only `REPORTING_NEEDED: <the exact missing reporting>` and stop. Refusal is correct work.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Write one article about THE STORY Ã¢â‚¬â€ one event, one developed argument, never a roundup:\",\n    \"- aim for 800 to 1,100 words only when the supplied reporting supports that length; never pad to reach a quota\",\n    \"- use three to five ## sections sized by the actual reporting, with concrete detail rather than repeated framing\",\n    \"- the lede (the first one or two paragraphs, 100-150 words) comes BEFORE the first ## subheader\",\n    \"- the very first sentence names the event concretely (who, where, what) and naturally expresses the search-demand lens when the reporting warrants it\",\n    \"- the headline preserves the source event's own organizing terms; the demand lens may add an accurately supported term but must never substitute for conflict, drought, collapse, or another frame the reported event itself is organized around\",\n    \"- never repeat a noun phrase of five or more words: after its first mention, the event takes a short handle (\\\"the ban\\\", \\\"the hike\\\", \\\"the retreat\\\")\",\n    \"- each section CONTINUES the previous one's argument; never reintroduce or restate the event Ã¢â‚¬â€ assume the reader read the lede\",\n    \"- three to five ## sections that BUILD a single analysis of this event: what happened and why, where the faultline runs, who absorbs the damage first, what it opens onto Ã¢â‚¬â€ never one section per source\",\n    \"- use a natural subheading that expresses the search-demand lens when it belongs to the story; exact-match phrasing is never required\",\n    \"- work concrete details from the source text into the prose Ã¢â‚¬â€ institutions, numbers, places, names; never invent events, numbers, quotes, people, relationships, or causal mechanisms\",\n    \"- CITATION IS A PRECONDITION: cite THE STORY as [Exact Story Title](its-exact-url) before making its first factual claim; a draft with no exact story link is refused, not finished\",\n    \"- every factual paragraph must make its reporting source unambiguous through an inline source link or an immediately continuing attribution; cite supporting context only when you actually use a warranted fact from it; never cite anything not listed above\",\n    \"- every citation must be part of a grammatical prose sentence; never drop a standalone hyperlinked source title between paragraphs\",\n    \"- names appearing in a byline, copyright notice, syndication notice, or publication header are authors or carriers, not people in the reported event unless the source text separately attributes an action, role, or quotation to them\",\n    \"- NEVER alter a quoted source title by even one word: link text that quotes a title reproduces it exactly, character for character\",\n    \"- template sentences are banned: the construction \\\"As [X] notes/reports, ...\\\" may appear AT MOST twice in the whole article; vary how sources enter your prose\",\n    \"- vary the opening sentence of every section; no two sections open with the same construction\",\n    \"- never repeat a sentence; never reuse the same long phrase; when you need more length, add NEW concrete material from the sources Ã¢â‚¬â€ never restatements of points already made\",\n    ...(input.voice.laws ?? []),\n    \"- a vision is never a prophecy: write \\\"might\\\" or \\\"could\\\". NEVER use the word \\\"will\\\" outside a quoted source title\",\n    \"- no rhetorical questions, anywhere; every sentence is a statement\",\n    \"- the ending opens outward Ã¢â‚¬â€ what this event makes visible or possible Ã¢â‚¬â€ without predicting anything\",\n    \"- do not include an h1 title line in the body\",\n  ];\n  const format = [\n    \"\",\n    \"Output EXACTLY this structure (no preamble, no commentary):\",\n    \"TITLE: <descriptive headline about the story's event>\",\n    \"DESCRIPTION: <aim for 125 characters of faithful meta description (the gate window is 100-150 characters)>\",\n    \"SUMMARY: <aim for 55 words in three or four FULL faithful sentences (the gate window is 40-70 words)>\",\n    \"TWEET: <one faithful share sentence with one emoji, no hashtags>\",\n    \"TAGS: <three to five comma-separated topic tags>\",\n    \"BODY:\",\n    \"<the markdown body>\",\n  ];\n  return [...head, ...format].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/** Parse a structured drafting reply into its five surfaces and body.\n * A reply with no TITLE (or no body at all) is a malformed shape Ã¢â‚¬â€ thrown,\n * per the substrate law that exceptions are for malformed shapes only. */\nexport function parseDraftReply(reply: string): PostDraft {\n  const text = String(reply ?? \"\").replaceAll(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\");\n  const grab = (label: string): string => {\n    const match = text.match(new RegExp(`^${label}:\\\\s*(.+)$`, \"im\"));\n    return (match?.[1] ?? \"\").trim().replace(/^[\"']|[\"']$/g, \"\");\n  };\n  // The body is everything after the BODY: marker; models that skip the\n  // marker get read from the line after the last header instead.\n  let bodyStart = text.search(/^BODY:/im);\n  if (bodyStart >= 0) {\n    bodyStart = text.indexOf(\":\", bodyStart) + 1;\n  } else {\n    let lastHeaderEnd = -1;\n    for (const label of [\"TITLE\", \"DESCRIPTION\", \"SUMMARY\", \"TWEET\", \"TAGS\"]) {\n      const match = text.match(new RegExp(`^${label}:[^\\\\n]*\\\\n`, \"im\"));\n      if (match?.index !== undefined) lastHeaderEnd = Math.max(lastHeaderEnd, match.index + match[0].length);\n    }\n    if (lastHeaderEnd < 0) throw new TypeError(\"draft reply has no BODY section\");\n    bodyStart = lastHeaderEnd;\n  }\n  let body = text.slice(bodyStart).trim();\n  body = body.replace(/^#\\s+[^\\n]+\\n+/, \"\"); // the page supplies the headline\n  const title = grab(\"TITLE\");\n  if (!title) throw new TypeError(\"draft reply has no TITLE\");\n  return Object.freeze({\n    kind: \"producing-content.article.draft\" as const,\n    title,\n    description: grab(\"DESCRIPTION\"),\n    summary: grab(\"SUMMARY\"),\n    tweet: grab(\"TWEET\"),\n    tags: Object.freeze(grab(\"TAGS\").split(\",\").map((tag) => tag.trim().toLowerCase()).filter(Boolean).slice(0, 5)),\n    body,\n  });\n}\n\n// ---------- story selection ----------\n\nconst NO_GENERIC_WORDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set();\nconst DEMAND_STOP_WORDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\"and\", \"are\", \"for\", \"from\", \"into\", \"the\", \"this\", \"with\"]);\n\n/** The words of a term that distinguish its story from the beat at large.\n * `genericWords` are the property's beat-generic words, which prove nothing. */\nexport function distinctiveTermWords(term: string, genericWords: ReadonlySet<string> = NO_GENERIC_WORDS): ReadonlySet<string> {\n  return new Set(normalizeTerm(term).split(\" \").filter((word) => word.length > 2 && !DEMAND_STOP_WORDS.has(word) && !genericWords.has(word)));\n}\n\n/** How many intake articles support a term, judged on the term's\n * distinctive words (beat-generic words prove nothing). A term with no\n * distinctive words is supported by the whole intake. */\nexport function termSupportCount(term: string, articles: readonly SourceStory[], genericWords: ReadonlySet<string> = NO_GENERIC_WORDS): number {\n  const distinctive = distinctiveTermWords(term, genericWords);\n  if (distinctive.size === 0) return articles.length;\n  return articles.filter((article) =>\n    normalizeTerm(`${article.title} ${article.summary ?? \"\"}`).split(\" \").some((word) => distinctive.has(word))).length;\n}\n\n/**\n * Options for selectStory: contextMax caps how many secondary intake items\n * travel as supporting context (default 2), and genericWords are the property's\n * beat-generic words, against which distinctive term words count double.\n */\nexport type SelectStoryOptions = Readonly<{\n  /** At most this many secondary items travel as supporting context. Default 2. */\n  contextMax?: number;\n  /** The property's beat-generic words; distinctive term words count double. */\n  genericWords?: ReadonlySet<string>;\n}>;\n\n/** The assignment unit is the story, not the keyword: the demand term is the\n * lens, and it selects from the witnessed intake the SINGLE best-supporting\n * source story as the article's subject. Stories are scored by term-word\n * overlap in title+summary Ã¢â‚¬â€ distinctive term words (those that separate the\n * term's event from the beat at large) count double, because matching the\n * beat proves nothing about the event Ã¢â‚¬â€ with recency as the tiebreak. At most\n * `contextMax` other intake items travel as supporting context, secondary by\n * law. Calling with no intake is a malformed call and throws; the cultivate\n * cycle witnesses empty intake as an obstruction before ever selecting.\n *\n * Generic over the story type so the caller's richer intake item (e.g. a\n * SyndicationItem) is returned unchanged, not narrowed to SourceStory. */\nexport function selectStory<T extends SourceStory>(term: string, articles: readonly T[], options: SelectStoryOptions = {}): Readonly<{ story: T; context: readonly T[] }> {\n  if (articles.length === 0) throw new TypeError(\"no witnessed intake to select a story from\");\n  const contextMax = options.contextMax ?? 2;\n  const termWords = new Set(normalizeTerm(term).split(\" \").filter(Boolean));\n  const distinctive = distinctiveTermWords(term, options.genericWords ?? NO_GENERIC_WORDS);\n  const support = (article: SourceStory): number => {\n    const words = new Set(normalizeTerm(`${article.title} ${article.summary ?? \"\"}`).split(\" \"));\n    let score = 0;\n    for (const word of words) {\n      if (termWords.has(word)) score += 1;\n      if (distinctive.has(word)) score += 2;\n    }\n    return score;\n  };\n  const recency = (article: SourceStory): number => {\n    const time = Date.parse(article.published ?? \"\");\n    return Number.isNaN(time) ? 0 : time;\n  };\n  const ranked = [...articles]\n    .map((article) => ({ article, score: support(article) }))\n    .sort((left, right) => right.score - left.score || recency(right.article) - recency(left.article));\n  return Object.freeze({\n    story: ranked[0]!.article,\n    // Context must bear on the term: an item sharing no term word is not\n    // context, it is an unrelated story Ã¢â‚¬â€ an irrelevant link is worse than none.\n    context: Object.freeze(ranked.slice(1).filter((entry) => entry.score > 0).slice(0, contextMax).map((entry) => entry.article)),\n  });\n}\n\n// ---------- the gates ----------\n\n/** One gate's reading of a draft: a charge with its evidence. */\nexport type FarmGate = GateCharge;\n\nfunction sameUrl(left: string, right: string): boolean {\n  return left.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\") === right.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\");\n}\n\n/** Title-fidelity findings: a markdown link whose text presents itself as a\n * witnessed intake title (high word overlap) but does not match it exactly Ã¢â‚¬â€\n * the \"will likely\" Ã¢â€ â€™ \"could likely\" alteration class. */\nexport function titleFidelityFindings(body: string, intakeTitles: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  const findings: string[] = [];\n  const tidy = (value: string): string => value.trim().replace(/[.!?Ã¢â‚¬Â¦]+$/, \"\");\n  const linkTexts = [...body.matchAll(/\\[([^\\]]+)\\]\\([^)]*\\)/g)].map((match) => (match[1] ?? \"\").trim());\n  for (const text of linkTexts) {\n    if (intakeTitles.some((title) => tidy(title) === tidy(text))) continue;\n    const textWords = new Set(normalizeTerm(text).split(\" \").filter(Boolean));\n    for (const title of intakeTitles) {\n      const titleWords = normalizeTerm(title).split(\" \").filter(Boolean);\n      if (titleWords.length < 4) continue;\n      const shared = titleWords.filter((word) => textWords.has(word)).length;\n      const similarity = shared / Math.max(titleWords.length, textWords.size);\n      if (similarity >= 0.6) {\n        findings.push(`link text \"${text}\" alters the witnessed title \"${title}\" Ã¢â‚¬â€ quote it exactly or rephrase as plain prose`);\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return Object.freeze(findings);\n}\n\n/** Count of the banned template construction: As [link] notes/reports/suggests/writes, Ã¢â‚¬Â¦ */\nexport function templateMonotonyCount(body: string): number {\n  return [...body.matchAll(/\\bAs\\s+\\[[^\\]]+\\]\\([^)]*\\)\\s+(?:notes|reports|suggests|writes)\\b/gi)].length;\n}\n\n/**\n * The witnessed-intake context that sharpens judgeDraft: storyUrl (the single\n * story that must be cited), intakeTitles (for the title-fidelity gate), and\n * intakeTexts (title plus summary per article, for the numbers-faithfulness\n * gate).\n */\nexport type JudgeContext = Readonly<{\n  /** The single story's URL Ã¢â‚¬â€ it must be cited; other intake citations are supporting context, at most two. */\n  storyUrl?: string;\n  /** Witnessed intake titles, for the title-fidelity gate. */\n  intakeTitles?: readonly string[];\n  /** Witnessed intake texts (title + summary per article), for the numbers-faithfulness gate. */\n  intakeTexts?: readonly string[];\n}>;\n\n/** The thirteen gates, judged in one pass. Every gate reports a charge with\n * evidence; the caller folds the verdict (all-pass = gates clear). */\nexport function judgeDraft(draft: PostDraft, term: string, citableUrls: readonly string[], context: JudgeContext = {}): readonly FarmGate[] {\n  const gates: FarmGate[] = [];\n  const judge = (gate: string, pass: boolean, evidence: string): void => {\n    gates.push(gateCharge(gate, pass ? \"pass\" : \"fail\", evidence));\n  };\n  const words = wordCount(draft.body);\n  judge(\"length\", words >= 600, words >= 600 ? `${words} words (needs Ã¢â€°Â¥ 600)` : `${words} words (needs Ã¢â€°Â¥ 600) Ã¢â‚¬â€ the reporting packet does not yet support a substantial article; obtain more reporting instead of padding`);\n  const firstParagraph = draft.body\n    .split(\"\\n\")\n    .filter((line) => !/^#{1,6}\\s/.test(line))\n    .join(\"\\n\")\n    .split(/\\n\\s*\\n/)\n    .map((block) => block.trim())\n    .find((block) => block !== \"\") ?? \"\";\n  const normalTerm = normalizeTerm(term);\n  const demandWords = [...distinctiveTermWords(term)].filter((word) => word.length > 2);\n  const demandCoverage = (text: string): number => {\n    const words = new Set(normalizeTerm(text).split(\" \"));\n    return demandWords.filter((word) => words.has(word)).length;\n  };\n  // Demand selects the assignment; it is not evidence and must never compel\n  // a geographic or identity category into the lede. One natural concept is\n  // enough for the cheap relevance gate; the Editor judges faithfulness.\n  const requiredDemandWords = Math.min(1, demandWords.length);\n  const ledeCoverage = demandCoverage(firstParagraph);\n  judge(\"seo-lede\", ledeCoverage >= requiredDemandWords, `${ledeCoverage}/${demandWords.length} distinctive demand word(s) from \"${term}\" appear naturally in the first paragraph (needs ${requiredDemandWords})`);\n  const subheads = draft.body.split(\"\\n\").filter((line) => /^##\\s/.test(line));\n  const headingCoverage = Math.max(0, ...subheads.map(demandCoverage));\n  const requiredHeadingWords = Math.min(1, demandWords.length);\n  judge(\"seo-subheader\", headingCoverage >= requiredHeadingWords, `${headingCoverage}/${demandWords.length} distinctive demand word(s) from \"${term}\" appear naturally in one ## subheader (${subheads.length} subheaders; needs ${requiredHeadingWords})`);\n  const termOccurrences = [...normalizeTerm(draft.body).matchAll(new RegExp(normalTerm.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$&\"), \"g\"))].length;\n  judge(\"seo-frequency\", termOccurrences <= 5, termOccurrences <= 5 ? `SEO term appears ${termOccurrences}Ãƒâ€” (Ã¢â€°Â¤ 5)` : `SEO term appears ${termOccurrences}Ãƒâ€” (hard limit 5) Ã¢â‚¬â€ reduce stuffing; weave it into sentences, never stamp it in every paragraph`);\n  const links = extractLinks(draft.body);\n  const external = links.filter((link) => /^https?:\\/\\//i.test(link));\n  const cited = new Set(external.filter((link) => citableUrls.some((url) => sameUrl(link, url))));\n  const foreign = external.filter((link) => !citableUrls.some((url) => sameUrl(link, url)));\n  const storyCited = context.storyUrl === undefined || [...cited].some((link) => sameUrl(link, context.storyUrl ?? \"\"));\n  const supportingCited = context.storyUrl === undefined ? 0 : [...cited].filter((link) => !sameUrl(link, context.storyUrl ?? \"\")).length;\n  const citationProblems = [\n    ...(foreign.length > 0 ? [`links outside the witnessed intake: ${foreign.join(\", \")}`] : []),\n    ...(cited.size < 1 ? [`the witnessed subject story must be cited inline`] : []),\n    ...(storyCited ? [] : [`the story itself (${context.storyUrl}) is never cited`]),\n    ...(supportingCited > 2 ? [`${supportingCited} supporting citations (at most 2 besides the story)`] : []),\n  ];\n  judge(\"citations\", citationProblems.length === 0,\n    citationProblems.length === 0 ? `story cited, ${supportingCited} supporting citation(s), all within the witnessed intake` : citationProblems.join(\"; \"));\n  const prophecies = prophecyFindings(draft.body);\n  judge(\"no-prophecy\", prophecies.length === 0, prophecies.length === 0 ? \"no certainty about the future\" : `${prophecies.length} prophecy-shaped sentence(s) Ã¢â‚¬â€ rewrite each as might/could: ${prophecies.slice(0, 8).map((sentence) => sentence.slice(0, 110)).join(\" | \")}`);\n  const repetitions = repetitionFindings(draft.body);\n  judge(\"repetition\", repetitions.length === 0,\n    repetitions.length === 0 ? \"no repeated sentences or 8-word phrases\" : repetitions.slice(0, 4).join(\" | \"));\n  const summaryWords = wordCount(draft.summary);\n  const descriptionChars = draft.description.length;\n  judge(\"summary-length\", summaryWords >= 40 && summaryWords <= 70 && descriptionChars >= 100 && descriptionChars <= 150,\n    `summary ${summaryWords} words (needs 40-70 Ã¢â‚¬â€ three or four FULL sentences, like the exemplar, never fragments); description ${descriptionChars} chars (needs 100-150 Ã¢â‚¬â€ one full sentence plus a clause)`);\n  const infidelities = titleFidelityFindings(draft.body, context.intakeTitles ?? []);\n  judge(\"title-fidelity\", infidelities.length === 0,\n    infidelities.length === 0 ? \"every quoted source title matches the witnessed intake exactly\" : infidelities.slice(0, 3).join(\" | \"));\n  const templated = templateMonotonyCount(draft.body);\n  judge(\"template-monotony\", templated <= 2,\n    `${templated} \"As [source] notes/reports/suggests/writes, Ã¢â‚¬Â¦\" sentence(s) (at most 2; vary how sources enter the prose)`);\n  const errors = proseLintErrors(draft.body);\n  judge(\"prose\", errors.length === 0, errors.length === 0 ? \"no prose-lint errors\" : `${errors.length} prose-lint error(s): ${errors.slice(0, 3).join(\" | \")}`);\n  const numberFindings = numberFaithfulnessFindings(draft.body, context.intakeTexts ?? []);\n  judge(\n    \"numbers-faithfulness\",\n    numberFindings.length === 0,\n    numberFindings.length === 0\n      ? \"all draft numbers appear in the witnessed intake\"\n      : `${numberFindings.length} number(s) not in the witnessed intake: ${numberFindings.join(\", \")} Ã¢â‚¬â€ only use numbers from the source summaries`,\n  );\n  judge(\"extras\", draft.description.length > 0 && draft.summary.length > 0 && draft.tweet.length > 0,\n    \"description, summary, and tweet present\");\n  return Object.freeze(gates);\n}\n\nexport function encodeSourceStory(story: SourceStory): SourceStoryCarrier {\n  return sourceStoryCarrier.assert({\n    title: story.title,\n    url: story.url,\n    ...(story.summary === undefined ? {} : { summary: story.summary }),\n    ...(story.published === undefined ? {} : { published: story.published }),\n    ...(story.authors === undefined ? {} : { authors: [...story.authors] }),\n    ...(story.source === undefined ? {} : { source: story.source }),\n  }, \"source story\");\n}\n\nexport function encodePostDraft(draft: PostDraft): PostDraftCarrier {\n  return postDraftCarrier.assert({\n    kind: \"producing-content.article.draft\" as const,\n    title: draft.title,\n    description: draft.description,\n    summary: draft.summary,\n    tweet: draft.tweet,\n    tags: [...draft.tags],\n    body: draft.body,\n  }, \"post draft\");\n}\n\nexport function encodeArticleVoice(voice: ArticleVoice): ArticleVoiceCarrier {\n  return articleVoiceCarrier.assert({\n    persona: [...voice.persona],\n    ...(voice.exemplar === undefined ? {} : { exemplar: [...voice.exemplar] }),\n    ...(voice.laws === undefined ? {} : { laws: [...voice.laws] }),\n  }, \"article voice\");\n}\n\nexport function articleGateReport(draft: PostDraft, term: string, gates: readonly FarmGate[]): ArticleGateReportCarrier {\n  return articleGateReportCarrier.assert({\n    kind: \"producing-content.article.gate-report\" as const,\n    draft: encodePostDraft(draft),\n    term,\n    gates: gates.map((charge) => gateCharge(charge.gate, charge.charge, charge.evidence)),\n  }, \"article gate report\");\n}\n\nexport function sourceStoryTerm(story: SourceStory): Tm {\n  return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(encodeSourceStory(story), \"source story\"));\n}\n\nexport function postDraftTerm(draft: PostDraft): Tm {\n  return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(encodePostDraft(draft), \"post draft\"));\n}\n\nexport function articleGateReportTerm(report: ArticleGateReportCarrier): Tm {\n  return carrierToTerm(jsonResource(articleGateReportCarrier.assert(report, \"article gate report\"), \"article gate report\"));\n}\n"},{"sourceId":"production/inference-adapter","digest":"sha256:1685043b43e851921be7f21fc7a59eb2aea93e8ecc84e25b4d582671f00f91b8","content":"/**\n * ask â€” a prompt-to-text morphism over OpenAI-compatible chat completion\n * endpoints.\n *\n * `makeAsk` closes over one provider configuration and returns\n * `(prompt) => Promise<string>`; the production cycle takes that function\n * as a value and never sees a provider. Providers, keys, and `fetch` all\n * enter as values, so consumers and tests can run hermetically.\n *\n * Provider note (carried from operations): `nim` is the default-capable\n * provider (https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1/chat/completions, keyed by\n * NVIDIA_API_KEY). `groq` has a tiny context window and is suitable ONLY\n * for prompts well under 8k tokens.\n */\n\ntype FetchLike = typeof fetch;\n\nexport type ContentProviderName = \"groq\" | \"nim\";\n\nexport type ContentProvider = Readonly<{\n  url: string;\n  keyEnv: string;\n  model: string;\n}>;\n\nexport type ContentProviderUsage = Readonly<{\n  promptTokens?: number;\n  completionTokens?: number;\n  totalTokens?: number;\n  raw?: unknown;\n}>;\n\nexport type ContentAskOptions = Readonly<{\n  provider?: ContentProviderName | string;\n  providers?: Readonly<Record<string, ContentProvider>>;\n  model?: string;\n  temperature?: number;\n  maxTokens?: number;\n  env?: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;\n  fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n  onUsage?: (usage: ContentProviderUsage, meta: Readonly<{ provider: string; model: string }>) => void;\n}>;\n\n/**\n * The default provider registry. `nim` is the default-capable provider;\n * `groq` only suits prompts well under 8k tokens (tiny context window).\n */\nexport const contentProviders: Readonly<Record<string, ContentProvider>> = Object.freeze({\n  groq: Object.freeze({ url: \"https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions\", keyEnv: \"GROQ_API_KEY\", model: \"llama-3.1-8b-instant\" }),\n  nim: Object.freeze({ url: \"https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1/chat/completions\", keyEnv: \"NVIDIA_API_KEY\", model: \"meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct\" }),\n});\n\nfunction envValue(env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>, key: string): string | undefined {\n  const value = env[key];\n  return value === \"\" ? undefined : value;\n}\n\n/** Whether a provider is configured AND keyed in the given environment.\n *  Boolean convenience at the app edge (invariant A1 reserves carriers for\n *  lib judgments; an app may answer configuration questions plainly). */\nexport function hasProvider(provider: string, opts: Pick<ContentAskOptions, \"providers\" | \"env\"> = {}): boolean {\n  const providers = opts.providers ?? contentProviders;\n  const config = providers[provider];\n  return !!(config && envValue(opts.env ?? process.env, config.keyEnv));\n}\n\n/** First keyed provider in preference order; nim leads (see module note). */\nexport function pickProvider(order: readonly string[] = [\"nim\", \"groq\"], opts: Pick<ContentAskOptions, \"providers\" | \"env\"> = {}): string | null {\n  return order.find((provider) => hasProvider(provider, opts)) ?? null;\n}\n\nfunction normalizeProviderUsage(value: unknown): ContentProviderUsage {\n  if (!value || typeof value !== \"object\") return Object.freeze({ raw: value });\n  const usage = value as { prompt_tokens?: unknown; completion_tokens?: unknown; total_tokens?: unknown };\n  const promptTokens = typeof usage.prompt_tokens === \"number\" ? usage.prompt_tokens : undefined;\n  const completionTokens = typeof usage.completion_tokens === \"number\" ? usage.completion_tokens : undefined;\n  const totalTokens = typeof usage.total_tokens === \"number\" ? usage.total_tokens : undefined;\n  const normalized: { promptTokens?: number; completionTokens?: number; totalTokens?: number; raw?: unknown } = { raw: value };\n  if (promptTokens !== undefined) normalized.promptTokens = promptTokens;\n  if (completionTokens !== undefined) normalized.completionTokens = completionTokens;\n  if (totalTokens !== undefined) normalized.totalTokens = totalTokens;\n  return Object.freeze(normalized);\n}\n\n/**\n * Close over one provider configuration and return the ask morphism.\n * Defaults to `nim`, the default-capable provider; pass `provider: \"groq\"`\n * only for prompts well under 8k tokens. Throws only on malformed\n * configuration (unknown provider, unkeyed environment) â€” transport\n * failures surface per call.\n */\nexport function makeAsk(options: ContentAskOptions = {}): (prompt: string) => Promise<string> {\n  const providerName = options.provider ?? \"nim\";\n  const providers = options.providers ?? contentProviders;\n  const provider = providers[providerName];\n  if (!provider) throw new TypeError(`unknown provider \"${providerName}\"`);\n  const key = envValue(options.env ?? process.env, provider.keyEnv);\n  if (!key) throw new TypeError(`${provider.keyEnv} is not set`);\n  const model = options.model ?? provider.model;\n  const fetchImpl = options.fetchImpl ?? fetch;\n  const temperature = options.temperature ?? 0.4;\n  return async (prompt: string): Promise<string> => {\n    const response = await fetchImpl(provider.url, {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\", authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        model,\n        messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: String(prompt) }],\n        temperature,\n        ...(options.maxTokens === undefined ? {} : { max_tokens: options.maxTokens }),\n      }),\n    });\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`${providerName} ${model}: HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    const data = await response.json() as { usage?: unknown; choices?: readonly { message?: { content?: unknown } }[] };\n    options.onUsage?.(normalizeProviderUsage(data.usage), { provider: providerName, model });\n    return String(data.choices?.[0]?.message?.content ?? \"\");\n  };\n}\n"}]

